var tafsirData_6 = {
  "1": "“All praise is for Allah Who created the heavens and the earth and made darkness and light.” A new chapter opens the way the Quran loves to open: with praise. The same words open the first chapter of the Quran: “All praise is for Allah—Lord of all worlds.” Here the praise is followed immediately by the reason: Allah “created the heavens and the earth and made darkness and light.” The heavens and the earth are the great visible creation; darkness and light are the opposites that fill the world we live in — the night and the day, the hidden and the seen, the cold and the warm. The One who created all of this, and placed darkness and light in their order, is the One deserving all praise.\n\nThe verse then states the tragedy of the disbelievers in one sentence: “Yet the disbelievers set up equals to their Lord ˹in worship˺.” After this creation — after the heavens and the earth and the darkness and the light — they still worshipped other things beside the Creator. The word “equals” means partners and rivals: they gave to created things a share of the worship that belongs to the Creator alone. The contrast is the whole message of the chapter: the Creator is one, His signs are everywhere, and yet people turn to equals.\n\nThe chapter that begins here is Al-An'am, the Cattle, revealed in Mecca. Its central theme is the oneness of Allah — clearing Him of every partner, every invented rule, and every false claim. The opening verse plants the argument: the One who created everything deserves the worship of everything. Whatever else the chapter says — about food, about idols, about the prophets — it all returns to this first truth: all praise is for Allah, and no equal belongs to Him.",
  "2": "“He is the One Who created you from clay, then appointed a term ˹for your death˺ and another known only to Him ˹for your resurrection˺ —yet you continue to doubt!” The verse moves from the creation of the heavens and the earth to the creation of the human being. “He is the One Who created you from clay” — every human being traces back to Adam, who was created from clay. The Quran reminds people of their origin so that they will not be proud: you began as clay, formed and shaped by Allah. Another chapter says: “How can you deny Allah? You were lifeless and He gave you life, then He will cause you to die and again bring you to life, and then to Him you will ˹all˺ be returned.” The Prophet described the same origin in a hadith recorded in Sahih Muslim: Allah created Adam from a handful taken from the whole earth.\n\nThen come the two terms: “then appointed a term ˹for your death˺ and another known only to Him ˹for your resurrection˺.” The first term is the lifespan of every person — the day of death, which Allah has fixed. The second term is the Day of Resurrection — the Hour, which no one knows except Allah. Everyone knows the first term will come; no one knows its moment. The second term is hidden in Allah's knowledge alone.\n\nAnd yet, the verse ends: “—yet you continue to doubt!” The One who created you from clay, fixed your death, and holds the knowledge of your resurrection — and you still doubt that He can raise you again? The argument is simple and powerful: the One who began the creation can repeat it. The verse teaches the believer to see in his own existence the proof of the resurrection, and to live with the two terms in mind — the term of death that is appointed, and the term of the Hour that is known only to Allah.",
  "3": "“He is the Only True God in the heavens and the earth.” The verse states the heart of the faith in the clearest words: Allah is the only true God — in the heavens and in the earth. Not a god among gods, not a local deity, not a national god: the Only True God, everywhere. The phrase “the Only True God” means that everything else that is called a god is false by definition — because there is only one true God, and everything else is a creature wearing the title of a god.\n\nThen the verse gives the proof that follows from His oneness: “He knows whatever you conceal and whatever you reveal, and knows whatever you do.” The false gods of the pagans could not hear, could not see, and knew nothing about their worshippers. The True God knows everything: what you conceal in your heart, what you reveal with your tongue, and what you do with your limbs. Another chapter says the same: “He knows whatever is in the heavens and the earth. And He knows whatever you conceal and whatever you reveal. For Allah knows best what is ˹hidden˺ in the heart.” And another: “Whether you speak openly ˹or not˺, He certainly knows what is secret and what is even more hidden.”\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the foundation of mindfulness: the One you worship is the One who knows. A person may hide his deeds from people, but he cannot hide them from the Only True God. This knowledge is both a warning and a comfort — a warning to those who sin in secret, and a comfort to those who do good in secret, because their hidden good is fully known to Allah and will never be lost.",
  "4": "“Whenever a sign comes to them from their Lord, they turn away from it.” The verse describes the habit of the disbelievers with one word repeated: “whenever.” Every time. Not once or twice — whenever a sign comes, they turn away. The signs are many: the verses of the Quran, the proofs in creation, the miracles of the prophets, the events of history. And the response is always the same: turning away. The sign comes from their Lord — the word “Lord” is important, because it reminds the reader that the signs are not random; they come from the One who created them and cares for them, like messages from a caring Lord to His servants.\n\nThe verb “turn away” describes the movement of the face and the heart together: they do not argue with the sign, they do not examine it — they simply turn their backs on it. This is the difference between the seeker and the denier: the seeker turns toward the sign, the denier turns away from it.\n\nThe verse is a warning to every generation, because the habit is not limited to the pagans of Mecca. Whenever the truth comes to a person — a verse read, a sermon heard, a lesson learned, a moment of reflection — the response reveals the heart. Those who turn away lose the sign; those who turn toward it are guided from sign to sign. The next verses will show where this turning away leads: to rejection of the truth itself, and to the consequences of ridicule.",
  "5": "“They have indeed rejected the truth when it came to them, so they will soon face the consequences of their ridicule.” The previous verse described the habit — turning away from every sign. This verse names the result: they have “rejected the truth.” Turning away from signs grows into rejecting the truth itself. First the signs are ignored, then the truth is denied. The verse says the rejection happened “when it came to them” — the truth did come; it reached them clearly; and they rejected it. There was no excuse of ignorance, because the truth arrived.\n\nThen comes the promise of the consequence: “so they will soon face the consequences of their ridicule.” The word “ridicule” points to their behaviour: they mocked the truth, laughed at the messenger, and made jokes of the revelation. The Quran tells them that they will face the consequences of that very mockery — the thing they laughed at will become the thing that destroys them. The same warning appears later in this chapter: “˹Other˺ messengers had already been ridiculed before you ˹O Prophet˺, but those who mocked them were overtaken by what they used to ridicule.”\n\nThe verse teaches a firm principle: mockery does not change truth, and it does not protect the mocker. History repeated this pattern — the nations that mocked their prophets were overtaken by the very punishment those prophets had warned about. The believers are taught not to be shaken by ridicule: the mockery of the deniers is recorded, and its consequences are certain. Truth can be mocked, but it cannot be defeated by mocking.",
  "6": "“Have they not seen how many ˹disbelieving˺ peoples We destroyed before them?” The verse calls the disbelievers to look at history. The question “have they not seen?” means: have they not heard, not learned, not reflected? Before them, many peoples lived on the earth — strong, wealthy, organized — and Allah destroyed them for their denial. The Quran repeats this call throughout: another chapter says, “We surely sent a messenger to every community... So travel throughout the land and see the fate of the deniers!”\n\nThen the verse describes the strength of those destroyed nations: “We had made them more established in the land than you. We sent down abundant rain for them and made rivers flow at their feet.” They were stronger than the Quraysh: more power, more land, more water, more wealth. The abundant rain and flowing rivers are pictures of provision and civilisation — everything a people need to prosper. And yet: “Then We destroyed them for their sins and replaced them with other peoples.”\n\nTheir strength did not save them; their rivers did not save them; their buildings did not save them. They were destroyed “for their sins” — the denial of the truth and the wrongdoing that followed it. And the earth did not remain empty: other peoples took their place. Another chapter describes the same pattern: “Whenever We sent a prophet to a society, We afflicted its ˹disbelieving˺ people with suffering and adversity, so perhaps they would be humbled.”\n\nThe lesson is written for every strong nation: power and provision are tests, not protections. The One who gave the rivers can take everything away. The believer looks at history and learns: what matters is not how established a people are in the land, but how they stand with their Lord.",
  "7": "“Had We sent down to you ˹O Prophet˺ a revelation in writing and they were to touch it with their own hands, the disbelievers would still have said, “This is nothing but pure magic!”” The verse imagines the most extreme proof possible: a revelation written on paper, sent down from heaven, so solid that the disbelievers could touch it with their own hands. Would they believe then? The verse answers: no. They would still say, “This is nothing but pure magic!”\n\nThe verse exposes the real problem. It is not the form of the proof that is missing; it is the will to believe. The disbelievers demanded a sign they could touch — but even touching would not convince them, because their denial was not based on evidence. It was based on stubbornness. Later in this same chapter they are described saying about the Quran: “This ˹Quran˺ is nothing but ancient fables!” The same accusation would be repeated against any proof: if it is written, it is magic; if it is recited, it is fables.\n\nThis is why the Quran never treated the demand for signs as a real request. Another chapter records their demands: “They challenge ˹the Prophet˺, “We will never believe in you until you cause a spring to gush forth from the earth for us,”” — and lists one demand after another, each one harder than the last. The demands grow because the goal is not proof; the goal is delay and escape.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer a valuable lesson: you cannot convince someone whose heart has decided not to believe. The Quran is the greatest sign — and it is enough for those who want the truth, and not enough for those who do not.",
  "8": "“They say, “Why has no ˹visible˺ angel come with him?”” The disbelievers asked why an angel did not come with the Prophet — a visible angel, walking beside him, confirming his message. It sounds like a reasonable request at first. But the verse answers: “Had We sent down an angel, the matter would have certainly been settled ˹at once˺, and they would have never been given more time ˹to repent˺.”\n\nWhy would an angel have ended everything? Because the coming of an angel in visible form is one of the signs that close the door of repentance. The pattern of Allah with the previous nations is clear from the Quran: when the decisive sign came — the sign that left no room for denial — the punishment followed quickly, and the chance to turn back was over. Sending a visible angel to the disbelievers of Mecca would have made the matter conclusive: believe or face destruction, right then. So Allah, in His mercy, did not send the angel — because He wanted to give them more time to repent.\n\nThe verse teaches a deep truth about Allah's patience: some things the disbelievers ask for are not given to them out of mercy, not out of weakness. The absence of the angel was itself a mercy — a door left open. The same principle appears in another chapter: “And nothing prevents people from believing when guidance comes to them and from seeking their Lord’s forgiveness except ˹their demand˺ to meet the same fate of earlier deniers or that the torment would confront them face to face.” The demand for the conclusive sign is, in reality, a demand for destruction.",
  "9": "“And if We had sent an angel, We would have certainly made it ˹assume the form of˺ a man—leaving them more confused than they already are.” The verse adds a second reason why no angel was sent. Even if an angel were sent, human eyes cannot see an angel in its true form. So the angel would have to appear in the form of a man — and then the disbelievers would say: this is only a man, like the messenger himself. Their confusion would only grow. They demanded an angel; if an angel came in human form, they would reject it as human; and they cannot see it any other way.\n\nThe same point is made in another chapter, where the Quran answers their demand: “Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Had there been angels walking the earth, well settled, We would have surely sent down for them an angel from heaven as a messenger.”” Messengers are sent from among the kind of beings who receive them: humans receive human messengers. If the earth were inhabited by angels, angel messengers would have come. The demand for an angel messenger is, in fact, a demand for the impossible — a sign that the demanders are not seeking guidance but excuses.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to see the wisdom in Allah's choices: the messenger is human because humans learn from humans, and the proof is a human life lived among them. The disbelievers' confusion was not caused by Allah's choice; it was caused by their own refusal — no form of the message would ever satisfy a heart that had decided to deny.",
  "10": "“˹Other˺ messengers had already been ridiculed before you ˹O Prophet˺, but those who mocked them were overtaken by what they used to ridicule.” The verse is a comfort to the Prophet, and a warning to the mockers. The ridicule he faced was not new: every messenger before him was mocked by his own people. Another chapter says: “˹Other˺ messengers had already been ridiculed before you ˹O Prophet˺, but those who mocked them were overtaken by what they used to ridicule” — the same words in another surah. And another chapter records the pattern with sadness: “Oh pity, such beings! No messenger ever came to them without being mocked.”\n\nBut the verse does not stop at the comfort; it states the end of the story: “those who mocked them were overtaken by what they used to ridicule.” The thing they laughed at — the message, the warning, the promise of punishment — came upon them. The flood overtook the mockers of Noah; the punishment overtook the mockers of every prophet. What they treated as a joke became their destruction.\n\nThe verse teaches two lessons. For the believers: do not be shaken by mockery, because mockery is the old habit of the deniers, and its end is known. The prophets before you were mocked and then helped; the mockers were mocked by their own fate. For the mockers: the warning is serious — the Quran does not say “perhaps” or “maybe”; it says the mockers were “overtaken.” The pattern of history is fixed, and the reader is invited to learn from it before the pattern repeats on him.",
  "11": "“Say, “Travel throughout the land and see the fate of the deniers.”” The command is short and powerful: travel, look, learn. The Prophet is told to say this to the disbelievers who rejected the signs and mocked the message. The invitation is to go out into the world and see with their own eyes the ruins of the nations that denied their prophets. The same command appears in another chapter: “We surely sent a messenger to every community, saying, “Worship Allah and shun false gods.” But some of them were guided by Allah, while others were destined to stray. So travel throughout the land and see the fate of the deniers!”\n\nThe verse assumes something important: history is a lesson. The ruins of past nations are not random stones; they are testimonies. The people of 'Ad, of Thamud, of Pharaoh — their remains and their stories carry the same message: denial ends in destruction. Travel is commanded here not for pleasure but for reflection — to see the fate of the deniers so that the living will not repeat their error.\n\nThe command is also an answer to the mockers: instead of demanding signs and ridiculing the messenger, go and look at what happened to those who ridiculed messengers before you. The earth itself is a sign, and the ruins are its verses.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the value of reflection and the value of history. A person who never travels, never sees, and never reflects remains trapped in his own small world. And a person who travels without reflecting sees nothing. The Quran commands travel with eyes open — so that the fate of the deniers becomes a teacher for the living.",
  "12": "“Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, “To whom belongs everything in the heavens and the earth?” Say, “To Allah!”” The verse commands the Prophet to put a simple question to the disbelievers: who owns everything in the heavens and the earth? The answer is so obvious that even the pagans would not deny it — everything belongs to Allah. The question is not asked to get information; it is asked to make them admit the truth with their own tongues, so that their worship of other gods stands exposed as a contradiction: everything belongs to Allah, yet they worship others besides Him.\n\nThen comes a sentence of profound mercy: “He has taken upon Himself to be Merciful.” Allah has written mercy upon Himself as His own rule. He is not merciful reluctantly or occasionally; mercy is His settled way. The same phrase appears later in this chapter, where the believers are told: “Your Lord has taken upon Himself to be Merciful.” The Lord of everything is the Lord whose mercy is His own promise.\n\nThen the verse continues: “He will certainly gather ˹all of˺ you together for the Day of Judgment— about which there is no doubt.” The same Lord who created everything and wrote mercy upon Himself will gather everyone — believer and disbeliever — on a Day about which there is no doubt. And then the verse ends with the tragedy: “But those who have ruined themselves will never believe.” The word “ruined themselves” is strong: they did not lose their souls to an enemy; they ruined themselves with their own denial. The verse teaches that belief and ruin are connected: the one who refuses to believe in the gathering is the one who ruins himself.",
  "13": "“To Him belongs whatever exists in the day and night.” The verse continues the statement of Allah's ownership. The previous verse said everything in the heavens and the earth belongs to Him; this verse adds the dimension of time: whatever exists in the day and whatever exists in the night — everything that appears and disappears, every creature that moves in the light and every creature that moves in the dark, every deed done openly and every deed done secretly — all of it belongs to Allah. Day and night together cover all of existence: there is no hour, no place, no action that is outside His ownership.\n\nThen the verse adds: “And He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.” The two names answer the two halves of the verse. He is All-Hearing — He hears every call, every whisper, every prayer said in the darkness of the night. He is All-Knowing — He knows every movement of the day, every intention, every hidden thing. The One to Whom everything belongs is also the One who hears and knows everything within His kingdom.\n\nThere is a deep comfort in this verse for the believer. Whatever exists in the day and night includes the believer's own life: his work in the day, his prayers at night, his secrets, his struggles. All of it belongs to Allah, and all of it is heard and known by Him. Nothing a servant does for Allah is lost, and nothing he hides from people is hidden from Allah. The verse teaches the believer to live the whole day — and the whole night — in the awareness that he is within the kingdom of the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.",
  "14": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Will I take any guardian other than Allah, the Originator of the heavens and the earth, Who provides for all and is not in need of provision?”” The Prophet is told to ask himself a question in front of the people — a question that shows the absurdity of taking guardians other than Allah. Why would anyone take a guardian other than the One who originated the heavens and the earth? The word “Originator” describes Allah as the One who created everything from nothing — no model, no helper, no partner. And this Originator “provides for all and is not in need of provision”: He feeds every creature, while He Himself needs no food. A guardian who needs provision cannot guard the one who provides for him; the true Guardian is the One who provides for everything.\n\nThen comes the second command: “Say, “I have been commanded to be the first to submit and not be one of the polytheists.”” The Prophet is commanded to be the first to submit — the first of his people to surrender to Allah in Islam. The same command appears in another chapter: “And I am commanded to be the first of those who submit ˹to His Will˺.” Submission and polytheism are opposites: the one who submits gives everything to Allah, and the polytheist divides his worship between Allah and others. The verse teaches the believer that true submission comes before everything else — before the approval of people, before the customs of the tribe, before the pressure of the crowd. The first to submit is not ashamed to be first.",
  "15": "“Say, “I truly fear—if I were to disobey my Lord—the torment of a tremendous Day.”” The Prophet is commanded to speak about his own fear. The verse is addressed to the man who is being pressured, mocked, and threatened — and he is told to say: I fear my Lord. Not the threats of the people, not their ridicule, not their power — but the torment of a tremendous Day, if he were to disobey his Lord. The fear of Allah replaces every other fear. The same words appear in another chapter: “Say, “I truly fear—if I were to disobey my Lord—the torment of a tremendous Day.”” — the exact wording repeated, because the lesson is central.\n\nThe phrase “if I were to disobey my Lord” is full of humility. The Prophet does not say “I will never disobey” as a boast; he says he fears the torment if he were to disobey. This is the fear of the sincere servant: not the fear of a punishment already deserved, but the fear of a possibility that keeps him alert. Even the best of creation speaks of his Lord with awe.\n\nThe verse also answers the disbelievers' demand for torment to be hastened. They kept asking: when is this punishment you warn about? The Prophet says: that Day is tremendous, and my concern is to obey my Lord and avoid it. The one who fears the tremendous Day does not play games with warnings. And the next verse will show the reward of that fear: whoever is spared that Day has won everything.",
  "16": "“Whoever is spared the torment of that Day will have certainly been shown Allah's mercy. And that is the absolute triumph.” The verse follows the Prophet's statement of fear with the prize of safety. “That Day” is the tremendous Day mentioned in the previous verse — the Day of Judgment. And the verse says: whoever is spared its torment has been shown Allah's mercy. The word “spared” is the key: salvation on that Day is not earned by the servant as a right; it is mercy from Allah. The believer works, and the believer prays, but in the end, being spared is a gift of mercy — “will have certainly been shown Allah's mercy.”\n\nThen comes the second half: “And that is the absolute triumph.” The verse uses the strongest word for success — the same word the Quran uses for the final victory of the believers in Paradise. Another chapter says: “So whoever believes in Allah and does good, He will absolve them of their sins and admit them into Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there for ever and ever. That is the ultimate triumph.” Being spared from the torment and being shown mercy — that is the triumph that ends all striving; nothing else in existence deserves the name of triumph beside it.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer how to measure success. People measure success by wealth, position, and comfort. The Quran measures success by one thing: being spared the torment of that Day. A person may lose everything in this world and still be the greatest winner if he is spared; and a person may gain everything in this world and be the greatest loser if he is not. The absolute triumph is safety on that Day.",
  "17": "“If Allah touches you with harm, none can undo it except Him.” The verse states the complete power of Allah over harm and good. “If Allah touches you with harm” — sickness, poverty, loss, difficulty — then no one in creation can remove it except Allah. Not the idols, not the helpers, not the guardians the pagans trusted. The same truth is stated in another chapter with almost the same words: “If Allah touches you with harm, none can undo it except Him. And if He intends good for you, none can withhold His bounty.”\n\nThen the second half: “And if He touches you with a blessing, He is Most Capable of everything.” The first half shows that no one can undo Allah's harm; the second shows that no one can prevent Allah's blessing. When Allah gives good — health, wealth, guidance, children, peace — nothing can stop it. And the verse describes Allah as “Most Capable of everything”: capable of bringing the harm and the blessing, capable of removing the harm and protecting the blessing, capable of everything in between.\n\nThe verse is a complete education in trust. The believer who understands it stops fearing creation and stops depending on creation: the one who can harm him cannot harm without Allah's will, and the one who can benefit him cannot benefit without Allah's will. All doors lead to Allah. This is why the believer turns to Allah first in every difficulty — not because other means are useless, but because the means themselves are in Allah's hands. And it is why the believer receives every blessing with gratitude: it is a touch from the Most Capable.",
  "18": "“He reigns supreme over His creation.” The verse is short, and it is the summary of everything that came before it. Allah reigns supreme over His creation: He is above them in power, above them in authority, above them in might. Nothing in creation escapes His rule — the heavens, the earth, the day, the night, the harm, the blessing. The word used here is the same word the Quran uses for the conqueror who has full control: nothing happens in His kingdom without His command.\n\nThen the verse adds: “And He is the All-Wise, All-Aware.” The two names explain how His supremacy works. He is All-Wise: His rule is never random, never cruel, never mistaken — every decree has wisdom behind it, even when the servant cannot see it. And He is All-Aware: His rule is never blind — He knows every detail of His creation, every heart, every intention, every hidden affair. A ruler may be supreme but ignorant; Allah is supreme and All-Aware. A ruler may be aware but weak; Allah is supreme and All-Wise.\n\nThis verse sits between two groups of verses about trust and fear. The previous verse taught that no one can undo Allah's harm or prevent His blessing; this verse gives the reason — He reigns supreme over His creation. The next verses will show the Prophet bearing witness to this truth before the people. The believer is taught to rest in this: the One who rules everything is wise in His rule and aware of everything He rules. There is no better place to be than under such a rule.",
  "19": "“Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, “Who is the best witness?” Say, “Allah is! He is a Witness between me and you.”” The disbelievers accused the Prophet of fabricating the Quran. So he is commanded to call the greatest witness: Allah Himself. “Who is the best witness?” — the answer is obvious: Allah. And Allah is a Witness between the Prophet and his people: He knows that the Prophet is truthful, and He knows that the people are denying. The appeal to Allah as Witness appears in another chapter: “Sufficient is He as a Witness between you and me.”\n\nThen the Prophet states his mission: “And this Quran has been revealed to me so that, with it, I may warn you and whoever it reaches.” The Quran was revealed for a purpose: warning. And the warning is not limited to the Quraysh of Mecca — it reaches “whoever it reaches,” meaning every person and every generation the Quran reaches until the end of time. Every reader of the Quran is being warned by it.\n\nThen comes the confrontation: “Do you ˹pagans˺ testify that there are other gods besides Allah?” — the pagans were then told to answer; and whatever they might say, the Prophet is commanded: “˹Then˺ say, “I will never testify ˹to this˺!” ˹And˺ say, “There is only One God. And I totally reject whatever ˹idols˺ you associate with Him.”” The Prophet declares: I will never testify to other gods; there is only One God; and I reject every idol you associate with Him. The verse teaches the believer the courage of testimony: when the truth is clear, the believer states it plainly — even against the whole society around him.",
  "20": "“Those to whom We gave the Scripture recognize him ˹to be a true prophet˺ as they recognize their own children.” The verse makes a striking claim: the People of the Book — the Jews and Christians who had the Torah and the Gospel — recognized the Prophet Muhammad as a true prophet, just as a person recognizes his own children. The recognition was not guesswork: their scriptures described him, his name, his description, his time — so clearly that when they saw him, they knew him. The same statement appears in an earlier chapter: “Those We have given the Scripture recognize this ˹Prophet˺ as they recognize their own children. Yet a group of them hides the truth knowingly.”\n\nAnd yet — the verse continues: “Those who have ruined themselves will never believe.” This is the tragedy: they recognized him, and still they did not believe. Their recognition was not the problem; their will was. They knew the truth in their hearts and hid it because belief would have cost them their status, their leadership, and their income. So the verse calls them “those who have ruined themselves” — the same phrase used in the earlier verses of this chapter for the deniers.\n\nThe verse teaches an important truth about knowledge and belief: knowing the truth is not the same as accepting the truth. A person may know with certainty and still refuse — because acceptance is a matter of the heart, not just the mind. The People of the Book knew the Prophet as they knew their own children, and some of them still turned away. The reader is invited to examine his own heart: what truth do you recognize, and what truth are you refusing?",
  "21": "“Who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah or deny His signs?” The verse asks a question that has only one answer: no one. Wrongdoing has levels, and the worst wrongdoing is named here — two kinds. First, “those who fabricate lies against Allah”: inventing false teachings, false gods, false laws, and attributing them to Allah. The polytheists of Mecca did this when they claimed their idols were partners of Allah and when they invented rules about animals and food. Second, “those who... deny His signs”: rejecting the proofs Allah sent — the Quran, the prophets, the creation. Fabricating lies against Allah is inventing falsehood about Him; denying His signs is rejecting the truth He sent. Both are the highest injustice.\n\nThen comes the verdict: “Indeed, the wrongdoers will never succeed.” The word “never” is absolute: no matter how strong, how rich, how influential the wrongdoers appear, they will never succeed — not in the final sense. They may gain the world, but they will lose everything that matters. The Quran repeats this principle in many forms: the wrongdoers' plots only destroy themselves, and their apparent success is a delay, not a victory.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer two things. First, the gravity of lying about Allah — inventing religion, claiming divine authority for human rules, or attributing false teachings to Him is the worst wrong. Second, the comfort that success belongs to truth: the wrongdoers may seem to win for a time, but their end is fixed. The believer's duty is to stand with the truth and leave the outcome to Allah.",
  "22": "“˹Consider˺ the Day We will gather them all together then ask those who associated others ˹with Allah in worship˺, “Where are those gods you used to claim?”” The verse paints a scene from the Day of Judgment. “The Day We will gather them all together” — everyone will be gathered: the polytheists, their idols, their followers, the prophets, the believers. Then the polytheists will be asked a question: “Where are those gods you used to claim?” — the gods they worshipped, defended, and trusted: where are they now? The question is asked on a Day when every false god will be absent. The idols cannot come, the angels they worshipped cannot come, the saints and stars they called upon cannot come. The question answers itself: the gods they claimed are nowhere.\n\nThe word “claim” is carefully chosen: they used to claim these things were gods — a claim, not a fact. They claimed partners for Allah; on that Day, the claim will be shown empty.\n\nThe verse is a warning to every generation that takes gods besides Allah — not only idols of stone, but also wealth, power, status, desires, and human beings treated as ultimate authorities. On that Day, everyone will be asked: where are those gods you used to claim? And the answer will be silence, because the false gods will have vanished. The scene also shows that the Day of Judgment is a day of questions. The people of the past will be asked about their deeds, the polytheists about their gods, the prophets about their message — and every question will be answered with the full truth. The believer learns from this scene to examine his heart now: what do I truly worship, and where will it be on that Day? The question asked then is worth asking today, while there is still time to change the answer.",
  "23": "“Their only argument will be: “By Allah, our Lord! We were never polytheists.”” The verse records the shocking answer of the polytheists on the Day of Judgment. When they are asked where their gods are, they will not admit their shirk — they will lie about it: “By Allah, our Lord! We were never polytheists.” They will swear by Allah that they never worshipped others beside Him. The very people who spent their lives worshipping idols will deny it completely.\n\nWhy would they lie? Because on that Day, they will finally understand what polytheism means — and they will try to escape its consequences. The denial is the last attempt of the desperate. The verse shows that the Day of Judgment will not automatically make people truthful: the deniers will carry their denial into the next life. The same scene appears in another chapter, where they say: “By Allah, our Lord! We were not polytheists.” — and Allah will say that they have lied, as the next verse of this chapter confirms.\n\nThe verse is a powerful warning about the state of the heart. A person who has trained himself to deny the truth in this world will find it easy to deny on the Day of Judgment. Habits of falsehood do not disappear at death; they are carried into the next life. The believer, by contrast, is taught to be truthful now — so that truthfulness becomes his nature, and he will not need to lie on the Day when lying will not help.",
  "24": "“See how they will lie about themselves and how those ˹gods˺ they fabricated will fail them!” The verse commands the reader to look at the scene of the previous verse — to really see it. “See how they will lie about themselves”: the polytheists will lie about their own lives, their own worship, their own deeds. They will swear that they were never polytheists. The lie is against themselves before it is against anyone else — they will deny the very record of their own actions.\n\nAnd at the same moment: “how those ˹gods˺ they fabricated will fail them!” The gods they worshipped will fail them completely — not because the gods were evil, but because they were nothing. They were fabricated: invented by the worshippers themselves, shaped by their hands, named by their tongues. How can a fabricated thing save its fabricator? On that Day, every idol will be absent, every false god will be silent, and the worshippers will stand alone with the lies they told about themselves.\n\nThe verse teaches the emptiness of false worship in the clearest picture: the worshipper is left with nothing but his own lies, and the worshipped is left with nothing at all. The contrast with the believer is complete: the believer's Lord is real, present, and true; the polytheist's gods are fabrications that fail him at the moment he needs them most.\n\nThe command “see” is addressed to the reader in this world: you do not have to wait for that Day to see the pattern. False gods fail their worshippers in this life too — they cannot help, cannot hear, cannot save. The wise person sees it now.",
  "25": "“There are some of them who ˹pretend to˺ listen to your recitation ˹of the Quran˺, but We have cast veils over their hearts—leaving them unable to comprehend it—and deafness in their ears.” The verse describes the state of the deniers when they hear the Quran. Some of them come and pretend to listen — they sit, they appear attentive, they even argue about what they heard. But their listening is blocked: “We have cast veils over their hearts—leaving them unable to comprehend it—and deafness in their ears.” The veils are not random; they are the result of the deniers' own stubbornness. Because they kept turning away, their hearts became sealed, and the Quran describes this as Allah's action in response to their choice.\n\nThe same description appears in another chapter: “We have cast veils over their hearts—leaving them unable to comprehend it—and deafness in their ears. And when you mention your Lord alone in the Quran, they turn their backs in aversion.” The sign of the sealed heart is aversion: when Allah alone is mentioned, they turn away.\n\nThen the verse adds: “Even if they were to see every sign, they still would not believe in them.” Every sign — every miracle, every proof — would still not move them. And their final argument is recorded: “The disbelievers would ˹even˺ come to argue with you, saying, “This ˹Quran˺ is nothing but ancient fables!”” They heard the greatest revelation and called it ancient fables. The verse teaches that guidance needs two things: a clear message and an open heart. The message was clear; the heart was closed. The reader is invited to check his own heart: when the Quran is recited, does it find an open heart or a veiled one?",
  "26": "“They turn others away from the Prophet and distance themselves as well.” The verse describes the double work of the deniers: they turned other people away from the Prophet, and they themselves stayed away. They did not want to believe, and they did not want anyone else to believe. This is the work of the enemies of truth in every age: first, they reject; then, they recruit others to reject. The turning away is active — they did not merely ignore the call; they blocked it, mocked it, and discouraged others from it.\n\nThen comes the surprising verdict: “They ruin none but themselves, yet they fail to perceive it.” The people who turned others away and distanced themselves believed they were harming the Prophet and his mission. But the verse says: they ruin none but themselves. Their turning away did not harm the truth; it harmed only them. Their opposition did not stop the message; it only added to their own loss. And the tragedy is in the last words: “yet they fail to perceive it.” They could not even see that they were the ones being ruined.\n\nThe verse teaches a deep principle: the enemies of truth cannot harm the truth. The truth is Allah's, and Allah protects it. The ones who fight it are only fighting themselves — like a man striking his own shadow. And it teaches the believer not to be alarmed by opposition: the opposition of the deniers is a sign that the truth is alive, and their ruin is their own work. The believer's duty is to keep conveying the truth and to leave the ruin of the deniers to their own actions.",
  "27": "“If only you could see when they will be detained before the Fire!” The verse invites the Prophet — and the reader — to imagine a scene that words can barely carry: the deniers being stopped and held before the Fire, unable to move forward, forced to look at what they denied. “If only you could see” — the seeing is beyond the power of this world; the Quran is giving a glimpse through words.\n\nThen their cry is recorded: “They will cry, “Oh! If only we could be sent back, we would never deny the signs of our Lord and we would ˹surely˺ be of the believers.”” At the edge of the Fire, they ask for one thing: to be sent back to the world. And they make a promise: we would never deny the signs of our Lord; we would surely be believers. The promise sounds sincere — but the next verse will expose it.\n\nThe same cry appears in another chapter: “If only you could see the wicked hanging their heads ˹in shame˺ before their Lord, ˹crying:˺ “Our Lord! We have now seen and heard, so send us back and we will do good. We truly have sure faith ˹now˺!”” And in another: “When death approaches any of them, they cry, “My Lord! Let me go back, so I may do good in what I left behind.”” The desire to return is universal at that moment — but the Quran teaches that it comes too late. The scene is a warning to be heeded now, before the seeing becomes real.",
  "28": "“But no! ˹They only say this˺ because the truth they used to hide will become all too clear to them.” The verse cuts through the cry of the previous verse. The deniers ask to be sent back with promises of belief — but the Quran answers: “But no!” Their request is refused, and the reason is given: they only say this because the truth they used to hide has now become all too clear. In this world, they hid the truth from themselves and from others; on that Day, the truth will be unmistakable. Their change of words is not a change of heart; it is the panic of those who can no longer hide.\n\nThen comes the proof: “Even if they were to be sent back, they would certainly revert to what they were forbidden. Indeed they are liars!” The verse declares what they would actually do if their wish were granted: they would return to the world and go straight back to what they were forbidden. Their promise of belief is a lie, and Allah knows it. This is the Quran's answer to every deathbed promise and every last-moment claim: the test of sincerity is what a person does when the choice is real — not what he says when the choice is gone.\n\nThe verse teaches a profound truth about the human heart: the return is not a matter of time but of will. If the will is corrupt, more time changes nothing. The believer is taught to use his time now, while belief is a choice and not a panic — because the person who only believes when he can no longer deny has never truly believed.",
  "29": "“They insisted, “There is nothing beyond this worldly life and we will never be resurrected.”” The verse records the creed of the deniers in their own words: “There is nothing beyond this worldly life.” No Hereafter, no judgment, no reckoning — this life is everything, and “we will never be resurrected.” The word “insisted” shows their attitude: they did not merely doubt the resurrection; they insisted on denying it. Their denial was not a conclusion of evidence; it was a position they defended.\n\nThe same claim appears in another chapter with more detail: “And they argue, “There is nothing beyond our worldly life. We die; others are born. And nothing destroys us but ˹the passage of˺ time.” Yet they have no knowledge ˹in support˺ of this ˹claim˺. They only speculate.” The Quran's answer to this creed is simple: they have no knowledge in support of it — they only speculate. The denial of the Hereafter is not knowledge; it is a guess, an assumption, a wish dressed as a belief.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the value of certainty: the Quran speaks of the Hereafter as certain, while the deniers speak of this world as everything. The one who believes in the resurrection lives differently — he knows his deeds are recorded, his life is a journey, and this world is not the end. The one who denies it lives as if this world is all — and the Quran says he will meet the truth he denied, as the next verse will show.",
  "30": "“But if only you could see when they will be detained before their Lord!” The scene now shifts: in the previous verses they were detained before the Fire; here they are detained before their Lord Himself. The Quran again says “if only you could see” — because the scene is beyond words. The deniers of the resurrection are now standing before the Lord whose resurrection they denied.\n\nThen the dialogue: “He will ask ˹them˺, “Is this ˹Hereafter˺ not the truth?” They will cry, “Absolutely, by our Lord!”” The question is asked to make them confess with their own mouths. And they do: “Absolutely, by our Lord!” — yes, this is the truth; we swear by our Lord. The people who insisted that there is nothing beyond this worldly life now swear by the Lord they denied that the Hereafter is real. Their confession is complete and absolute.\n\nThen comes the verdict: “He will say, “Then taste the punishment for your disbelief.”” The confession becomes the sentence: since you now admit it is the truth — and you denied it when denial could have been changed into belief — taste the punishment for your disbelief. The punishment is not for ignorance; it is for disbelief — for knowing and refusing.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer that the Hereafter is not a theory to be debated; it is a truth to be lived. The people in this scene would have given anything to go back and believe — but the time for belief was in this world. The reader is standing in that time right now. The verse is an invitation to confess the truth now, willingly, before the Day when it will be confessed by force.",
  "31": "“Losers indeed are those who deny the meeting with Allah until the Hour takes them by surprise, then they will cry, “Woe to us for having ignored this!”” The verse opens with the verdict of loss: “Losers indeed are those who deny the meeting with Allah.” The meeting with Allah is the resurrection and the judgment — the return of the servant to his Lord. Those who deny it are losers — not in the market of this world, but in the market that matters. And the verse adds a detail: they denied it “until the Hour takes them by surprise.” The Hour came suddenly — death or the resurrection — while they were still denying. It did not wait for their permission.\n\nThen their cry: “Woe to us for having ignored this!” The cry is full of regret: we ignored it — we put it aside, we refused to think about it, we treated it as a distant possibility. And now it has arrived. The same regret is described in another chapter: “When death approaches any of them, they cry, “My Lord! Let me go back, so I may do good in what I left behind.” Never! It is only a ˹useless˺ appeal they make.”\n\nThen comes the terrifying picture: “They will bear ˹the burden of˺ their sins on their backs. Evil indeed is their burden!” Their sins are pictured as a load carried on their backs — every denial, every mockery, every wrong deed weighing them down. The picture is of people bent under the weight of what they did. And the verse ends: “Evil indeed is their burden!” — an exclamation of horror at the load they carry.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to take the meeting with Allah seriously now — to carry the load of sins lightly while it can still be put down through repentance, before the Day when it is carried on the back forever.",
  "32": "“This worldly life is no more than play and amusement, but far better is the ˹eternal˺ Home of the Hereafter for those mindful ˹of Allah˺.” The verse compares the two lives in a picture everyone can understand. This worldly life — with its games, its competitions, its glitter — is “no more than play and amusement.” Play is something children do that ends when the game ends; amusement is entertainment that leaves nothing behind. The Quran uses this picture in several places. Another chapter says: “Know that this worldly life is no more than play, amusement, luxury, mutual boasting, and competition in wealth and children.” And another: “This worldly life is no more than play and amusement. But the Hereafter is indeed the real life, if only they knew.” The world is not evil in itself — the Quran calls its good things lawful — but it is not the goal. It is a stage, not a home.\n\nThen comes the contrast: “but far better is the ˹eternal˺ Home of the Hereafter for those mindful ˹of Allah˺.” The Hereafter is described as a Home — a place of settlement, while the world is a playground. And it is “far better” — not slightly, not in some ways, but far better. The verse adds the condition: for those mindful of Allah — those who live with awareness that the world is a test and the Home is the destination. For them, the Hereafter is the better choice, and they live accordingly.\n\nThe verse ends: “Will you not then understand?” The question is gentle: if the world is play and the Hereafter is the Home, why do people build their whole lives on the play and forget the Home? The believer is invited to understand — and to live by what he understands: enjoy the world as a traveller enjoys a rest, and build for the Home that lasts.",
  "33": "“We certainly know that what they say grieves you ˹O Prophet˺.” The verse begins with comfort from Allah to His Messenger. The denial and mockery of the people hurt him deeply — he was a human being with a human heart, and the rejection of his own people weighed on him. So Allah tells him: We certainly know. We see your grief, We know the words that wounded you, and nothing of it is hidden from Us. The comfort is in the knowing itself: the Prophet is not alone in his pain; his Lord knows it and shares the concern.\n\nThen comes the clarification: “It is not your honesty they question—it is Allah’s signs that the wrongdoers deny.” The Prophet might have thought the rejection was about him — about his honesty, his character, his message. The verse corrects this: it is not your honesty they question. The people of Mecca knew him as al-Amin, the trustworthy; they had called him truthful since before the revelation. They did not deny his honesty; they denied the signs. The problem was not the messenger but the message — and the wrongdoers’ denial of it.\n\nThe word “wrongdoers” is important: their denial is described as wrongdoing, not as honest doubt. They knew the truth and turned from it, and that is injustice against their own souls.\n\nThe verse teaches every caller to the truth a valuable lesson: when people reject the message, do not carry the rejection as a personal failure. The rejection is not about you; it is about the truth and about the hearts that refuse it. Do your part with honesty, and leave the denial of the wrongdoers to their own account.",
  "34": "“Indeed, messengers before you were rejected but patiently endured rejection and persecution until Our help came to them.” The verse comforts the Prophet with the history of the prophets. He was not the first to be rejected; every messenger before him was rejected by his people. But the prophets did not break under the rejection — they “patiently endured rejection and persecution.” They were hurt, driven out, mocked, and harmed, and they kept going. The word “patiently endured” describes their character: firm, steady, not shaken by the hostility around them. And their patience had an end and a reward: “until Our help came to them.”\n\nThen comes the promise: “And Allah’s promise ˹to help˺ is never broken.” The help of Allah to His messengers is a promise, and a promise from Allah does not fail. The prophets were helped in this world — Noah was saved, Abraham was protected, Moses was given victory — and the pattern was fixed: patience first, then help.\n\nThe verse adds: “And you have already received some of the narratives of these messengers.” The Prophet had been told their stories — and the Quran tells them in detail to strengthen his heart. Another chapter says: “And We relate to you ˹O Prophet˺ the stories of the messengers to reassure your heart.” The stories of the prophets are not entertainment; they are medicine for the heart of every caller to the truth.\n\nThe lesson for the believer: rejection is part of the path, patience is the way of the prophets, and Allah’s help is a promise that never breaks. The one who carries the truth must carry patience with it — and wait for the help that always comes.",
  "35": "“If you find their denial unbearable, then build—if you can—a tunnel through the earth or stairs to the sky to bring them a ˹more compelling˺ sign.” The verse uses a striking image to correct the Prophet’s longing. He wished so deeply for his people to believe that he kept hoping for a sign that would force them. The verse says: if you find their denial unbearable, then — if you can — dig a tunnel through the earth or climb stairs to the sky to bring them a sign. The meaning is: you cannot. No tunnel reaches a sign that will force belief, and no stairs reach a proof that will conquer stubborn hearts. The demand for a decisive sign is beyond human power, and it was never the way.\n\nThen comes the decisive statement: “Had Allah so willed, He could have guided them all.” The guidance of all people is within Allah’s power — He could have made every soul believe by force. Another chapter says: “Had your Lord so willed ˹O Prophet˺, all ˹people˺ on earth would have certainly believed, every single one of them! Would you then force people to become believers?” And another: “Had We willed, We could have easily imposed guidance on every soul.” But Allah did not will forced belief — because belief that is forced is not belief at all. He willed that people choose.\n\nThen the verse ends: “So do not be one of those ignorant ˹of this fact˺.” The Prophet is told not to behave like someone who does not know that guidance is in Allah’s hands. The lesson for every caller: do not exhaust yourself chasing the impossible. Your duty is to convey; the guidance is Allah’s. Let the heart rest in this knowledge.",
  "36": "“Only the attentive will respond ˹to your call˺.” The verse states a law of guidance: the call of the Prophet reaches everyone, but only the attentive respond. The attentive are those whose hearts are alive — those who listen, reflect, and accept the truth when it comes. The same truth is described in another chapter: “You certainly cannot make the dead hear ˹the truth˺. Nor can you make the deaf hear the call when they turn their backs and walk away.” The one who refuses to hear is like the dead: the call reaches his ears, but nothing enters.\n\nThen the verse continues: “As for the dead, Allah will raise them up, then to Him they will ˹all˺ be returned.” The word “dead” here carries two meanings. The first is the spiritual dead — those whose hearts are sealed to the truth; they will not respond to the call of any messenger, and only Allah can raise them from their spiritual death when He wills. The second meaning is the literally dead: Allah will raise every person from the grave on the Day of Judgment, and to Him they will all be returned. Both meanings are true, and the verse moves from the response of the living to the resurrection of the dead.\n\nThe verse teaches the caller of truth to keep a realistic heart: not everyone will respond, and that is not your failure. The response belongs to the attentive; the raising of the dead belongs to Allah. Your duty is the call; the results are in His hands. And it teaches the listener: be among the attentive — because the call of truth is passing by, and only the attentive will catch it.",
  "37": "“They ask, “Why has no ˹other˺ sign been sent down to him from his Lord?”” The disbelievers kept demanding signs: why has no sign been sent down to the Prophet? They were not satisfied with the Quran; they wanted something else — a miracle of their choosing. The demand sounds like a request for proof, but the Quran has already exposed it: even if they touched the proof with their hands, they would call it magic. Their demand for signs was a stalling tactic, not a search for truth.\n\nThe answer is given: “Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Allah certainly has the power to send down a sign”—though most of them do not know.” The Prophet is told to affirm Allah’s power: yes, Allah can send a sign — any sign, at any time. The ability is not the question. But the verse adds: “though most of them do not know.” They do not know that the sending of signs follows wisdom — that signs are sent when they serve guidance, and withheld when they would only bring destruction. They think the absence of their chosen sign means the Prophet is false; in reality, the withholding of the sign is mercy.\n\nThis connects with what was said earlier in this chapter about the angel: had the angel been sent, the matter would have been settled at once and they would have had no more time to repent. The sign they demand would end their chance, not extend it.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to trust Allah’s wisdom in what is given and what is withheld. Allah is powerful enough to do anything — and wise enough to do everything at the right time. The demand for signs is a demand for the timetable; the believer submits to the timetable of the All-Wise.",
  "38": "“All living beings roaming the earth and winged birds soaring in the sky are communities like yourselves.” The verse opens a window into the animal world. “All living beings roaming the earth” — the animals that walk and crawl on the land; “and winged birds soaring in the sky” — the birds that fly with their wings. The verse says these are “communities like yourselves” — nations, peoples, groups with their own lives, their own ways, their own Lord. The word “communities” is the same word used for human nations. The animals are not random machines; they are communities that live, move, feed, and return to their Lord — exactly like human communities, in the sense that both are creatures of Allah under His care.\n\nAnother chapter confirms the care: “There is no moving creature on earth whose provision is not guaranteed by Allah. And He knows where it lives and where it is laid to rest. All is ˹written˺ in a perfect Record.” Every creature’s provision is guaranteed; every creature’s place is known.\n\nThen the verse continues: “We have left nothing out of the Record.” The Record — the preserved tablet with Allah — contains everything: every creature, every action, every provision. Nothing is forgotten.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “Then to their Lord they will be gathered all together.” The animals will also be gathered to their Lord on the Day of Judgment. The Prophet said, in a hadith recorded in Sahih Muslim, that the hornless sheep will take its right from the horned sheep on the Day of Resurrection — meaning even the wrongs between animals will be settled. The verse teaches the believer to see the world with wider eyes: all creation is the creation of Allah, all life is under His care, and all of it will return to Him.",
  "39": "“Those who deny Our signs are ˹wilfully˺ deaf and dumb—lost in darkness.” The verse describes the deniers with three pictures: deaf, dumb, and lost in darkness. They are deaf — they do not hear the truth even when it is recited to them; dumb — they do not speak the truth even when they could; and lost in darkness — they wander without light. The same three pictures appear in an earlier chapter: “They are ˹wilfully˺ deaf, dumb, and blind, so they will never return ˹to the Right Path˺.” Here the third picture is darkness itself instead of blindness — the darkness that surrounds them and the darkness within them.\n\nThe verse then states a difficult truth: “Allah leaves whoever He wills to stray and guides whoever He wills to the Straight Way.” The wording raises a question many readers ask: does Allah force people to stray? The Quran answers this throughout: Allah’s leaving to stray is a response to the person’s own choice. The deniers here are described as wilfully deaf and dumb — the wilfulness is theirs. When a person insists on denial, Allah seals his heart as a just consequence, and the Quran describes that sealing as Allah’s action. The same verse of another chapter puts both sides together: “Are those whose evil-doing is made so appealing to them that they deem it good ˹like those who are rightly guided˺? ˹It is˺ certainly Allah ˹Who˺ leaves to stray whoever He wills, and guides whoever He wills.” The evil-doing is made appealing because they chose it; the leaving to stray follows their choice.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer not to judge by appearances: some people seem to be walking in light while they are in darkness, and the Straight Way is a gift that is received through the heart’s own turning.",
  "40": "“Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, “Imagine if you were overwhelmed by Allah’s torment or the Hour—would you call upon any other than Allah ˹for help˺? ˹Answer me˺ if your claims are true!”” The verse challenges the polytheists with a question about their own behaviour. Imagine — the Quran says — that Allah’s torment came upon you, or that the Hour arrived. Who would you call? Would you call your idols? Would you call your gods of stone and stars? The answer is known before they speak: in that moment, they would call upon Allah alone. “Answer me if your claims are true” — if your idols truly deserve worship and truly have power, call them now, in your mind’s eye, at the moment of greatest need.\n\nThe Quran returns to this scene in many places, because it exposes the contradiction of the polytheists. Another chapter says: “Whenever someone is touched by hardship, they cry out to Us, whether lying on their side, sitting, or standing.” And another: “When you are touched with hardship at sea, you ˹totally˺ forget all ˹the gods˺ you ˹normally˺ invoke, except Him.” In the moment of real danger, every false god disappears from the heart, and only Allah remains. The people who worshipped idols all their lives call upon Allah when the ship is sinking.\n\nThe verse teaches that the instinct to call upon Allah is planted in every human heart. The polytheist himself proves it in moments of crisis. The believer is invited to live by this instinct all the time — not only in the storm, but in the calm; not only at the edge of death, but in the middle of life.",
  "41": "“No! He is the only One you would call.” The verse answers the question of the previous verse with certainty: no — you would not call your idols; you would call Allah alone. In the moment of overwhelming danger, the false gods vanish, and the heart turns to the only One who can help. The word “No!” corrects the claim of the polytheists firmly: they claimed partners for Allah, but their own hearts know better.\n\nThen the verse continues: “And if He willed, He could remove the affliction that made you invoke Him.” The same Lord who is called in the crisis can remove the crisis. The affliction itself is from His will — it came by His decree, and it leaves by His decree. The One called in the storm is the One who controls the storm.\n\nThen comes the striking ending: “Only then will you forget whatever you associate with Him ˹in worship˺.” When Allah removes the affliction and saves them, the polytheists forget their idols — not in the sense that they stop worshipping them, but in the sense that the idols disappear from their hearts the moment real power is needed. The crisis exposes the truth: the idols were never real to them; they were habits. The same scene appears in another chapter: “If they happen to be aboard a ship ˹caught in a storm˺, they cry out to Allah ˹alone˺ in sincere devotion. But as soon as He delivers them ˹safely˺ to shore, they associate ˹others with Him once again˺.”\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the difference between faith in calm and faith in crisis. The polytheists call upon Allah in the crisis and forget Him in safety. The true believer is the opposite: he remembers Allah in safety, so that when the crisis comes, his calling is not a first meeting but a continuation of a lifelong relationship.",
  "42": "“Indeed, We have sent messengers before you ˹O Prophet˺ to other people who We put through suffering and adversity ˹for their denial˺, so perhaps they would be humbled.” The verse turns to the method of Allah with the nations of the past. Messengers were sent to many peoples — and when those peoples denied the messengers, Allah “put them through suffering and adversity.” The purpose is stated: “so perhaps they would be humbled.” The hardship was not random cruelty; it was a wake-up call. When a people refuse the message, Allah sometimes shakes their world — with illness, hunger, loss — so that their pride breaks and their hearts soften enough to turn back.\n\nThe same method is described in another chapter: “Whenever We sent a prophet to a society, We afflicted its ˹disbelieving˺ people with suffering and adversity, so perhaps they would be humbled.” The pattern is fixed: the messenger comes, the people deny, the hardship follows as a lesson.\n\nThe verse teaches a deep lesson about hardship. Not every difficulty is a punishment; some difficulties are mercy in disguise — a call to wake up. When a person or a nation faces adversity, the wise response is to ask: what is this hardship calling me to? The humble heart sees the hand of Allah in the hardship and turns to Him; the proud heart only grows harder.\n\nAnd the verse shows that the messengers were sent before the punishment — the prophets came first, the warning was given, and only then did the hardship follow. Allah never destroys a people without first sending a warner. The coming verses will show what happened when the people refused to be humbled.",
  "43": "“Why did they not humble themselves when We made them suffer?” The verse asks the painful question: the hardship came, the wake-up call was made — why did they not humble themselves? The question exposes the failure of the past nations: the suffering did not do its work. They received the warning and remained standing in their pride.\n\nThen the verse explains what happened instead: “Instead, their hearts were hardened, and Satan made their misdeeds appealing to them.” Two things replaced humility. First, their hearts hardened: the suffering that should have softened them made them harder. This is the tragedy of the sealed heart — the same fire that melts wax hardens clay. Second, Satan made their misdeeds appealing to them: their sins looked beautiful in their eyes. They saw their stubbornness as strength, their denial as freedom, their evil as good. Another chapter describes the same: “Are those whose evil-doing is made so appealing to them that they deem it good ˹like those who are rightly guided˺?”\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to check the effect of hardship on his own heart. Hardship comes to humble — but the same hardship can harden. The one who responds to difficulty with complaint, denial, and sin has lost the lesson; the one who responds with turning to Allah has gained it. The difference is not in the hardship; it is in the heart that receives it.\n\nThe verse also warns about the appeal of sin: when a person keeps sinning, sin begins to look normal, then attractive, then beautiful. Satan decorates it. The believer must catch sin early — while it still looks ugly — before the heart reaches the state where evil is deemed good.",
  "44": "“When they became oblivious to warnings, We showered them with everything they desired.” The verse describes the second stage of Allah’s dealing with the past nations. They ignored the warnings — the messengers, the hardship, the lessons all became background noise. So Allah changed the method: instead of hardship, He gave them abundance. “We showered them with everything they desired” — wealth, children, ease, everything they asked for. This is the stage that deceives people: they think the abundance means Allah is pleased with them. In reality, the abundance was part of the test — the Quran calls it the gradual leading to destruction: when a people forget Allah, He gives them more of what they love, so that their attachment to the world deepens and their return becomes harder.\n\nThen comes the sudden end: “But just as they became prideful of what they were given, We seized them by surprise, then they instantly fell into despair!” They became proud of their blessings — “look at us, we are favoured” — and at that very moment, Allah seized them by surprise. The seizure came without warning: no gradual decline, no final notice. And they fell into despair instantly — their joy turned to hopelessness in a moment.\n\nAnother chapter paints the same picture: “And Allah sets forth the example of a society which was safe and at ease, receiving its provision in abundance from all directions. But its people met Allah’s favours with ingratitude, so Allah made them taste the clutches of hunger and fear for their misdeeds.”\n\nThe verse teaches a crucial principle: blessings are not always approval, and delay is not always mercy. The believer is taught to look at the abundance of this world with the eyes of the Hereafter: is this blessing being used for Allah or against Him? And it teaches that the sudden seizure is a real possibility — so the wise servant does not wait for warnings; he turns to Allah while the door is open.",
  "45": "“So the wrongdoers were utterly uprooted.” The verse closes the story of the past nations with a final picture: they were uprooted, like plants pulled from the earth by the root. Not merely defeated — uprooted, so that nothing of their strength remained. The word pictures total destruction: the people who were showered with everything they desired, who were prideful in their blessings, were gone — their power, their wealth, their pride, all uprooted.\n\nThe verse calls them “the wrongdoers” — this is the name the Quran uses for them at the end, not “the disbelievers” only. Their wrongdoing was the denial of the truth and the harm that followed it; and the end of the wrongdoers is being uprooted.\n\nThen comes the beautiful turn: “And all praise is for Allah—Lord of all worlds.” After the destruction of the wrongdoers, the Quran turns to praise. The praise is for the justice of Allah: when the wrongdoers are uprooted and the truth is established, praise belongs to Allah, the Lord of all worlds. The same words open the first chapter of the Quran: “All praise is for Allah—Lord of all worlds.” Here the praise closes a scene of justice, as if the Quran is saying: the destruction of the wrongdoers is itself a sign of the Lord’s justice, and justice deserves praise.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to see the end of the wrongdoers with a balanced heart: not with glee, but with praise — praise for Allah’s justice, praise for the protection of the truth, praise for the Lord of all worlds who never leaves wrongdoing without an end. And it teaches that the final word of every story of this world belongs to Allah: His praise, His justice, His lordship over all worlds.",
  "46": "“Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, “Imagine if Allah were to take away your hearing or sight, or seal your hearts—who else other than Allah could restore it?”” The verse returns to the method of questions. The Prophet is told to ask the disbelievers to imagine: what if Allah took away your hearing — the ears that hear the call; or your sight — the eyes that see the signs; or sealed your hearts — the hearts that receive the truth? Who else other than Allah could restore them? The idols cannot restore a single sense. No power in creation can reopen a sealed heart except its Creator. The question forces the listener to admit: the One who gives hearing, sight, and understanding is the only One who can give them back.\n\nThen comes the observation: “See ˹O Prophet˺ how We vary the signs, yet they still turn away.” The Quran presents the proofs in every form: questions, stories, comparisons, warnings, promises — varying the signs so that every heart finds its door. And yet: they still turn away. The variety of the signs is not the problem; the turning away is.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer gratitude for his senses: hearing, sight, and a working heart are gifts from Allah — taken for granted until they are lost, and restorable only by the Giver. And it teaches the method of the Quran: it does not tire of presenting the truth in new forms. The reader is invited to stop turning away — because the signs keep coming, and the One who varies them is the One who can also restore the hearts that receive them.",
  "47": "“Ask, “Imagine if Allah’s punishment were to overwhelm you with or without warning—who would be destroyed other than the wrongdoers?”” The verse continues the series of questions. The Prophet is told to ask the disbelievers to imagine Allah’s punishment coming upon them — whether suddenly, without warning, or after a warning, as happened to the nations of the past. In both cases, the result is the same: only the wrongdoers are destroyed. The punishment does not touch the believers; it does not touch the innocent. It overwhelms the wrongdoers — those who denied the signs and persisted in wrongdoing.\n\nThe question is a reassurance wrapped in a warning. It is a warning to the wrongdoers: if the punishment comes, you will be the ones destroyed. And it is a reassurance to the believers: the punishment, whenever it comes, will only destroy the wrongdoers — the truth will not be harmed, and the people of truth will not be touched.\n\nThe verse also answers the mockers who kept asking when the punishment would come. The answer is: whenever it comes — with warning or without — its target is fixed: the wrongdoers. No one else has anything to fear from Allah’s justice.\n\nThe believer learns from this verse to stand on the side that is safe: the side of truth, the side that is not destroyed. The storms of this world — whether they come with warning or by surprise — destroy only the wrongdoers. Those who hold firmly to Allah and His signs are sheltered in the very justice that destroys the wrongdoers.",
  "48": "“We have sent messengers only as deliverers of good news and as warners.” The verse states the mission of all messengers in two titles. A “deliverer of good news” announces the reward: Paradise, forgiveness, Allah’s pleasure for those who believe and do good. A “warner” announces the danger: the Fire, the punishment, the loss for those who deny. Every prophet carried both messages — not only fear, not only hope, but the two together, because human beings need both: hope to move toward good, and fear to move away from evil.\n\nThe same description appears in this chapter’s earlier verse about the messengers to other nations, and throughout the Quran. The balance is important: a message of only fear makes people despair; a message of only hope makes people careless. The messengers came with the complete call.\n\nThen comes the promise: “Whoever believes and does good, there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.” The two words “fear” and “grieve” cover the two pains of human life: fear of what is coming, and grief over what has passed. The believer who believes and does good is promised freedom from both: no fear of the future, because his future is with Allah; no grief over the past, because his past is covered by Allah’s mercy.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the shape of the religion: belief in the heart and good deeds in the life — the two together. Belief without deeds is a claim; deeds without belief are a building without a foundation. And it teaches the believer to live without fear and without grief — not by ignoring reality, but by trusting the promise of the One who sent the messengers.",
  "49": "“But those who deny Our signs will be afflicted with punishment for their rebelliousness.” The verse is the dark mirror of the one before it. The previous verse promised the believers: no fear, no grief. This verse names the fate of the other side: “those who deny Our signs will be afflicted with punishment.” The punishment is for “their rebelliousness” — the word points to their defiant attitude, not mere ignorance. They did not deny because the signs were unclear; they rebelled against the signs that were clear. The denial was a choice, and the punishment follows the choice.\n\nThe word “rebelliousness” also connects with the repeated theme of this chapter: the deniers are described as wilfully deaf and dumb, as those who turn away from every sign, as those who fabricate lies. The punishment is the just end of a pattern, not a random strike.\n\nThe pairing of the two verses teaches the believer where the dividing line runs: not between nations, not between tribes, not between rich and poor — but between those who believe and do good, and those who deny the signs. The first group is promised safety; the second is promised punishment. The same signs are presented to both; the difference is the response.\n\nThe verse also teaches the seriousness of denial: “deny Our signs” — the signs of Allah are everywhere, and denying them is not a small matter. Every sign denied is a proof rejected. The believer is invited to be among those who receive the signs with belief — because the same signs that carry good news to the believers carry punishment for those who deny them.",
  "50": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “I do not say to you that I possess Allah’s treasuries or know the unseen, nor do I claim to be an angel. I only follow what is revealed to me.”” The verse states the Prophet’s honesty about his own rank. He does not claim to possess Allah’s treasuries — the stores of provision and miracles; he does not claim to know the unseen — the hidden knowledge that belongs to Allah alone; and he does not claim to be an angel — he is a human being, like the people he addresses. The same statement appears in the words of Noah in another chapter: “I do not say to you that I possess Allah’s treasuries or know the unseen, nor do I claim to be an angel.” The prophets all spoke the same way: with honesty about their humanity.\n\nThen comes the positive statement: “I only follow what is revealed to me.” His knowledge, his miracles, his message — all of it is revelation: he follows, he does not invent. The Prophet is a follower of revelation, not a source of it. This is the complete answer to those who demanded wonders from him: he is not a magician, not a soothsayer, not a possessor of hidden treasures — he is a servant following what his Lord revealed.\n\nThe verse then adds a question: “Say, “Are those blind ˹to the truth˺ equal to those who can see? Will you not then reflect?”” The blind are those who do not see the truth; the seeing are those who accept it. They are not equal — in this world or the next. The question ends with the invitation: “Will you not then reflect?” — think about it, and the answer will change your life.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer humility in carrying the truth: no one among us possesses treasuries or knows the unseen; we only follow what is revealed. And it teaches that the value of a person is not in claims but in following — the follower of revelation is the one who sees.",
  "51": "“Warn with this ˹Quran˺ those who are awed by the prospect of being gathered before their Lord—when they will have no protector or intercessor besides Him—so perhaps they will be mindful ˹of Him˺.” The verse directs the Prophet’s warning to a specific audience: those who are awed by the prospect of being gathered before their Lord. These are the people whose hearts tremble at the thought of standing before Allah — the sincere ones, the seekers, the believers whose faith is alive with fear and hope. The Prophet is told to warn them with the Quran.\n\nWhy warn those who already fear? Because the warning deepens their awareness and strengthens their preparation. The verse describes what awaits them on that Day: “they will have no protector or intercessor besides Him.” On the Day of Gathering, no one will protect them from Allah’s judgment, and no one will intercede without Allah’s permission. The only refuge is Allah Himself.\n\nThe same warning appears in another chapter: “O believers! Donate from what We have provided for you before the arrival of a Day when there will be no bargaining, friendship, or intercession.” On that Day, wealth will not buy, friendship will not save, and intercession will not help except by Allah’s will.\n\nThen comes the purpose: “so perhaps they will be mindful ˹of Him˺.” The warning is not to frighten for the sake of frightening; it is to produce mindfulness — the state of living with awareness of Allah, which changes how a person acts in the world.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the value of the Quran as a warning: it is not only for the deniers; it is for the believers too — to keep their hearts awake, their deeds pure, and their eyes fixed on the Day when there will be no protector or intercessor besides Him. The one who is awed by that Day prepares for it; the one who forgets it sleeps through his life.",
  "52": "“˹O Prophet!˺ Do not dismiss those ˹poor believers˺ who invoke their Lord morning and evening, seeking His pleasure.” The verse was revealed in a famous situation, reported in Sahih Muslim and other collections. Some leaders of the Quraysh came to the Prophet and said: we want to sit with you, but we cannot sit with those poor companions of yours — the slaves and the weak ones like Bilal, Ammar, Suhayb, and Salman. They asked the Prophet to dismiss those believers so that the nobles would sit with him. The verse came down forbidding him to do so: “Do not dismiss those ˹poor believers˺ who invoke their Lord morning and evening, seeking His pleasure.” The measure of a person is not wealth or rank; it is the worship of the Lord, morning and evening.\n\nThe verse adds: “You are not accountable for them whatsoever, nor are they accountable for you.” Each person carries his own account before Allah. The poor believers are not a burden on the Prophet's account, and he is not a burden on theirs. Their value is with Allah, not with the nobles of Quraysh.\n\nThen the warning: “So do not dismiss them, or you will be one of the wrongdoers.” The command is absolute, and the warning is severe: dismissing them would make the Prophet one of the wrongdoers. The same teaching appears in another chapter: “And patiently stick with those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, seeking His pleasure. Do not let your eyes look beyond them, desiring the luxuries of this worldly life.” The verse teaches the believer the true measure of people — and the danger of measuring people by the standards of the powerful instead of the standards of Allah.",
  "53": "“In this way We have tested some by means of others, so those ˹disbelievers˺ may say, “Has Allah favoured these ˹poor believers˺ out of all of us?”” The verse explains the wisdom behind the situation of the previous verse. Allah tested the rich by the poor and the poor by the rich: the rich were tested by whether they would accept the poor believers as their equals in faith, and the poor were tested by whether they would remain firm despite their poverty. The test produced the question of the disbelievers: “Has Allah favoured these ˹poor believers˺ out of all of us?” The nobles of Quraysh could not understand how Allah would choose slaves and the weak over them. Their question exposed their false measure: they thought status and wealth were the standard of Allah's favour.\n\nThe same test runs through the history of the prophets: the people of every prophet asked why the weak followed him. The question is asked by every generation that measures people by worldly standards.\n\nThen comes the answer: “Does Allah not best recognize the grateful?” Allah knows who is truly grateful for His favours — and gratitude is a matter of the heart, not of wealth. The poor believer who uses his poverty for gratitude and patience is dearer to Allah than the rich man who uses his wealth for arrogance. The verse teaches the believer to be grateful for what he has — because Allah recognizes the grateful, and their rank with Him is higher than the rank of the wealthy who are ungrateful. The test of some by means of others is not a random arrangement; it is a wise arrangement that reveals the grateful from the ungrateful.",
  "54": "“When the believers in Our revelations come to you, say, “Peace be upon you! Your Lord has taken upon Himself to be Merciful.”” The verse commands the Prophet to welcome the believers who come to him. The greeting is given in full: “Peace be upon you!” — the greeting of safety and blessing, the same greeting that will be used in Paradise. And the reason for their peace is stated: “Your Lord has taken upon Himself to be Merciful.” The same phrase appeared earlier in this chapter: “He has taken upon Himself to be Merciful.” Mercy is not something Allah may or may not show; He has written it upon Himself as His settled way.\n\nThen comes the promise: “Whoever among you commits evil ignorantly ˹or recklessly˺ then repents afterwards and mends their ways, then Allah is truly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” The verse describes the path of return in three steps. First, the evil is committed “ignorantly ˹or recklessly˺” — every sinner is in some way ignorant at the moment of sin, even when he knows the rule, because he ignores the consequences and the greatness of Allah. Second, the person “repents afterwards” — he turns back to Allah. Third, he “mends their ways” — he changes his behaviour, not only his words. For such a person, the promise is absolute: “Allah is truly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.”\n\nThe same teaching appears in another chapter: “Allah only accepts the repentance of those who commit evil ignorantly ˹or recklessly˺ then repent soon after—Allah will pardon them.” The verse teaches the believer the two sides of the religion: the mercy of Allah is vast, and the condition of receiving it is a real turning back — repentance that changes the road, not just the direction of the words.",
  "55": "“This is how We make Our signs clear, so the way of the wicked may become distinct.” The verse closes the passage about the believers who come to the Prophet and the mercy promised to them. “This is how We make Our signs clear” — the Quran presents its signs and rulings with clarity: the promise of mercy, the conditions of repentance, the distinction between the believer and the denier. The clarity is not accidental; it is the method of the Quran.\n\nThen comes the purpose: “so the way of the wicked may become distinct.” The signs are made clear so that the way of the wicked — the path of denial and wrongdoing — becomes distinct from the way of the believers. When the truth is clear, the difference between the two ways is visible, and no one can claim confusion. The word “wicked” points to those who persist in evil after the truth has been shown to them.\n\nThe verse teaches that clarity is a mercy and a responsibility. A clear sign guides the seeker and convicts the denier: the seeker finds the path, and the denier loses every excuse. The Quran does not hide its message in riddles; it makes the signs clear so that choosing the way of the wicked becomes a choice made with open eyes.\n\nAnd the verse reminds the reader where he stands: the signs have been made clear, the two ways have been distinguished — the believer walks the way of the righteous, and the wicked walk their own way toward its end. The distinction is before everyone; the choice is the reader's.",
  "56": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “I have been forbidden to worship those you invoke besides Allah.”” The Prophet is commanded to declare his position with complete clarity: he has been forbidden to worship the idols and false gods that the pagans invoke besides Allah. The word “forbidden” is strong — it is not that he chose not to; he was commanded not to. His worship is restricted by revelation, and the restriction is a protection: he worships only Allah.\n\nThen the second command: “Say, “I will not follow your desires, for I then would certainly be astray and not one of those ˹rightly˺ guided.”” The Prophet connects their idols with their desires: the worship of false gods is the worship of desires. They did not worship their idols because of evidence; they worshipped them because of desire — the desire to keep their fathers' religion, their status, their habits. If the Prophet followed their desires, he would be astray: “I then would certainly be astray and not one of those ˹rightly˺ guided.” Guidance and the following of desires are opposites: the guided one follows revelation, and the one who follows desires is astray.\n\nThe same statement appears in the earlier verses of this chapter, where the Prophet says: “I have been commanded to be the first to submit and not be one of the polytheists.” The theme of the chapter is consistent: the Prophet's position is submission to Allah alone and total separation from the worship of anything else.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the same clarity: the religion is not negotiable with desires. A person who bends his worship, his values, or his obedience to the desires of people — or to his own desires — has left the path of the guided. The believer's loyalty is fixed: Allah alone is worshipped, and desires are not followed.",
  "57": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Indeed, I stand on a clear proof from my Lord—yet you have denied it.”” The Prophet declares the ground he stands on: “a clear proof from my Lord.” His message is not guesswork, not tradition, not invention — it is built on clear proof: the revelation, the signs, the truth that cannot be mistaken. And the tragedy is added in the same breath: “yet you have denied it.” The proof is clear, and they denied it anyway. The denial is not a failure of the proof; it is a failure of the will.\n\nThen comes the reply to their taunts: “That ˹torment˺ you seek to hasten is not within my power.” The disbelievers kept mocking: bring the punishment you warn about, if you are truthful. The Prophet says: the punishment is not in my hand. I am not the one who sends it; I am only the one who warns about it. “It is only Allah Who decides ˹its time˺.” The time of punishment belongs to Allah alone — He sends it when His wisdom decides, not when the mockers demand it.\n\nThen the verse ends with two beautiful statements: “He declares the truth. And He is the Best of Judges.” Allah declares the truth — His word is the truth about everything, including the time of punishment. And He is the Best of Judges — His judgment between the Prophet and the deniers is the fairest judgment, and it will come.\n\nThe verse teaches the caller of truth to be clear about his role: he conveys the warning; he does not control the punishment. And it teaches the believer to leave judgment to the Best of Judges — the One who declares the truth and decides its time.",
  "58": "“Say ˹also˺, “If what you seek to hasten were within my power, the matter between us would have already been settled.”” The verse continues the Prophet's answer to the mockers who demanded the punishment. He says: if the punishment you ask for were in my power, I would have already settled the matter — the affair between us would have been finished. The meaning is not that the Prophet would punish out of anger; the meaning is that the punishment belongs to Allah alone, and its timing is His. The Prophet has no personal power to bring it; he only delivers the warning.\n\nThen comes the decisive statement: “But Allah knows the wrongdoers best.” The mockers think they can escape; they think their mockery is safe. But Allah knows the wrongdoers best — He knows them, their deeds, their plans, and their true state. The verse implies both a warning and a reassurance: a warning to the wrongdoers that Allah's knowledge covers everything they do, and a reassurance to the Prophet that the One who knows the wrongdoers will deal with them in His perfect time.\n\nThe same idea appears in another chapter: “If Allah were to hasten evil for people as they wish to hasten good, they would have certainly been doomed.” If punishments came the moment they were demanded, no one would survive. The delay of punishment is itself a mercy — a door left open for repentance.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to trust Allah's timing. The wrongdoers may seem to prosper and mock without consequence — but Allah knows them best, and the matter between truth and falsehood will be settled by the One who knows everything, at the time He has chosen.",
  "59": "“With Him are the keys of the unseen—no one knows them except Him.” The verse opens one of the greatest statements of Allah's knowledge. “The keys of the unseen” — the treasures of hidden knowledge — are with Allah, and no one knows them except Him. The Prophet spoke of these keys in a hadith recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari, saying that the keys of the unseen are five, and then he recited the verse from another chapter: “Indeed, Allah ˹alone˺ has the knowledge of the Hour. He sends down the rain, and knows what is in the wombs. No soul knows what it will earn for tomorrow, and no soul knows in what land it will die.” Five things: the Hour, the rain, what is in the wombs, tomorrow's earnings, and the land of death — the unseen that belongs to Allah alone.\n\nThen the verse widens the picture: “And He knows what is in the land and sea.” His knowledge covers every place: the land, the sea, the depths, the surfaces. And then the smallest details: “Not even a leaf falls without His knowledge, nor a grain in the darkness of the earth or anything—green or dry—but is ˹written˺ in a perfect Record.” A single leaf falling from a tree — Allah knows it. A grain buried in the darkness of the earth — Allah knows it. Everything green and dry, alive and dead, moving and still — all of it is written in a perfect Record.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the meaning of Allah's knowledge: nothing is too small for Him. The leaf, the grain, the hidden seed — all are known and recorded. If Allah's knowledge covers the leaf that falls, how could it not cover the deeds of a person — his words, his thoughts, his secret acts? The awareness of this knowledge is the foundation of mindfulness: the believer lives knowing that his whole life, down to the smallest detail, is in the knowledge and the Record of Allah.",
  "60": "“He is the One Who calls back your souls by night and knows what you do by day, then revives you daily to complete your appointed term.” The verse describes the daily miracle of sleep and wakefulness. “He is the One Who calls back your souls by night” — when a person sleeps, Allah takes back his soul in a kind of mini-death: the soul is lifted, the senses are closed, the person is unaware of the world. Then “He knows what you do by day” — the waking hours are also under His knowledge: every action, every word, every movement of the day is known. Then “revives you daily to complete your appointed term” — every morning, Allah returns the soul to the body, waking the person again, so that his appointed lifespan can continue to its end.\n\nAnother chapter explains the same mystery: “˹It is˺ Allah ˹Who˺ calls back the souls ˹of people˺ upon their death as well as ˹the souls˺ of the living during their sleep. Then He keeps those for whom He has ordained death, and releases the others until ˹their˺ appointed time.” Sleep is the sister of death: every night, the soul is returned to its Lord; every morning, it is sent back — until the morning when it is not sent back.\n\nThen the verse ends: “To Him is your ˹ultimate˺ return, then He will inform you of what you used to do.” The daily cycle of sleep and waking is a rehearsal for the final return: the One who takes the soul at night takes it at death, and the One who revives daily will raise all people on the Day of Judgment. Then He will inform them of what they used to do — the full record of their days and nights.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to see the hand of Allah in his own bed: every sleep is a trust, every waking is a return, and every day is a page added to the record that will be read on the Day of return.",
  "61": "“He reigns supreme over all of His creation, and sends recording-angels, watching over you.” The verse repeats the opening statement of the earlier verse — “He reigns supreme over His creation” — and now adds the recording-angels. The One who reigns supreme sends angels who watch over every person: angels who record, angels who protect, angels who witness. The picture is of complete supervision: the supreme King has placed watchers over His servants. Another chapter describes them: “As the two recording-angels— ˹one˺ sitting to the right, and ˹the other to˺ the left—note ˹everything˺.” And another: “while you are certainly observed by vigilant, honourable angels, recording ˹everything˺.”\n\nThen the verse continues: “When death comes to any of you, Our angels take their soul, never neglecting this duty.” At the appointed moment, the angels come to take the soul — they do not delay it and do not rush it; they never neglect the duty assigned to them. The taking of the soul is not random; it is a duty performed by the angels of the supreme King, at the time He has fixed.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to live under the awareness of the watchers: the recording-angels are with him day and night, on the right and on the left, noting everything. And it teaches the believer to prepare for the moment when the angels come to take his soul — because that moment is fixed, and the duty of the angels is never neglected. The awareness of the recording-angels keeps the believer's words clean and his deeds straight; the awareness of the final taking keeps his heart ready.",
  "62": "“Then they are ˹all˺ returned to Allah— their True Master.” The verse describes the final return. After the soul is taken by the angels, the person is returned to Allah — “their True Master.” The phrase is deep: Allah is the True Master, the only real owner and judge. All the masters of this world — kings, rulers, owners, guardians — are temporary and false in comparison. The return to the True Master is the return to reality: everything false falls away, and the servant stands before the One who truly owns him.\n\nThen the verse adds: “Judgment is His ˹alone˺.” The judgment on that Day belongs to Allah alone — no one shares it, no one overrules it, no one appeals it. The judgment of the True Master is the final word on every deed.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “And He is the Swiftest Reckoner.” The reckoning of Allah is swift — He does not need time, records, or deliberation like human judges; His knowledge is complete, and His reckoning is instant. The Prophet taught that on the Day of Judgment, the reckoning of the creation will happen quickly, and Allah's justice will settle every account. The swiftness is not carelessness; it is the perfection of knowledge: the One who knows everything does not need to search for anything.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to live in the awareness of the return: every person will be returned to the True Master, judged by His judgment alone, and reckoned with swiftness. The one who lives with this awareness prepares his account before the return — because the return comes to everyone, and the reckoning of the Swiftest Reckoner waits for no one.",
  "63": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Who rescues you from the darkest times on land and at sea? He ˹alone˺ you call upon with humility, openly and secretly: \"If You rescue us from this, we will be ever grateful.\"\"” The Prophet is commanded to ask the polytheists a question about their own experience. “The darkest times on land and at sea” — the moments of extreme danger: the storm at sea, the darkness of the night, the loss of direction, the edge of death. In those moments, who rescues? The idols cannot; the false gods cannot; the statues are silent. The answer is known to every person who has been in such a moment.\n\nThe verse continues with the description of what happens in those moments: people call upon Allah alone — “with humility, openly and secretly” — the proudest person becomes humble, the loudest becomes quiet, and the secret prayer of the heart joins the open prayer of the tongue. And they promise: “If You rescue us from this, we will be ever grateful.” The same scene appears in another chapter: “He is the One Who enables you to travel through land and sea... They cry out to Allah ˹alone˺ in sincere devotion, “If You save us from this, we will certainly be grateful.”” And in another: “When you are touched with hardship at sea, you ˹totally˺ forget all ˹the gods˺ you ˹normally˺ invoke, except Him.”\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the truth about the human heart: in the moment of real danger, every false god falls away, and only Allah remains. The polytheist himself proves it in the storm. The believer is invited to make this instinct his permanent state — calling upon Allah with humility in the calm as well as in the storm, and keeping the promise of gratitude after every rescue.",
  "64": "“Say, \"˹Only˺ Allah rescues you from this and any other distress, yet you associate others with Him ˹in worship˺.\"” The verse completes the answer to the question of the previous verse. Who rescues you from the darkest times? “˹Only˺ Allah rescues you” — no one else. The rescue from the storm, from the darkness, from the edge of death — it is from Allah alone. And not only from that particular distress: “and any other distress” — every distress, every difficulty, every danger: the Rescuer is one.\n\nThen comes the contradiction: “yet you associate others with Him ˹in worship˺.” The verse exposes the inconsistency of the polytheists: in the moment of danger, they call upon Allah alone; when the danger passes, they return to worshipping others beside Him. They prove with their own hearts that the rescuer is Allah — and then they give His worship to others. Another chapter records the same contradiction: “If they happen to be aboard a ship ˹caught in a storm˺, they cry out to Allah ˹alone˺ in sincere devotion. But as soon as He delivers them ˹safely˺ to shore, they associate ˹others with Him once again˺.”\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the standard of consistency: the worship of the crisis and the worship of the calm must be the same. The person who remembers Allah in danger and forgets Him in safety has learned nothing from the rescue. The believer is taught to see every rescue — from the storm, from the illness, from the difficulty — as a sign of the Rescuer, and to keep his worship for the One who rescues, in safety exactly as in danger.",
  "65": "“Say, \"He ˹alone˺ has the power to unleash upon you a torment from above or below you or split you into ˹conflicting˺ factions and make you taste the violence of one another.\"” The verse states the complete power of Allah over the people who deny Him. He can send torment “from above” — like the stones and the flood that destroyed past nations; or “from below” — like the earth swallowing communities; or He can “split you into ˹conflicting˺ factions and make you taste the violence of one another” — the punishment of division, when people turn against each other and taste each other's violence.\n\nThe Prophet reacted to this verse with deep seriousness. In a hadith recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari, when the verse about the torment from above or below was revealed, the Prophet said: “I seek refuge with Your Face” — seeking Allah's protection from these two torments. And when the verse about the splitting into factions was revealed, he said: “This is easier” — meaning the division is lighter than the destruction from above or below. The hadith shows the Prophet's fear of Allah's power and his complete trust in Allah's protection.\n\nThe verse then closes: “See how We vary the signs, so perhaps they will comprehend.” The Quran presents the warnings in different forms — from above, from below, through division — so that people may comprehend the power of Allah and turn back.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the reality of Allah's power: the One who created the heavens can send punishment from them; the One who created the earth can raise it against its people; and the One who united the community can divide it. The believer fears Allah's power and seeks His protection — and works to keep the community united, knowing that division is itself one of the great punishments.",
  "66": "“Still your people ˹O Prophet˺ have rejected this ˹Quran˺, although it is the truth.” The verse turns from the power of Allah to the rejection of the Prophet's own people. “Your people” — the Quraysh, the Prophet's own tribe and family — rejected the Quran, even though it is the truth. The word “still” carries the sadness: after all the signs, all the warnings, all the proofs, they still rejected. The rejection of the closest people is the hardest rejection: the Prophet's own relatives, his own tribe, the people who knew him best — they turned away from the truth they could not deny.\n\nThen comes the Prophet's position: “Say, \"I am not a keeper over you.\"” The Prophet is not a keeper — not a guardian forced to control their beliefs, not a jailer of their hearts. His duty is delivery, not compulsion. The same statement appears in other places of the Quran, and it settles the limits of the messenger's role: he conveys, he warns, he calls — and he does not force.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer two lessons. First, the pain of rejection: the people closest to the truth can be the farthest from it, and a caller of truth may face rejection from his own family and community — the Prophet faced it, and it is not a sign of failure. Second, the limit of the caller's duty: you are not a keeper over anyone. You deliver the truth with love and clarity, and you leave the hearts to Allah. The keeper is Allah; the caller is only a conveyor.",
  "67": "“Every ˹destined˺ matter has a ˹set˺ time to transpire. And you will soon come to know.” The verse is a calm answer to all the mockers who demanded immediate punishment. “Every ˹destined˺ matter has a ˹set˺ time” — everything that Allah has decreed has an appointed time: the punishment of the deniers, the victory of the truth, the coming of the Hour, the death of every person. Nothing comes before its time, and nothing comes after it. The same truth appears in another chapter: “Every destined matter has a ˹set˺ time.” The mockers demanded the punishment as if it were late; the verse says: it has a time, and the time is fixed.\n\nThen the verse adds: “And you will soon come to know.” The mockers will know — soon — when the destined matter arrives. The promise of knowledge is a warning: what is delayed is not cancelled; the time will come, and then they will know what they mocked.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the virtue of patience with Allah's timing. The truth may seem slow; the wrongdoers may seem victorious; the punishment may seem absent. But every destined matter has its set time, and the believer's certainty does not depend on what he sees — it depends on the promise of the One who set the times. And it teaches the mocker to stop mocking: the matter is coming, and you will soon come to know. The knowledge that comes at the time of the punishment is not the knowledge of faith; it is the knowledge of regret.",
  "68": "“And when you come across those who ridicule Our revelations, do not sit with them unless they engage in a different topic.” The verse sets a rule for the believer's company: if he finds himself in a gathering where the revelations of Allah are being ridiculed — mocked, joked about, belittled — he must not sit with them. The command is practical: leave the gathering, or stay only if they change the subject. The believer cannot sit quietly while the Book of Allah is mocked; his presence would be a silent approval. The same rule appears in another chapter: “He has already revealed to you in the Book that when you hear Allah’s revelations being denied or ridiculed, then do not sit in that company unless they engage in a different topic, or else you will be like them.” Sitting with the mockers makes the believer “like them” — the silent listener shares the guilt.\n\nThen comes the exception for forgetfulness: “Should Satan make you forget, then once you remember, do not ˹continue to˺ sit with the wrongdoing people.” The believer may forget and sit; the moment he remembers, he must get up. The verse is gentle with human weakness: forgetfulness happens, and the remedy is the remembrance — the moment you remember, act.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the value of his presence: where you sit, you vote. Sitting with the mockers of the revelation is a vote for their mockery; leaving is a vote for the truth. And it teaches the practical method: you do not need to argue with every mocker; you need to guard your company. The believer protects his faith by protecting his seat.",
  "69": "“Those mindful ˹of Allah˺ will not be accountable for those ˹who ridicule it˺ whatsoever—their duty is to advise, so perhaps the ridiculers will abstain.” The verse answers a question the believers might have asked: if we leave the gatherings of the mockers, are we responsible for their mockery? Are we accountable for their deeds? The verse says: no — “Those mindful ˹of Allah˺ will not be accountable for those ˹who ridicule it˺ whatsoever.” Each person carries his own account: the mockers will answer for their mockery, and the mindful believers will answer for their own deeds. The believer is not a keeper over the mocker, as the earlier verse said about the Prophet's people.\n\nBut then comes the balance: “their duty is to advise, so perhaps the ridiculers will abstain.” The believer is not accountable for the mocker, yet he is not free of duty toward him: the duty is advice — reminding, warning, calling with gentleness. The purpose is stated with the word “perhaps”: the advice may work, and the ridiculers may stop. The word “perhaps” shows that the result is not in the adviser's hands; the duty is his, and the outcome is Allah's.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the complete picture of dealing with the mockers: do not sit with them in their mockery; do not carry guilt for their choices; and do not stop advising them. The three together keep the believer balanced: separated from the sin, free from the burden, and still calling to the truth. The duty of advice continues even when the hope is only a “perhaps” — because the seed of guidance may grow where it is least expected.",
  "70": "“And leave those who take this faith ˹of Islam˺ as mere play and amusement and are deluded by ˹their˺ worldly life.” The verse commands the Prophet to leave the company of those who treat the faith as a joke. “Take this faith ˹of Islam˺ as mere play and amusement” — they laugh at the religion, mock its rituals, and treat its commands as entertainment. And they are “deluded by ˹their˺ worldly life” — deceived by the glitter of the world into thinking it is everything. For such people, the command is: leave them — do not sit with them, do not argue with them endlessly, do not waste the call on hearts that treat it as a game.\n\nBut then comes the exception that keeps the door open: “Yet remind them by this ˹Quran˺.” Leave them, but remind them. The leaving is not abandonment; it is a change of method. The reminder continues — through the Quran — because the reminder may reach a heart that the argument could not.\n\nThen the verse explains the danger they face: “so no one should be ruined for their misdeeds. They will have no protector or intercessor other than Allah. Even if they were to offer every ˹possible˺ ransom, none will be accepted from them.” On the Day of Judgment, the one ruined by his misdeeds will find no protector and no intercessor except Allah — and no ransom will be accepted, not even every possible ransom. The same warning appears in another chapter: “before the arrival of a Day when there will be no bargaining, friendship, or intercession.”\n\nAnd the verse ends with their fate: “Those are the ones who will be ruined for their misdeeds. They will have a boiling drink and painful punishment for their disbelief.” The boiling drink and the painful punishment — the end of those who played with the faith and were deluded by the world. The verse teaches the believer to take the faith seriously — it is not a game, and the world is not the prize.",
  "71": "“Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, \"Should we invoke, other than Allah, those ˹idols˺ which cannot benefit or harm us, and turn back to disbelief after Allah has guided us? ˹If we do so, we will be˺ like those disoriented by devils in the wilderness, while their companions call them to guidance, ˹saying˺, 'Come to us!'\"” The Prophet is commanded to ask the polytheists a question that exposes the absurdity of their call. They wanted the Prophet to return to the worship of their idols — to turn back to disbelief after Allah had guided him. The question is: should we invoke idols that cannot benefit or harm us? The idols have no power to bring good and no power to push away harm — so what would be the point of invoking them? And should we turn back to disbelief after guidance? The two are joined: worshipping the powerless idols is turning back to disbelief.\n\nThen comes the picture of the lost one: “like those disoriented by devils in the wilderness, while their companions call them to guidance, ˹saying˺, 'Come to us!'” The one who turns back after guidance is like a person lost in the wilderness, disoriented by devils, while his companions stand on the road and call to him: come to us! The picture is full of movement: the lost one wanders, the devils confuse him, and the companions keep calling. Another chapter paints a similar picture of those driven by Satan: “Those who consume interest will stand ˹on Judgment Day˺ like those driven to madness by Satan’s touch.”\n\nThen the Prophet's answer: \"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Allah's guidance is the ˹only˺ true guidance. And we are commanded to submit to the Lord of all worlds.\"\" The guidance of Allah is the only true guidance; everything else is wandering. And the command upon the believers is clear: submit to the Lord of all worlds — not to idols, not to desires, not to the calls of the wilderness, but to the Lord of everything. The verse teaches the believer to hear the call of the companions on the road — the call of guidance — and to answer it with submission to the Lord of all worlds.",
  "72": "“Establish prayer, and be mindful of Him. To Him you will all be gathered together.” The verse follows the statement of the previous verse, where the believers declared: “And we are commanded to submit to the Lord of all worlds.” Now the command is spelled out in action: “Establish prayer, and be mindful of Him.” The two commands belong together — prayer is the standing worship of the body, and mindfulness is the standing awareness of the heart. Prayer without mindfulness becomes movement without presence; mindfulness without prayer becomes a feeling without action. The Quran joins them so that the believer worships with body and heart together.\n\nThe command to establish prayer appears throughout the Quran as one of the first duties of the believer — it was the command given to every prophet, and it is the pillar that holds the religion. Here it follows the declaration of submission, because submission to Allah is shown in the prayer: the servant stands before his Lord, bows to Him, and prostrates to Him — the body itself confessing what the tongue declared.\n\nThen comes the second half: “To Him you will all be gathered together.” The prayer is performed in this world, and the gathering is in the next: the One who is worshipped in prayer is the One to whom everyone will return. The verse teaches the believer to connect his worship with his hope: every prayer is a rehearsal for the gathering, every prostration is a practice for standing before the Lord. The one who establishes prayer and is mindful of Him lives his whole life facing the gathering — and the gathering, for him, is not a terror but a return to the One he already knows through prayer.",
  "73": "“He is the One Who created the heavens and the earth in truth.” The verse returns to the creation: the heavens and the earth were not created for play or by chance — they were created “in truth,” with purpose, wisdom, and justice. The creation is not a meaningless accident; it is the work of a wise Creator, and it points to its Creator.\n\nThen comes the picture of the Day of Judgment: “On the Day ˹of Judgment˺ He will say, 'Be!' And there will be!” The resurrection itself is described as a command: Allah says “Be!” and the new creation comes into existence — instantly, effortlessly, exactly as the first creation came. The same teaching appears in another chapter: “All it takes, when He wills something ˹to be˺, is simply to say to it: “Be!” And it is!” The One who created the heavens and the earth in truth is the One whose command “Be!” raises the dead. The resurrection is no harder for Him than the first creation — both are simply His command.\n\nThe verse continues: “His command is truth. All authority is His ˹alone˺ on the Day the Trumpet will be blown.” On the Day of the Trumpet — the day the trumpet is blown to raise the dead — all authority belongs to Allah alone. No king, no judge, no master of this world has any say. “He is the Knower of all—seen or unseen. And He is the All-Wise, All-Aware.” The One who commands the resurrection knows everything — what was seen and what was hidden — and His wisdom and awareness cover everything He does.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the connection between creation and resurrection: the same power that made the world will remake it, and the same truth that built the heavens will judge the earth. The command “Be!” that created is the command “Be!” that resurrects — and the believer lives in the certainty of both.",
  "74": "“And ˹remember˺ when Abraham said to his father, Ȃzar, \"Do you take idols as gods? It is clear to me that you and your people are entirely misguided.\"” The chapter now turns to the story of Abraham — the father of the prophets, the one whose argument against idol worship is the model for all who call to the oneness of Allah. The Quran commands the Prophet to remember this scene: Abraham speaking to his own father, Ȃzar — the closest relation a person has. Abraham's call began at home, with his father.\n\nHis question is direct and gentle at the same time: “Do you take idols as gods?” The question exposes the absurdity: these are idols — stones carved by human hands — and you take them as gods? And then Abraham states his certainty: “It is clear to me that you and your people are entirely misguided.” He does not say “perhaps” or “maybe”; he says the truth is clear to him: worshipping idols is misguidance, and his father and his people are in it.\n\nThe same scene appears in other chapters of the Quran. In one chapter, Abraham asks his father: “O dear father! Why do you worship what can neither hear nor see, nor benefit you at all?” In another, he asks his people: “What are you worshipping?” — and the answer is always the same: the idols are false, and the call is to Allah alone.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the manners of calling to the truth: begin with the closest, speak with clarity, and do not soften the truth out of love for the listener. Abraham loved his father — and he still told him the clear truth. Love does not cancel truth; it delivers it more gently and more honestly.",
  "75": "“We also showed Abraham the wonders of the heavens and the earth, so he would be sure in faith.” The verse describes the education of Abraham. After his statement against the idols, Allah showed him “the wonders of the heavens and the earth” — the marvels of creation: the stars, the planets, the sun, the moon, the earth with its signs. The purpose is stated clearly: “so he would be sure in faith.” Abraham was a prophet with certain faith, and Allah increased his certainty by opening his eyes to the wonders of the universe. Faith and reflection belong together: the believer looks at the creation and sees the Creator; the wonders of the heavens and the earth are verses written in the universe.\n\nThe same call is addressed to everyone in another chapter: “Have they ever reflected on the wonders of the heavens and the earth, and everything Allah has created, and that perhaps their end is near?” The wonders are open to all — the One who made them is known through them.\n\nThe verses that follow will show Abraham in the night, looking at the star, the moon, and the sun — using the wonders he was shown to build his argument against the worshippers of created things. The stars set, the moon sets, the sun sets — and the Lord does not set. The One who made all of them is above them all.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the value of reflection: certainty is built by looking at the signs of Allah with an open heart. The heavens and the earth are not silent; they are speaking — and the one who learns their language becomes sure in faith.",
  "76": "“When the night grew dark upon him, he saw a star and said, \"This is my Lord!\" But when it set, he said, \"I do not love things that set.\"” The verse begins the famous scene of Abraham under the night sky. “When the night grew dark upon him, he saw a star” — one star shining in the darkness. And Abraham said: \"This is my Lord!\" The words are part of Abraham's argument, and the commentators explain them in two ways. Some say this was Abraham's reasoning before he reached the rank of prophethood — he was searching for his Lord, testing the things his people worshipped, and Allah guided him from one stage to the next. Others say he said it as a challenge to his people, using their own method to show its emptiness — as if saying: you worship this star? Very well, let me worship it as you do — and then the star sets, and its worship is exposed as false.\n\nWhichever way it is read, the argument is the same: “when it set, he said, \"I do not love things that set.\"” The star set — it disappeared. A god that disappears is not a god; a lord that vanishes is not a Lord. The words “I do not love things that set” are among the most beautiful in the Quran: the true beloved — the true object of worship — does not set, does not vanish, does not disappear. The One who deserves love is the One who never leaves.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the test of every false god: does it set? Does it change? Does it end? Whatever sets, changes, or ends cannot be the Lord. The worship of created things is worship of things that set — and the heart that loves them will be left in the dark when they disappear.",
  "77": "“Then when he saw the moon rising, he said, \"This one is my Lord!\" But when it disappeared, he said, \"If my Lord does not guide me, I will certainly be one of the misguided people.\"” The scene continues: the star set, and then the moon rose — bigger, brighter, more impressive than the star. Abraham said: \"This one is my Lord!\" — and then the moon also disappeared, and the argument was complete for the second time. The moon set like the star; the second false god failed the same test.\n\nBut the verse adds something new: “If my Lord does not guide me, I will certainly be one of the misguided people.” The words are full of humility. Abraham had been shown the wonders of the heavens and the earth, and he was being guided from sign to sign — yet he did not claim the guidance as his own achievement. He said: if my Lord does not guide me, I would be lost. The guidance was from Allah, and Abraham knew it: his certainty was a gift, not a right.\n\nThis sentence is also part of the argument: the people worshipped the moon, and the moon itself needed a Lord to guide it — it rose and set by His command. If the moon is a thing that disappears, it cannot be the guide; the Guide is the One who never disappears and who guides both the moon and Abraham.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer two lessons. First, the false gods keep failing the same test: everything that rises sets, and everything that appears disappears — only Allah remains. Second, guidance is a gift: the believer never outgrows his need for his Lord's guidance, and the prayer “guide us” remains on his tongue until the end.",
  "78": "“Then when he saw the sun shining, he said, \"This must be my Lord—it is the greatest!\" But again when it set, he declared, \"O my people! I totally reject whatever you associate ˹with Allah in worship˺.\"” The scene reaches its climax. The sun rose — the greatest light, the most impressive object in the sky. Abraham said: \"This must be my Lord—it is the greatest!\" — the third and final stage. The star set, the moon set, and now the sun set. The greatest light disappeared like the smallest star. The test was complete: every created light sets, and the Lord of light does not set.\n\nAt that moment, Abraham turned to his people and declared his position openly: \"O my people! I totally reject whatever you associate ˹with Allah in worship˺.\" The word “totally” is decisive: he rejected all of it — the star, the moon, the sun, the idols, every created thing they worshipped. His rejection was not partial: he did not keep one god among their gods; he rejected the whole category of false worship. And his address “O my people!” shows that his rejection was not hatred — it was a call: I am telling you the truth, because I am one of you and I want the truth for you.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the completeness of tawhid: the declaration of Allah's oneness includes the rejection of every partner. “I totally reject whatever you associate with Allah” — this is the other half of “there is no god but Allah”: the rejection of all false gods and the affirmation of the One God. The believer's worship is not complete until his rejection is complete.",
  "79": "“I have turned my face towards the One Who has originated the heavens and the earth—being upright—and I am not one of the polytheists.\"” The verse completes Abraham's declaration. After rejecting the false gods, he states his own position: “I have turned my face towards the One Who has originated the heavens and the earth.” The “face” here means the whole self — the direction of the heart, the intention, the entire being turned toward Allah. The One he turns to is “the One Who has originated the heavens and the earth” — the Originator, the Creator who made everything from nothing. The direction of Abraham's worship is the direction of creation itself: toward the Originator, not toward the originated; toward the Creator, not toward the created.\n\nThen come two qualifications. First: “being upright” — his turning was not crooked, not partial, not mixed: he turned to Allah alone, standing straight on the path of truth. The word carries the meaning of complete sincerity and devotion, without any deviation to the right or the left. Second: “and I am not one of the polytheists” — he declares his separation from the worship of partners, in the clearest words.\n\nThe verse is the summary of Abraham's whole mission: turn to the Originator, be upright, and never be a polytheist. The same declaration is the core of every believer's faith. And because Abraham turned his face to Allah being upright, Allah made him the father of the prophets and the friend of Allah — the one whose way every believer is commanded to follow, as the coming verses will show. The verse teaches the believer that the direction of his life — his face, his heart, his deeds — must be turned to Allah alone, uprightly and completely.",
  "80": "“And his people argued with him. He responded, \"Are you arguing with me about Allah, while He has guided me? I am not afraid of whatever ˹idols˺ you associate with Him—˹none can harm me,˺ unless my Lord so wills. My Lord encompasses everything in ˹His˺ knowledge. Will you not be mindful?\"” The scene moves from the night sky to the argument. Abraham's people came to argue with him — they could not answer his proofs, so they argued: about the idols, about the religion of their fathers, about his rejection of their gods. Abraham's answer is remarkable: “Are you arguing with me about Allah, while He has guided me?” The argument is absurd: he has been guided by Allah Himself — through the wonders of the heavens and the earth, through the setting of the star, the moon, and the sun — and they want to argue him out of his guidance. The one who has been guided does not need to fear the arguments of those who are lost.\n\nThen he continues: “I am not afraid of whatever ˹idols˺ you associate with Him—˹none can harm me,˺ unless my Lord so wills.” The people probably threatened him with the anger of their gods — the idols will harm you, they warned. Abraham answers: I am not afraid of your idols; they cannot harm me at all — unless my Lord wills something, because nothing happens except by His will. The idols have no power; the only power is Allah's.\n\nAnd he adds: “My Lord encompasses everything in ˹His˺ knowledge. Will you not be mindful?” His Lord's knowledge surrounds everything — the idols, their worshippers, their plots, their threats — all of it is within Allah's knowledge. The question “Will you not be mindful?” is the call of the prophet: if you understood that Allah's knowledge encompasses everything, you would fear Him instead of your idols. The verse teaches the believer the confidence of the guided: arguments do not shake him, threats do not frighten him, and the knowledge of his Lord surrounds everything he faces.",
  "81": "“And how should I fear your associate-gods, while you have no fear in associating ˹others˺ with Allah—a practice He has never authorized?” The verse completes Abraham's argument with a powerful comparison. He asks: how should I fear your associate-gods? The idols have no power to harm — fearing them is empty fear. And in the same breath: while you have no fear in associating others with Allah — the greatest crime — a practice He has never authorized. The comparison is devastating: Abraham is asked to fear things that are powerless, while his people commit the worst wrong without any fear. The one who should fear is the one who associates partners with Allah; the one who is safe is the one who worships Him alone.\n\nThe phrase “a practice He has never authorized” is the key: the worship of partners is not something Allah commanded or permitted — it is an invention of the worshippers. No prophet brought it, no scripture revealed it, no evidence supported it. The worship of Allah alone, by contrast, is what every prophet called to and every scripture confirmed.\n\nThen comes the question: “Which side has more right to security? ˹Tell me˺ if you really know!” Two sides stand before the question: the side that fears powerless idols and worships the powerful Lord, and the side that fears nothing while committing the greatest wrong. Which side has more right to security — to safety from punishment? The answer is obvious: the one who worships Allah alone. The next verse will state the answer directly.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer where true fear belongs: fear of Allah, not fear of false gods; and where true security is found: with Allah, not with idols.",
  "82": "“It is ˹only˺ those who are faithful and do not tarnish their faith with falsehood who are guaranteed security and are ˹rightly˺ guided.” The verse answers the question of the previous verse: who has more right to security? The answer: “those who are faithful and do not tarnish their faith with falsehood.” The word “falsehood” here means the association of partners with Allah — shirk. The verse describes the believers as those who keep their faith pure: they do not mix the worship of Allah with the worship of anything else.\n\nThe Companions were deeply moved by this verse, and the story is recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim. They came to the Prophet troubled, saying: which of us does not tarnish his faith with falsehood? Every person wrongs himself in some way — so who is safe? The Prophet explained: the verse does not mean every wrongdoing; it means the association of partners with Allah. And he pointed to the words of Luqman to his son: “O my dear son! Never associate ˹anything˺ with Allah ˹in worship˺, for associating ˹others with Him˺ is truly the worst of all wrongs.” The “falsehood” that tarnishes faith and destroys security is shirk — the one wrong that cancels everything else.\n\nFor those who keep their faith pure from shirk, the promise is double: “guaranteed security and are ˹rightly˺ guided.” Security in this life and the next — safety from the punishment — and guidance on the straight path. The same teaching appears in another chapter: “if you associate others ˹with Allah˺, your deeds will certainly be void and you will truly be one of the losers.” The verse teaches the believer the heart of the religion: faith must be kept pure from the falsehood of shirk, and the pure faith carries security and guidance — the two gifts every servant needs.",
  "83": "“This was the argument We gave Abraham against his people.” The verse closes the story of Abraham's debate with a divine seal: the argument Abraham used — the setting of the star, the moon, the sun; the fearlessness before the idols; the question of security — was not his own invention. “This was the argument We gave Abraham” — Allah gave him the proof, taught him the argument, and supported him with it against his people. The prophets argue with the proof of Allah: their arguments are revelation-guided, not personal opinions.\n\nThe verse continues: “We elevate in rank whoever We please.” The elevation of Abraham — and of every prophet — is from Allah: He chose them, guided them, and raised their ranks. The ranks of the prophets are not earned by tribal pride or ancestry alone; they are gifts of Allah placed in hearts He knows. And the verse adds: “Surely your Lord is All-Wise, All-Knowing.” The elevation is not random: the All-Wise knows where to place His favours, and the All-Knowing knows who deserves them.\n\nThe story of Abraham's argument is also a lesson in how the truth confronts falsehood: with clear proof, with calm courage, with the question that exposes the emptiness of idols. The same argument is available to every believer who faces the false gods of his time — the idols of wealth, power, desire, and custom: they set, they disappear, they cannot harm or benefit — and the Lord who originated the heavens and the earth is the only One worthy of worship. The argument that defeated the idols of Abraham's people is the argument that defeats every false god.",
  "84": "“And We blessed him with Isaac and Jacob.” The verse moves from Abraham's argument to his family: the descendants of Abraham who carried the message after him. He was blessed with Isaac — his son — and Jacob — Isaac's son, also called Israel, the father of the twelve tribes. The blessing is not only the gift of children; it is the gift of prophetic children, the continuation of the mission.\n\nThen the verse lists the line of guided prophets: “We guided them all as We previously guided Noah and those among his descendants: David, Solomon, Job, Joseph, Moses, and Aaron.” The list is a roll-call of the great prophets: Noah — the second father of humanity after the flood; then David — the king-prophet who was given the Psalms; Solomon — David's son, the prophet-king of wisdom and power; Job — the prophet of patience in affliction; Joseph — the prophet of the dream, the prison, and the throne of Egypt; Moses — the prophet of the Torah, the one who spoke with Allah; and Aaron — Moses' brother, the prophet who supported him. The same line of prophets is mentioned in another chapter: “We also sent revelation to Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and his descendants, ˹as well as˺ Jesus, Job, Jonah, Aaron, and Solomon. And to David We gave the Psalms.”\n\nEach of these prophets was guided — chosen and directed by Allah — and each carried the same message: the worship of Allah alone. The verse closes with the general rule: “This is how We reward the good-doers.” Guidance is the reward of goodness: these prophets were guided because they were good-doers — people of faith, patience, and righteousness. The verse teaches the believer that the line of guidance runs through the good-doers: the reward of doing good is being guided to more good, and the highest reward of the good-doers is the company of the prophets.",
  "85": "“Likewise, ˹We guided˺ Zachariah, John, Jesus, and Elias, who were all of the righteous.” The list of the guided continues with four more prophets. Zachariah — the prophet who was given John in his old age, the guardian of Mary. John — his son, the prophet of purity and firmness, the one who confirmed the word of Allah. Jesus — the son of Mary, the messenger of the Gospel, the one who was supported with the holy spirit. And Elias — the prophet sent to his people in the ancient past, the one who called them away from the worship of Baal. The verse describes them all with one honour: “who were all of the righteous.”\n\nThe order of the list is meaningful: Zachariah and John are joined as father and son; Jesus comes after them because John was the one who announced him and prepared the way for him; and Elias is mentioned with them because of the similarity of their missions — the call to the oneness of Allah against the worship of other gods.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer that guidance is a chain that runs through families and across centuries: Zachariah and John, father and son, both prophets; Jesus and Elias, both callers to the oneness of Allah. The message never dies — it is carried from one righteous servant to the next. And the word “righteous” is the common description: the prophets were not only guided — they were righteous, people whose faith showed in their lives, whose worship was real, and whose deeds matched their words. The believer who aspires to the company of the prophets aspires to righteousness — the quality that gathers all of them in one verse.",
  "86": "“˹We also guided˺ Ishmael, Elisha, Jonah, and Lot, favouring each over other people ˹of their time˺.” The roll-call of the prophets continues with four more names. Ishmael — the son of Abraham, the father of the Arabs, the one who helped his father build the Ka'bah, the prophet of patience and truthfulness. Elisha — the prophet who followed after Elijah, mentioned in the Quran with honour. Jonah — the prophet of the whale, the one who was swallowed and called out from the darkness, the one whose people were saved by their repentance. And Lot — the nephew of Abraham, the prophet sent to the people of Sodom, the one who called them away from their wickedness.\n\nEach of them was guided by Allah, and the verse adds the honour: “favouring each over other people ˹of their time˺.” Every prophet was raised above the people of his own time — not by wealth or power, but by guidance, prophethood, and righteousness. The favour of Allah over the prophets is the favour of chosen servants: they were picked from their nations to carry the message.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the diversity of the prophetic line: Ishmael the builder, Elisha the successor, Jonah the one saved from the depths, Lot the caller in the city of wickedness — different missions, different trials, same message: the worship of Allah alone. The prophets were not a single family only; they were chosen from different peoples, different lands, different centuries — and the guidance of Allah reached them all. The believer sees in this list the mercy of Allah: no nation was left without a guide, and no prophet was left without the favour of Allah.",
  "87": "“And ˹We favoured˺ some of their forefathers, their descendants, and their brothers. We chose them and guided them to the Straight Path.” The verse widens the circle beyond the named prophets: Allah favoured some of their forefathers — the ancestors of the prophets, like Abraham and his fathers in faith; their descendants — the children and grandchildren who carried the message; and their brothers — the relatives and companions of the prophets. Not everyone was chosen; the verse says “some of” them — the choice was selective, based on Allah's knowledge of their hearts.\n\nThen comes the double action: “We chose them and guided them to the Straight Path.” The choosing is from Allah — He selects whom He wills; and the guidance is from Allah — He leads the chosen to the Straight Path. The two belong together: the chosen are guided, and the guided are chosen. The Straight Path is the path of the prophets — the worship of Allah alone, the obedience to His commands, the truth in belief and action.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer an important truth about Allah's selection: the favour of Allah is not random and not based on ancestry alone. The prophets were chosen for what Allah knew in their hearts — their truthfulness, their readiness, their love of the truth. And it teaches that guidance to the Straight Path is the greatest favour a person can receive: above wealth, above health, above power — to be chosen and guided to the path that leads to Allah. The believer asks for this favour in every prayer: “Guide us along the Straight Path” — the prayer of the one who knows that the Straight Path is the greatest gift.",
  "88": "“This is Allah’s guidance with which He guides whoever He wills of His servants.” The verse summarizes the whole list of prophets with a general principle: the guidance that reached Noah, Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, David, Solomon, Job, Joseph, Moses, Aaron, Zachariah, John, Jesus, Elias, Ishmael, Elisha, Jonah, and Lot — all of it was Allah’s guidance. It was not a human achievement; it was a divine gift, given to whoever Allah willed of His servants. The prophets did not invent their guidance; they received it. And the guidance remains in Allah's hand: He guides whoever He wills — not by favouritism, but by His knowledge of who will receive it.\n\nThen comes the warning that protects the whole message: “Had they associated others with Him ˹in worship˺, their ˹good˺ deeds would have been wasted.” The prophets themselves were not above this law: if any of them had committed shirk — associating partners with Allah — all their good deeds would have been wasted. The verse protects the prophets from any claim of partnership with Allah, and it establishes the law that shirk destroys deeds. Another chapter states the same: “if you associate others ˹with Allah˺, your deeds will certainly be void and you will truly be one of the losers.” The verse also carries the earlier teaching of this chapter: “It is ˹only˺ those who are faithful and do not tarnish their faith with falsehood who are guaranteed security.” The falsehood that tarnishes faith is shirk, and shirk wastes deeds.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the value of purity in faith: the deeds that are accepted are the deeds built on tawhid — the worship of Allah alone. A person may pray, give charity, and do good — but if his faith is tarnished with shirk, everything is wasted. The believer guards his faith as he guards his deeds — because deeds without pure faith are a building without a foundation.",
  "89": "“Those were the ones to whom We gave the Scripture, wisdom, and prophethood.” The verse gathers all the mentioned prophets under three gifts: the Scripture — the revealed Books given to them, like the scrolls of Abraham, the Torah of Moses, the Psalms of David, the Gospel of Jesus; wisdom — the understanding of the truth and the ability to judge rightly; and prophethood — the rank of being chosen to receive revelation and deliver it. The three gifts belong to the prophets together: the Book carries the message, the wisdom applies it, and the prophethood delivers it.\n\nThen comes the turn to the present: “But if these ˹pagans˺ disbelieve in this ˹message˺, then We have already entrusted it to a people who will never disbelieve in it.” The pagans of Mecca disbelieved in the Quran — the message that confirmed and completed the earlier Books. The verse says: their disbelief does not stop the message. The message has already been entrusted to another people — a people who will never disbelieve in it. The commentators explain this as the believers of the Muslim community — the Muhajirun and the Ansar who received the Quran with faith and carried it — and every generation of believers after them. The Quran was not sent to the pagans only; it was sent to whoever believes — and the believers of this community accepted it and will not abandon it.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the independence of the truth: the truth does not depend on any particular people. If one people rejects it, Allah has other people who will carry it. The same lesson was given in this chapter's earlier verse: “Whoever among you abandons their faith, Allah will replace them with others.” The believer's duty is to be among the people who receive the message with faith — the people who will never disbelieve in it.",
  "90": "“These ˹prophets˺ were ˹rightly˺ guided by Allah, so follow their guidance.” The verse turns the whole list of prophets into a command: they were guided by Allah — so follow their guidance. The guidance of the prophets is one guidance: the worship of Allah alone, the submission to His will, the call to the truth. And the Prophet Muhammad is commanded to follow it — the same guidance his predecessors followed. The command is addressed to him, and through him to his community: the way of the prophets is the way of the believers. The same message is repeated in the Quran's earlier command to the Prophet: “Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ 'I am not an innovation among the messengers...'” — he came with the same message, not a new religion.\n\nThen comes the second command: “Say, \"I ask no reward of you for this ˹Quran˺—it is a reminder to the whole world.\"” The Prophet is told to declare that he asks no payment for his message: no wealth, no status, no reward from the people. His reward is with Allah. The same declaration appears in another chapter: “even though you are not asking them for a reward for this ˹Quran˺. It is only a reminder to the whole world.” And in another: “I do not ask you ˹Meccans˺ for a reward for this ˹message˺.”\n\nThe purpose of the Quran is stated in two words: “a reminder to the whole world.” The Quran is not a private document for one tribe or one century — it is a reminder for the whole world, for every people and every generation. The verse teaches the believer the sincerity of the call: the message is delivered without price, because it is a reminder — and reminders are given freely. The one who conveys the truth asks nothing but the good of the people and the pleasure of Allah.",
  "91": "“And they have not shown Allah His proper reverence when they said, “Allah has revealed nothing to any human being.”” The verse records a shocking claim made by some of the People of the Book: they said that Allah has revealed nothing to any human being. The claim denies the revelation of the Torah itself, the Gospel, the Psalms — everything. The Quran calls this the failure to show Allah His proper reverence: to claim that the Lord of the worlds has never spoken to His creation is to strip Allah of the greatest sign of His care. The reports of the commentators mention that this statement was made by some Jews in debate with the Prophet — a claim so extreme that it denied their own scripture.\n\nThe answer comes in the form of a question: “Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Who then revealed the Book brought forth by Moses as a light and guidance for people, which you split into separate sheets—revealing some and hiding much? You have been taught ˹through this Quran˺ what neither you nor your forefathers knew.”” The Torah itself — the Book that Moses brought, described here as “a light and guidance for people” — was a revelation to a human being. If Allah has revealed nothing to any human, then who revealed the Torah? The question leaves no answer except the truth: Allah revealed it.\n\nThen the verse exposes their mistreatment of their own Book: “which you split into separate sheets—revealing some and hiding much.” They showed parts of the Torah and hid parts — revealing what suited them and concealing what did not. The same accusation appears in an earlier chapter: “Do you believe in some of the Scripture and reject the rest?” And the verse adds the proof of the Quran's truth: “You have been taught ˹through this Quran˺ what neither you nor your forefathers knew.” The Quran taught them things beyond the knowledge of their own scholars and ancestors — proof that it was a revelation.\n\nThen comes the final answer and the closing command: “Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Allah ˹revealed it˺!” Then leave them to amuse themselves with falsehood.” The truth is declared in two words: Allah revealed it. And then the command: leave them — those who argue against the clear truth — to amuse themselves with their falsehood. The verse teaches the believer to defend the honour of revelation: Allah has spoken to His creation through His prophets, and the Books of Allah are His words. And it teaches that after the truth is declared clearly, arguing with those who insist on falsehood has an end: leave them to their amusement — the amusement will end, and the truth will remain.",
  "92": "“This is a blessed Book which We have revealed—confirming what came before it—so you may warn the Mother of Cities and everyone around it.” The verse presents the Quran with three descriptions. First, it is “a blessed Book”: blessed in its source, its message, and its effect — every recitation of it brings good, and every guidance from it brings blessing. Second, it is a confirmation of what came before it: it agrees with the truth in the earlier scriptures and confirms their call to the worship of Allah alone. Third, it is a warning: the Prophet is sent with it to warn “the Mother of Cities” — Mecca, the city of the Sacred House — “and everyone around it,” meaning all the peoples of the earth, near and far. The same mission is described in another chapter: “And so We have revealed to you a Quran in Arabic, so you may warn the Mother of Cities and everyone around it.”\n\nThen the verse describes those who receive it: “Those who believe in the Hereafter ˹truly˺ believe in it and guard their prayers.” Belief in the Hereafter is the key: the one who truly believes that he will stand before Allah takes the Quran seriously, and his belief shows in action — “guard their prayers.” The word “guard” means praying on time, with care, and never neglecting the prayer. The verse joins belief in the Hereafter with guarding the prayers: the one who is certain of the return is the one who protects his covenant with Allah.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the connection between the Book and the prayer: the Quran is guidance from Allah, and the prayer is the standing response to that guidance. The one who believes in the Hereafter and guards his prayers has received the blessed Book in the way it deserves to be received.",
  "93": "“Who does more wrong than the one who fabricates lies against Allah or claims, “I have received revelations!”—although nothing was revealed to them—or the one who says, “I can reveal the like of Allah’s revelations!”?” The verse names the three worst kinds of falsehood against revelation. The first is fabricating lies against Allah — inventing false teachings and attributing them to Him. The second is claiming prophethood: a person who says “I have received revelations!” when nothing was revealed to him — the false prophet. The third is challenging the revelation: the one who says “I can reveal the like of Allah's revelations!” — the one who claims he could produce something like the Quran. All three are forms of the same crime: lying about the word of Allah. The question “Who does more wrong...?” answers itself: no one.\n\nThen the verse paints a terrifying scene: “If you ˹O Prophet˺ could only see the wrongdoers in the throes of death while the angels are stretching out their hands ˹saying˺, “Give up your souls! Today you will be rewarded with the torment of disgrace for telling lies about Allah and for being arrogant towards His revelations!”” At death, the angels come to the wrongdoers with severity — stretching out their hands to take the souls, and announcing the verdict: the torment of disgrace, because they lied about Allah and were arrogant toward His revelations. The scene is the opposite of the believers' death, which the Quran describes with peace and good news.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the gravity of lying about Allah — in belief, in prophecy, or in challenging His word. And it teaches that the falsehood of this world meets its reward at the edge of death, when the angels stretch out their hands and the truth can no longer be denied.",
  "94": "“˹Today˺ you have come back to Us all alone as We created you the first time —leaving behind everything We have provided you with.” The scene continues on the Day of Judgment. Each person returns to Allah “all alone” — exactly as he was created the first time: no wealth, no family, no title, no army. The same truth appears in another chapter: “How can you deny Allah? You were lifeless and He gave you life, then He will cause you to die and again bring you to life, and then to Him you will ˹all˺ be returned.” Everything this world provided — money, homes, children, positions — is left behind. The hands that held so much are empty.\n\nThen comes the second loss: “We do not see your intercessors with you—those you claimed were Allah’s partners ˹in worship˺.” The gods they worshipped — the idols, the partners they claimed would intercede for them — are nowhere to be seen. The intercessors they trusted did not come. The verse exposes the emptiness of their claim: they claimed partners, and the partners are absent.\n\nAnd the verse ends with the double verdict: “All your ties have been broken and all your claims have let you down.” The ties — of kinship, of alliance, of worship — are broken; and the claims — that the idols would help, that the partners would intercede — have failed them completely. The picture is total isolation: the person stands alone before Allah, with nothing behind him and nothing beside him.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to examine his own trust: the things he relies on besides Allah — wealth, status, connections, false hopes — will all be left behind or fail him. The only thing that returns with a person is his deeds. The wise one builds his return on what will not be left behind.",
  "95": "“Indeed, Allah is the One Who causes seeds and fruit stones to sprout.” The verse returns to the signs of Allah in creation, and it begins with the smallest and most common miracle: the seed. A dry seed or a fruit stone is placed in the earth — hard, lifeless, closed — and Allah causes it to sprout: it splits open, sends out a root and a shoot, and grows into a plant. Every farmer, every gardener, every person who eats fruit has seen this sign. The sprouting of the seed is a daily resurrection — life coming out of what looked dead.\n\nThen the verse states the wider law: “He brings forth the living from the dead and the dead from the living.” The plant comes from the seed, the bird from the egg, the child from the drop — the living from the dead; and the seed from the plant, the egg from the bird — the dead from the living. The two directions are both in Allah's hand. The same phrase appears in another chapter: “He brings forth the living from the dead and brings forth the dead from the living.”\n\nThen comes the conclusion: “That is Allah! How can you then be deluded ˹from the truth˺?” The One who does all this — who splits the seed, who turns death into life and life into death — that is Allah. And after all these signs, how can anyone turn away from Him to idols that cannot split a single seed? How can anyone doubt that the One who brings life from the dead can raise the dead again? The resurrection, which the deniers mocked, is the same power they see every day in the sprouting seed.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to see the signs: the field, the garden, the fruit — all of them are proofs of the Creator and promises of the resurrection. Another chapter says: “There is a sign for them in the dead earth: We give it life, producing grain from it for them to eat.”",
  "96": "“He causes the dawn to break, and has made the night for rest and ˹made˺ the sun and the moon ˹to travel˺ with precision.” The verse moves from the seed to the sky. “He causes the dawn to break” — the darkness of the night is split open by the light of morning, gently and precisely, every day without fail. The breaking of dawn is one of the most regular miracles: it has never been late, never been missed, never been confused with night. Then the night is described by its purpose: “has made the night for rest” — the darkness is not an accident; it is made for sleep and stillness, a mercy for the tired body. And the sun and the moon “travel with precision” — each follows its path, measured and exact, the sun by day and the moon by night, with their phases and their times. The same subjection is described in another chapter: “He has ˹also˺ subjected for you the sun and the moon, both constantly orbiting, and has subjected the day and night for you.”\n\nThen comes the conclusion: “That is the design of the Almighty, All-Knowing.” The dawn, the night, the sun, the moon — all of it is design, not chance. The Designer is the Almighty — nothing forces His order and nothing breaks it — and the All-Knowing — every movement of every star is placed by His knowledge.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to read the sky as a book: the dawn speaks of mercy, the night speaks of rest, the sun and moon speak of precision, and all of them speak of their Maker. The one who wakes at dawn, rests at night, and watches the sky is standing in the middle of the signs of Allah.",
  "97": "“And He is the One Who has made the stars as your guide through the darkness of land and sea.” The verse continues the list of signs, moving to the stars. In the time of the Quran, the stars were the compass of the traveller: across the deserts and the seas, without roads and without maps, people found their way by the stars. The verse says Allah “has made the stars as your guide” — the stars are not only decoration in the sky; they are guidance in the darkness. The night traveller looks up, finds the familiar stars, and knows his direction. The same stars guide the sailor on the open sea and the traveller in the empty desert.\n\nThen the verse adds: “We have already made the signs clear for people who know.” The word “know” is important: the signs are clear, but they are clear “for people who know” — those who look, reflect, and learn. The stars guide the traveller only if he knows them; the signs of Allah guide the believer only if he reflects on them. The same phrase is repeated in the coming verses with different endings: “for people who comprehend,” “for people who believe.”\n\nThe verse teaches the believer two lessons. First, gratitude for the guidance of Allah: the stars that guide the traveller are a mercy from the Lord, placed in the sky for the service of His servants. Second, reflection: the signs are clear for those who know — and knowledge here means the knowledge of the heart, the awareness that every star, like every sign, points to its Maker. The one who knows the stars only by their names has gained a little; the one who knows them as signs of Allah has gained everything.",
  "98": "“And He is the One Who originated you all from a single soul, then assigned you a place to live and another to ˹be laid to˺ rest.” The verse turns from the stars to the human being. “Originated you all from a single soul” — the whole human family traces back to one soul: Adam. Every person, every tribe, every nation — all from one origin. The phrase carries both the fact of creation and the lesson of unity: the human family is one family, created by one Lord. The same teaching appears in the chapter of the Women: the Quran says that Allah created you from a single soul and from it created its mate.\n\nThen comes the two places: “then assigned you a place to live and another to ˹be laid to˺ rest.” The commentators explain the two places in two ways: some say the first is the womb — where the child lives before birth — and the second is the earth — where the body rests after death; others say the first is the earth of this life and the second is the grave, or the first is this world and the second is the Hereafter. Both readings agree on the meaning: Allah assigned every person a place for his life and a place for his rest. The journey of every human being passes through places assigned by Allah.\n\nThen the verse closes: “We have already made the signs clear for people who comprehend.” The creation of all people from one soul, the places of life and rest — these are signs, clear for those who comprehend: those who understand that the One who began the human story from one soul is the One who will gather it back to Himself. The verse teaches the believer the unity of humanity and the certainty of the journey: one origin, assigned places, and a return.",
  "99": "“And He is the One Who sends down rain from the sky—causing all kinds of plants to grow—producing green stalks from which We bring forth clustered grain.” The verse describes the water cycle of mercy. Allah sends down rain from the sky; the rain falls on the earth; and from the rain, all kinds of plants grow. The verse follows one plant closely: the green stalk, from which the clustered grain comes out — the head of wheat or barley with its many grains. One seed becomes a stalk, one stalk becomes a head, one head becomes many grains — the multiplication of provision.\n\nThen the verse moves to the trees: “And from palm trees come clusters of dates hanging within reach. ˹There are˺ also gardens of grapevines, olives, and pomegranates, similar ˹in shape˺ but dissimilar ˹in taste˺.” The palm tree is singled out — the tree of the Arabs, with its clusters of dates hanging low “within reach,” so that the poor and the passer-by can take from them. Then the gardens: grapevines, olives, pomegranates — similar in shape but dissimilar in taste: the same water, the same earth, yet different fruits with different colours and different flavours. The difference in taste from the same soil is itself a sign.\n\nThen comes the command to look: “Look at their fruit as it yields and ripens!” The believer is commanded to look — not a quick glance, but a careful look: the fruit as it first appears, small and green, then as it yields and ripens, changing colour and sweetness. The looking is worship: to see the hand of Allah in the ripening fruit.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “Indeed, in these are signs for people who believe.” The rain, the stalk, the grain, the dates, the gardens, the ripening — all of them are signs, and they are signs “for people who believe”: those whose faith lets them see the Maker in the fruit. The believer eats his food and sees more than food — he sees the mercy, the power, and the knowledge of Allah.",
  "100": "“Yet they associate the jinn with Allah ˹in worship˺, even though He created them,” — the verse opens with the strangest contradiction of the polytheists, and it exposes the strangest contradiction of the polytheists. They associated the jinn with Allah in worship — they worshipped some of the jinn, or claimed that the jinn were partners of Allah or His daughters, as some of the pagan Arabs believed. And the verse adds the contradiction: “even though He created them.” The jinn are creatures of Allah — created by Him, under His command, like everything else. To worship a creature of Allah beside Allah is to worship the creation alongside the Creator — and the created thing has no share in lordship at all.\n\nThen the second falsehood: “and they falsely attribute to Him sons and daughters out of ignorance.” The pagans claimed that Allah had sons and daughters — the angels were called daughters of Allah, and some claimed sons. The verse describes the claim as false and its source as ignorance: they said it “out of ignorance,” not out of knowledge. No revelation brought it, no proof supported it, no reason accepted it.\n\nThen comes the declaration of Allah's majesty: “Glorified and Exalted is He above what they claim!” The two words “Glorified and Exalted” push Allah far above every false description: He is free from every partner, free from every child, free from every imperfection. The same declaration appears in another chapter, where the claim of offspring is described as a statement “by which the heavens are about to burst.”\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the purity of faith: Allah is One, and every claim of partner or child is falsehood born of ignorance. The believer guards his tongue and his belief: he describes Allah only as Allah described Himself — Glorified and Exalted above what the false claimants say.",
  "101": "“˹He is˺ the Originator of the heavens and earth.” The verse opens with the title of creation: Allah is the Originator — the One who created the heavens and the earth from nothing, without any previous model. He did not copy; He originated. The heavens and the earth, in all their vastness and order, came into being by His will alone.\n\nThen comes the logical question: “How could He have children when He has no mate?” The question destroys the claim of offspring from its root. A child comes from a mate — that is the way of created beings. Allah has no mate; therefore the very idea of His having a child is impossible. The question is stated as a challenge: how could He have children when He has no mate? The Quran's answer to the claim of sons and daughters is reason itself: the One who created everything does not reproduce like His creation.\n\nThen the verse adds: “He created all things and has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of everything.” The two statements complete the picture. He created all things — everything in existence is His making, so nothing can be His equal or His child in the sense the pagans claimed. And He has perfect knowledge of everything — He knows every creature, every atom, every secret, which is the knowledge of the Creator over His creation.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the majesty of Allah's oneness: He is the Originator, with no mate and no child; He created all things and knows all things. The faith of the believer is built on this foundation: a Lord who is above every need, above every partner, and above every likeness — the One who originated the heavens and the earth and holds perfect knowledge of everything within them.",
  "102": "“That is Allah—your Lord! There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him.” The verse concludes the argument with the great declaration: “That is Allah—your Lord!” The One who originates the heavens and the earth, who created all things and knows all things — that is Allah, and He is “your Lord”: the Lord of the listeners, the Lord of every reader, the One who created them, provides for them, and will judge them. Then comes the heart of the faith: “There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him.” The declaration of oneness is stated in its full form: no deity deserves worship except Allah. Every other object of worship — idol, star, jinn, human being — is false, because there is no god except Him.\n\nThen the verse continues: “˹He is˺ the Creator of all things, so worship Him ˹alone˺.” The argument is complete: since He is the Creator of all things, worship belongs to Him alone. The worship follows the creation: the One who made everything is the only One who deserves everything. The logic is simple and inescapable — the Creator is worshipped, and the creation is never worshipped beside Him.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “And He is the Maintainer of everything.” Allah does not only create; He maintains. He sustains everything: the heavens in their places, the earth in its orbit, the creatures in their lives, the believer in his faith. The One who created and maintains everything is the One who deserves the worship of everything.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the shape of true faith: Allah is your Lord, there is no god except Him, worship Him alone, and trust Him as the Maintainer of everything. In these four statements, the whole religion is gathered.",
  "103": "“No vision can encompass Him, but He encompasses all vision.” The verse states the relationship between Allah and sight. “No vision can encompass Him” — the eyes of creation cannot see Allah fully or surround Him; in this world, no eye can see Him, and in the Hereafter, the believers will see Him by His grace, but even then no vision can encompass Him. The sight of the servant does not contain the Lord; the Lord contains the sight. “But He encompasses all vision” — Allah sees every vision: every eye, every glance, everything that is seen and everything that sees. Nothing that the eyes perceive escapes His sight.\n\nThen the verse adds two names: “For He is the Most Subtle, All-Aware.” The Most Subtle — His knowledge reaches the finest things, the hidden details, the secrets within secrets; nothing is too small or too fine for Him. The All-Aware — He knows everything in its full reality, the inner and the outer, the apparent and the hidden.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer a profound lesson about Allah's greatness: the creation cannot contain its Creator — no vision can encompass Him. The believer does not try to picture Allah with the eyes of the mind or the imagination; he affirms what Allah affirmed and leaves what Allah left. And the same verse teaches the believer to live under the eye of Allah: since He encompasses all vision, every glance of the servant is seen by the Lord. The believer guards his eyes because his eyes are seen — by the Most Subtle, the All-Aware.",
  "104": "“Indeed, there have come to you insights from your Lord.” The verse calls the revelations and proofs of Allah “insights” — lights by which the heart sees, clear evidences that distinguish truth from falsehood. The Quran is an insight: it opens the eyes of the heart. The insights have come “from your Lord” — they are not human inventions; they are gifts from the Lord to His servants, sent so that no one can say he was left in darkness.\n\nThen comes the choice: “So whoever chooses to see, it is for their own good. But whoever chooses to be blind, it is to their own loss.” The two words “chooses” are decisive: seeing and blindness are choices. The insights are presented, and the person chooses: the one who chooses to see benefits himself — his sight is for his own good; the one who chooses to be blind harms himself — his blindness is to his own loss. The benefit of guidance returns to the guided, and the harm of blindness returns to the blind. Allah does not need the faith of His servants, and He is not harmed by their denial.\n\nAnd the verse ends with the same statement that closed an earlier verse of this chapter: “And I am not a keeper over you.” The Prophet is not a keeper — he does not force belief and does not carry the guilt of refusal. His duty is to present the insights; the choice is the listener's.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the freedom and the responsibility of faith: the insights have come, the eyes are open, and the choice is real. The one who sees walks in light; the one who blinds himself walks in loss. And it teaches the caller of truth to present the insights clearly and then leave the choice — he is not a keeper over anyone.",
  "105": "“And so We vary our signs to the extent that they will say, “You have studied ˹previous scriptures˺,” and We make this ˹Quran˺ clear for people who know.” The verse explains the method of the Quran: “We vary our signs.” The Quran presents its proofs in different forms — stories, arguments, comparisons, warnings, promises — varying the presentation so that every heart may find its door. The varying is deliberate: the same truth is shown from many angles.\n\nBut the variation produced an accusation from the deniers: “You have studied ˹previous scriptures˺.” They claimed the Prophet had learned from earlier scriptures — from the Jews and the Christians — and that the Quran was his own composition from what he studied. The verse records their accusation and leaves it to answer itself: the Quran that exposes their own secrets and corrects their own books cannot be a borrowed composition. Another chapter answers the same accusation: the Prophet was unlettered, and his knowledge of the past stories could not have come from study.\n\nThen comes the purpose: “and We make this ˹Quran˺ clear for people who know.” The clarity of the Quran is for “people who know” — those who approach it with open minds and honest hearts. The same Quran is clear to the seeker and confusing to the accuser: the difference is not in the Book but in the heart.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to receive the varying signs with humility: the Quran repeats, rephrases, and re-presents its truths because hearts need repetition. And it teaches the believer not to be shaken by the accusations of the deniers — the accusation of the pagans that the Prophet studied old books was answered by the Book itself, which remains clear for people who know.",
  "106": "“˹O Prophet!˺ Follow what is revealed to you from your Lord—there is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him—and turn away from the polytheists.” The verse commands the Prophet to do three things. First: “Follow what is revealed to you from your Lord.” The Prophet's path is revelation — he follows what Allah revealed, not the customs of the people, not the pressure of the tribe, not his own desire. The command is the foundation of his mission: the follower of revelation does not negotiate the message. Second, the verse places the creed in the middle of the command: “there is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him.” The revelation the Prophet follows is the revelation of the oneness of Allah — the message of every prophet, the core of the Quran.\n\nThird: “and turn away from the polytheists.” The command to turn away is the command of separation: the Prophet does not follow their ways, does not accept their compromises, does not worship what they worship. The same command appeared in an earlier chapter of the Quran, where the Prophet is told to turn away from the polytheists after declaring his innocence of their worship.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the three foundations of his own life: follow revelation, affirm the oneness of Allah, and turn away from polytheism. The believer lives by the revealed word, guards the declaration of oneness in his heart and on his tongue, and keeps his distance from the worship of anything besides Allah — in belief, in practice, and in loyalty.",
  "107": "“Had Allah willed, they would not have been polytheists.” The verse answers a question that troubled the believers: why do the polytheists persist in their polytheism? Could Allah not have made them believers? The verse states the truth plainly: “Had Allah willed, they would not have been polytheists.” Allah has full power over their hearts — if He had willed to force them into faith, they would have believed. But He did not will forced faith, because forced belief is not belief at all: the test of this world requires a real choice. The same truth was stated earlier in this chapter: “Had Allah so willed, He could have guided them all.”\n\nThen the verse defines the Prophet's role: “We have not appointed you as their keeper, nor are you their maintainer.” The Prophet is not their keeper — he is not responsible for forcing their beliefs; and he is not their maintainer — he is not responsible for their provision or their guidance in the sense of control. His duty is delivery; their choice is theirs; Allah's will covers everything.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer two lessons. First, the mystery of guidance: hearts are in Allah's hand, and His wisdom in leaving some to their choice is beyond our full understanding — the believer accepts it and does not despair over the persistence of the deniers. Second, the limit of responsibility: the caller of truth is not a keeper and not a maintainer. He conveys, he warns, he prays — and he leaves the hearts and the outcomes to the One whose will covers everything.",
  "108": "“˹O believers!˺ Do not insult what they invoke besides Allah or they will insult Allah spitefully out of ignorance.” The verse teaches the believers the manners of argument — a lesson that applies in every age. The polytheists invoked their idols; the believers were angry at the idols and the worship of them; and some believers began to insult the idols. The verse forbids it: “Do not insult what they invoke besides Allah.” And the reason is given: “or they will insult Allah spitefully out of ignorance.” If the believers insult the idols, the polytheists will answer by insulting Allah — out of anger and ignorance. The prohibition protects the honour of Allah Himself: the believer's zeal for Allah must not become the cause of insult against Allah.\n\nThe same lesson applies to every sacred thing of any people: insulting what others hold sacred usually produces the opposite of what the believer wants — it hardens them and leads them to insult what the believer holds sacred. The Quran commands the believers to argue with wisdom and good advice, not with insults.\n\nThen the verse explains the deeper reality: “This is how We have made each people's deeds appealing to them.” Each people sees its own deeds as good — the polytheists see their polytheism as good, and their deeds are made appealing to them as a test and a consequence. The believer should not be shocked: every people is deceived by its own deeds until Allah's truth reaches it and its heart opens.\n\nAnd the verse ends with the just conclusion: “Then to their Lord is their return, and He will inform them of what they used to do.” The settling is not by insults and counter-insults; it is by the return to Allah, who will inform everyone of what they used to do. The verse teaches the believer to guard his tongue even in defending the truth — because the honour of Allah is protected by wisdom, not by insults.",
  "109": "“They swear by Allah their most solemn oaths that if a sign were to come to them, they would certainly believe in it.” The verse records the strongest promise of the polytheists: they swore by Allah their most solemn oaths — the most binding promises they could make — that if a sign came to them, they would certainly believe. The demand for signs and the promise of belief were repeated throughout the Prophet's mission: bring a miracle, and we will believe. The same demand is recorded in many chapters of the Quran, where the pagans asked for springs, gardens, and angels.\n\nThe answer comes in two parts. First: “Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Signs are only with Allah.”” The sending of signs is not in the Prophet's hand; it is with Allah alone, and Allah sends signs according to His wisdom — not according to the demands of the mockers. Second, the verse turns to the believers with a penetrating question: “What will make you ˹believers˺ realize that even if a sign were to come to them, they still would not believe?” The question exposes the truth: the believers hoped for a sign because they thought it would bring belief; but the signs had already come — the Quran, the miracles, the clear proofs — and the polytheists did not believe. A new sign would not change hearts that had already decided to deny. The Quran had already established this: “Even if they were to see every sign, they still would not believe in them.”\n\nThe verse teaches the believer not to wait for miracles to convince people: the proofs have come, and belief is a matter of the heart, not of signs. The One who sends signs knows when signs serve guidance — and when they would only add to the proof against the deniers.",
  "110": "“We turn their hearts and eyes away ˹from the truth˺ as they refused to believe at first, leaving them to wander blindly in their defiance.” The verse describes the consequence of the deniers' choice. Because they refused to believe at first — because they turned away when the truth was presented to them — Allah turned their hearts and eyes away from the truth. The turning is a just consequence: the hearts that refused the truth when it was clear are no longer given the ability to see it. The same principle appears in another chapter: the Quran says that when a people turned away, Allah made their hearts hard.\n\nThen comes the picture of their end: “leaving them to wander blindly in their defiance.” The deniers are left to wander — moving without direction, stumbling in the dark — while remaining in their defiance. They are not destroyed at once; they are left to walk the path of their own choice, blind to the truth that once stood before them.\n\nThe verse raises the question of divine justice: is it fair for Allah to turn their hearts away? The verse answers by pointing to the cause: “as they refused to believe at first.” The turning is a response to their refusal, not a random act. They chose first; the consequence followed. The same pattern is described in the earlier verses of this chapter about the veils over the hearts: the veils came after their persistent turning away.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the preciousness of the present moment: the heart that accepts the truth today keeps its sight; the heart that refuses today may find the door closed tomorrow. The believer does not gamble with his heart — he turns to the truth while the turning is still in his own hands.",
  "111": "“Even if We had sent them the angels, made the dead speak to them, and assembled before their own eyes every sign ˹they demanded˺, they still would not have believed— unless Allah so willed.” The verse takes the demand for signs to its absolute limit. The polytheists asked for signs; the verse imagines the most extreme signs possible: the angels themselves sent down to them; the dead raised to speak to them; every sign they ever demanded assembled before their own eyes. Would they believe then? The verse answers: “they still would not have believed.” Their disbelief was not caused by a shortage of signs; it was a disease of the heart that no sign could cure. The earlier verses of this chapter said the same: even if they touched the revelation with their hands, they would call it magic.\n\nThen comes the exception: “— unless Allah so willed.” The only thing that could change them is Allah's will — and His will is not forced belief, but the guidance He gives to hearts that turn to Him. The exception points to the truth: guidance is in Allah's hand, not in the power of signs.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “But most of them are ignorant ˹of this˺.” Most of the deniers do not know — they do not know that signs would not have helped them, that their hearts are the barrier, that the sending of signs follows Allah's wisdom. They keep demanding signs, ignorant of the fact that the sign they demand would change nothing.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the deepest lesson about guidance: the proof was never the problem, and the sign was never the solution. The Quran itself is the greatest sign, and it is enough for those who want the truth and not enough for those who do not. The believer trusts Allah's wisdom in what is given and what is withheld — and keeps his own heart open to the signs that have already come.",
  "112": "“And so We have made for every prophet enemies—devilish humans and jinn—whispering to one another with elegant words of deception.” The verse reveals a fixed law of the prophetic mission: every prophet had enemies. The enemies are described as “devilish humans and jinn” — the devils of the jinn and the wicked of mankind, working together against the messengers. The two kinds of devils “whisper to one another with elegant words of deception”: they decorate falsehood with beautiful language, making evil look attractive and truth look weak. The same method runs through history: the enemies of the prophets did not come with ugly arguments; they came with polished words designed to deceive.\n\nThen the verse adds the divine wisdom: “Had it been your Lord’s Will, they would not have done such a thing.” The existence of these enemies is within Allah’s will — He permitted the enmity as part of the test of this world: the truth is presented, the devils decorate falsehood, and hearts choose. The same statement was made in the previous verses about the polytheists: had Allah willed, they would not have been polytheists.\n\nAnd the verse ends with the command: “So leave them and their deceit.” The Prophet is told to leave the enemies and their deception — not to fight their whispers with whispers, not to enter their game. The believer’s answer to decorated falsehood is not more decoration; it is the clear truth, delivered and then left.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to expect enmity against the truth — it is the law of every prophetic mission — and to recognise the method of the enemy: elegant words of deception. The believer who knows the method is not deceived by the decoration; he sees the falsehood behind the beautiful words.",
  "113": "“so that the hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter may be receptive to it, be pleased with it, and be persistent in their evil pursuits.” The verse continues the previous one and explains the purpose of the devilish whispering. The elegant words of deception are aimed at a specific target: “the hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter.” Those who deny the return to Allah are the easiest prey of the devils — because without the Hereafter, there is no accountability, and without accountability, every evil becomes acceptable. The verse describes the three effects of the deception on their hearts: they become “receptive to it” — open to the falsehood; “be pleased with it” — they enjoy the evil and find satisfaction in it; and “be persistent in their evil pursuits” — they keep going deeper, sin after sin, without stopping.\n\nThe verse teaches a profound truth about the connection between belief and behaviour: the denial of the Hereafter opens the heart to every deception. When a person does not believe he will stand before Allah, the whisperers find an open door: the sin is decorated, the pursuit is pleasurable, and the persistence is easy. The believer, by contrast, is protected by his certainty of the return: the whisperer’s decoration cannot change the fact that every deed will be weighed.\n\nAnd the verse teaches the believer to examine his own heart: which whispers find a door? The heart that remembers the Hereafter is not receptive to deception; the heart that forgets it becomes the playground of the devils. The protection is the certainty of the Day of Return — the belief that closes the door of the whispers and keeps the heart from being pleased with evil.",
  "114": "“˹Say, O Prophet,˺ “Should I seek a judge other than Allah while He is the One Who has revealed for you the Book ˹with the truth˺ perfectly explained?”” The verse answers those who wanted the Prophet to follow their judgments and customs. The question is: should I seek a judge other than Allah? The answer is hidden in the question — of course not. And the reason is given: Allah is the One who revealed the Book, “perfectly explained.” The Quran is complete in its explanations: the lawful and the unlawful, the commands and the prohibitions, the truth about Allah and the Hereafter. When the Book is perfectly explained, there is no need for another judge — the judge has already spoken.\n\nThe verse continues: “Those who were given the Scripture know that it has been revealed ˹to you˺ from your Lord in truth.” The People of the Book — the honest ones among them — knew with certainty that the Quran was revealed from Allah in truth: their scriptures had described this Prophet and this Book, and their scholars recognised him as they recognised their own children. The testimony of the earlier scripture-holders is evidence for the truth of the revelation.\n\nAnd the verse ends with the command to the Prophet: “So do not be one of those who doubt.” The Prophet is told not to doubt — the certainty of the revelation is confirmed by the Book itself, by the testimony of the People of the Book, and by the knowledge of Allah. The command is a protection: the believer stands on certainty, not on the shifting opinions of people.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer where to take his judgments: to the Book of Allah, perfectly explained, before any other source. The One who revealed the Book is the only judge whose judgment is complete — and the believer who seeks his rulings from the perfectly explained Book is never left without guidance.",
  "115": "“The Word of your Lord has been perfected in truth and justice.” The verse declares the perfection of Allah’s word. “The Word of your Lord” — His revelation, His promises, His rulings — “has been perfected in truth and justice”: perfect in truth, because everything it reports is true; perfect in justice, because everything it commands and decides is fair. Nothing is missing from the Word of Allah and nothing is excessive: it is complete and balanced, exactly as it should be. The same meaning is carried in an earlier verse of this chapter, where the Quran is described as “perfectly explained.”\n\nThen comes the statement of protection: “None can change His Words.” No one can change the words of Allah — not by addition, not by deletion, not by distortion. The earlier scriptures were changed by their people, but the Word of Allah itself is protected: what Allah has decreed stands, what He has promised comes, and what He has revealed remains. The attempts of the deniers to alter or dismiss the revelation change nothing.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “And He is the All-Hearing, All- Knowing.” The One whose Word is perfected is the All-Hearing — He hears every word of His servants, including the attempts to change His words; and the All-Knowing — He knows every intention behind every statement. The two names guarantee that the Word of Allah is spoken, heard, and known in complete awareness.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the foundation of certainty: the Word of Allah is perfect in truth and justice, protected from every change, and issued by the All-Hearing, All-Knowing. The believer rests on this Word — it is the firmest thing in existence, because it is the Word of the Lord, perfected and protected.",
  "116": "“˹O Prophet!˺ If you were to obey most of those on earth, they would lead you away from Allah’s Way.” The verse is a warning of the highest importance: the majority is not a proof of truth. The Prophet is told — and through him every believer — that if he were to obey most of the people on earth, they would lead him away from Allah’s Way. The verse does not say “some” or “many”; it says “most of those on earth.” The majority of humanity, left to its own desires, is not on the path of Allah — and the believer must never measure truth by the crowd.\n\nThen the verse explains why: “They follow nothing but assumptions and do nothing but lie.” The two descriptions expose the foundation of the majority’s way: assumptions — opinions without knowledge, guesses without proof; and lies — falsehood in belief and speech. The one who follows the crowd follows assumptions; the one who follows revelation follows certainty. The same accusation is repeated in this chapter against the polytheists who defended their forefathers’ religion.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the courage of being with the truth even when it is rare. The prophets were always in the minority — Noah with a few believers, Abraham alone against his people, the Prophet in the early years with a handful of companions. The Quran’s warning is clear: the size of the crowd is not the measure of the truth. The believer takes his way from Allah’s revelation, not from the assumptions of the many — and he is not shaken when the majority walks in the other direction.",
  "117": "“Indeed, your Lord knows best who has strayed from His Way and who is ˹rightly˺ guided.” The verse follows the warning about the majority with a statement of Allah’s knowledge. “Your Lord knows best” — Allah’s knowledge is the most complete: He knows who has strayed from His Way and who is rightly guided. The straying and the guidance are both fully known to Him — not only the outward state, but the heart behind it: the one who strays out of ignorance and the one who strays out of stubbornness are not the same in His knowledge; the one who seeks guidance and the one who rejects it are not the same.\n\nThe verse is both a warning and a comfort. It is a warning to those who follow the assumptions of the majority: your straying is not hidden; the Lord knows best who has strayed, and His knowledge will be the judgment. And it is a comfort to the believers who find themselves in the minority: your guidance is also known — the Lord knows best who is rightly guided, and the minority of the guided is not lost in His knowledge.\n\nThe verse also teaches the believer to leave the final judgment of people to Allah: we see the outside, and He knows the inside; we judge by appearances, and He judges by realities. The believer does not claim to know who is finally guided and who is finally lost — that knowledge belongs to the Lord, who knows best. The believer’s duty is to follow the guidance himself, and to leave the knowledge of hearts and endings to the One who knows best.",
  "118": "“So eat only of what is slaughtered in Allah’s Name if you truly believe in His revelations.” The verse turns from arguments about guidance to the law of food. The command is direct: “eat only of what is slaughtered in Allah’s Name.” When an animal is slaughtered, the Name of Allah is pronounced over it — the act of taking its life is performed in the name of its Creator and Giver. The believer eats from what was slaughtered in Allah’s Name, and he does not eat from what was slaughtered in the name of idols or without the Name of Allah.\n\nThe condition is attached: “if you truly believe in His revelations.” The law of food is tied to faith: the one who truly believes in Allah’s revelations accepts His commands about what is lawful and what is forbidden. The belief is not only in the heart; it shows in the table. The same connection appears in the earlier verses of this chapter, where the believers are commanded to eat the good lawful things.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer that even his food is an act of faith. The believer does not eat like an animal — he eats as a servant: he mentions the Name of Allah over his food, he eats what Allah made lawful, and he avoids what Allah forbade. The pronouncement of Allah’s Name over the slaughter is a remembrance of the Giver of life: the animal is taken in the name of the One who created it, and the food is received as a provision from Him. The believer’s table is a place of worship — a daily reminder that every bite is from Allah and every law is from Allah.",
  "119": "“Why should you not eat of what is slaughtered in Allah’s Name when He has already explained to you what He has forbidden to you—except when compelled by necessity?” The verse answers the hesitation some believers felt. Some were uncertain about the food of the People of the Book and about what was slaughtered in Allah’s Name. The verse asks: why should you not eat of what is slaughtered in Allah’s Name? The answer is clear: there is no reason to hold back, because Allah has already explained what He forbade — the forbidden things are known and limited — and everything else is lawful. The explanation is a mercy: the believer does not live in confusion about his food; the lawful and the forbidden are clearly mapped.\n\nThe exception is stated: “except when compelled by necessity.” In genuine necessity — when a person is forced by hunger or danger and no lawful food is available — the forbidden may be eaten to save life. The principle of necessity runs through the laws of Islam: the preservation of life overrides the prohibition when there is no other way, and the person is not blamed for what necessity compelled.\n\nThen the verse warns against the other danger: “Many ˹deviants˺ certainly mislead others by their whims out of ignorance.” The deviants mislead people about food — some by forbidding what Allah made lawful, some by making lawful what Allah forbade — all by their whims, out of ignorance. The believer takes his food laws from Allah’s explanation, not from the whims of the deviants.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “Surely your Lord knows the transgressors best.” The deviants who mislead people are known to Allah — their transgression is not hidden, and their account is with Him. The verse teaches the believer to hold firmly to Allah’s explained laws of food, to trust the exception of necessity, and to beware of the misleaders who replace Allah’s explanation with their own whims.",
  "120": "“Shun all sin—open and secret.” The verse commands the believer to avoid sin completely — both kinds: the open sin done publicly, and the secret sin done privately. The word “shun” is stronger than “avoid a little”: it means leave sin entirely, stay away from it, do not approach it. The open sin is shameful before people, and the secret sin is shameful before Allah — and the believer shuns both, because Allah sees the secret as clearly as the open.\n\nThe same command appears in this chapter in the verse about indecencies: “Do not come near indecencies, openly or secretly.” The pairing of open and secret covers every sin: the sin done in public and the sin done in private are both sins, and the believer does not excuse one because of the other. Some people guard their public behaviour while their private life is neglected — the verse closes that door: shun all sin, open and secret.\n\nThen comes the reason: “Indeed, those who commit sin will be punished for what they earn.” The punishment is tied to what the person himself earned: the sin is his earning, and the consequence belongs to him. No one else’s sin is on his shoulders, and no sin of his is lost. The earning of sin is like the earning of wealth: what the person worked for, he receives.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the completeness of self-control: the guard must cover both the public and the private, because the One who sees both is the One who rewards and punishes. The believer who shuns open sin but indulges secret sin has only moved his sin into the presence of Allah — and the presence of Allah is everywhere. The true shunning is complete: open and secret, public and private.",
  "121": "“Do not eat of what is not slaughtered in Allah’s Name. For that would certainly be ˹an act of˺ disobedience.” The verse completes the law of slaughter. The previous verse commanded eating what is slaughtered in Allah’s Name; this verse forbids the opposite: eating what is not slaughtered in Allah’s Name — the animal that died without slaughter, or was slaughtered in the name of idols, or was dedicated to false gods. The reason is stated: eating it “would certainly be ˹an act of˺ disobedience.” The food itself carries the mark of obedience or disobedience: the Name of Allah over the slaughter makes the eating an act of obedience, and its absence makes the eating disobedience.\n\nThen the verse exposes the whispering of the devils: “Surely the devils whisper to their ˹human˺ associates to argue with you.” The devils send their human allies to argue about this law — to debate, to mock, to find loopholes. The arguments of the pagans against the food laws are the whispers of the devils working through human voices.\n\nAnd the verse ends with the sharp warning: “If you were to obey them, then you ˹too˺ would be polytheists.” Obeying them in making lawful what Allah forbade would draw the believer toward polytheism — because the acceptance of the pagans’ religious rules is the acceptance of their religious authority, and their authority is built on associating partners with Allah. The warning is severe: the food law is not a small detail; it is a line between the worship of Allah alone and the following of the pagans’ religion.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the weight of the food laws: they are not mere customs but acts of faith. The Name of Allah over the food is a declaration of loyalty, and the refusal to eat what was not slaughtered in His Name is a refusal of every false authority. The believer eats as a servant of Allah alone — and he does not let the whispers of the devils, through any human voice, pull him across the line.",
  "122": "“Can those who had been dead, to whom We gave life and a light with which they can walk among people, be compared to those in complete darkness from which they can never emerge?” The verse paints a comparison in pictures. The first picture: a person who “had been dead” — dead in his heart, lost in misguidance — and Allah “gave life” — brought him out of the death of disbelief into the life of faith — and gave him “a light with which they can walk among people” — the light of guidance, the light of the Quran, by which he walks safely among the darkness of the world. The second picture: a person “in complete darkness from which they can never emerge” — the darkness of disbelief that surrounds him completely, with no way out.\n\nThe question answers itself: can these two be compared? Of course not. The living one walks in light; the lost one is trapped in darkness. The same picture appears in another chapter: “Is one who was dead, then We raised him to life and made for him a light by which to walk among people, like one who is in darkness from which he cannot emerge?” The Quran repeats the picture because it captures the difference between faith and disbelief perfectly.\n\nThen the verse adds the explanation of the disbelievers’ state: “That is how the misdeeds of the disbelievers have been made appealing to them.” Their misdeeds look good to them — their disbelief, their stubbornness, their following of desires — all decorated until they see their darkness as light. The decoration is the work of the whisperers described in the previous verses.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to value his faith: it is life and light, and nothing in the world compares to it. The believer who understands this does not envy the apparent success of those in darkness — the comparison has already been decided: the living with the light, and the trapped in the darkness, are not equal.",
  "123": "“And so We have placed in every society the most wicked to conspire in it.” The verse reveals another fixed law: in every society, Allah has placed the most wicked as its conspirators — the criminals, the schemers, the leaders of evil who plot and deceive. The placing is part of the test: every society faces its wicked ones, who conspire against the truth and its people. The prophets faced the wicked of their societies — the chiefs who plotted against them; and the Prophet faced the leaders of Quraysh who conspired against him.\n\nThen comes the astonishing truth: “Yet they plot only against themselves, but they fail to perceive it.” The conspirators think they are plotting against the believers and the truth. But the verse says: they plot only against themselves. Their schemes do not harm the truth; they harm their own souls — every plot adds to their account, and every scheme brings them closer to their own ruin. And the tragedy is in the last words: “they fail to perceive it.” They cannot see that their own conspiracy is the rope around their own necks.\n\nThe same truth is stated in another chapter: “The evil plot only ensnares none but its own people.” The evil plot returns to its maker.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer not to fear the conspiracies of the wicked: the conspirators are digging their own pit. The believer’s duty is to hold firmly to the truth and leave the conspirators to their plots — which will only destroy them, even while they fail to perceive it. And it teaches the believer to check his own heart: let him never be among the conspirators, whose scheming blinds them to their own destruction.",
  "124": "“Whenever a sign comes to them, they say, “We will never believe until we receive what Allah’s messengers received.”” The verse records the demand of the arrogant: whenever a sign came to the disbelievers, they said they would not believe until they themselves received what the messengers received — direct revelation, angels, or the rank of prophethood. Their demand was not a search for guidance; it was arrogance: they wanted to be the ones chosen, not to follow the one chosen. The same attitude is described in other chapters, where the arrogant of the past said they would not believe in a messenger who was merely human like them.\n\nThe answer comes: “Allah knows best where to place His message.” The message — prophethood and revelation — is placed by Allah according to His knowledge: He knows who deserves it, who can carry it, and who will deliver it. The demand of the arrogant does not move the placement: Allah places His message where He knows best, not where the arrogant demand.\n\nThen comes the promise of their end: “The wicked will soon be overwhelmed by humiliation from Allah and a severe punishment for their evil plots.” Two things await them: humiliation — the disgrace that strips their arrogance — and a severe punishment — the recompense for their evil plots. The same end is described in the Quran’s accounts of the past nations: the arrogant were humiliated and punished.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to accept Allah’s placement with humility: Allah places His message where He knows best, and the believer follows the messenger Allah sent, without demanding a share of the rank. And it teaches the believer to see the end of arrogance: the demand of the wicked — to receive what the messengers received — ends in humiliation and severe punishment, while the humble follower of the messenger receives honour and safety.",
  "125": "“Whoever Allah wills to guide, He opens their heart to Islam.” The verse describes the two states of the heart. First, guidance: “Whoever Allah wills to guide, He opens their heart to Islam.” The heart is described as a closed container, and guidance as the opening: the heart opens to Islam — to submission, to faith, to the truth — and the light enters. The opening is from Allah: He opens the heart of the one He wills to guide — and His will is tied to the person’s own seeking, as the Quran makes clear elsewhere: those who strive in the way of Allah are guided in its paths.\n\nThen the second state: “But whoever He wills to leave astray, He makes their chest tight and constricted as if they were climbing up into the sky.” The chest becomes tight and constricted — narrow, closed, unable to receive the truth — and the verse pictures the feeling: “as if they were climbing up into the sky,” where the air thins and the chest tightens with every breath. The picture captures the suffocation of the heart that refuses guidance: every step toward the truth becomes harder, and the truth itself feels like a climb it cannot complete.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “This is how Allah dooms those who disbelieve.” The tightness and constriction are the doom of the disbelievers — the consequence of their own refusal, sealed by Allah’s justice.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the preciousness of an open heart: the opened heart is the greatest gift — it receives Islam, finds peace, and walks in light. And it teaches the believer to pray for his own heart and for the hearts of others: the opening is from Allah, and the tightness is from Him as well — so the believer asks the Opener of hearts to open his heart to Islam, and he guards his heart from the refusal that leads to constriction and doom.",
  "126": "“That is your Lord’s Path—perfectly straight.” The verse points to the path: “That is your Lord’s Path” — the path of Islam, the path of the prophets, the path described in the previous verses — and it is “perfectly straight”: no curve, no deviation, no crookedness. The straightness of the path is its perfection: it leads directly to Allah, without detours and without dead ends. The same description appears in the opening chapter of the Quran, where the believers pray to be guided “along the Straight Path.”\n\nThen the verse adds: “We have already made the signs clear to those who are mindful.” The signs of the path are clear — the verses of the Quran, the proofs of creation, the stories of the prophets — and they are clear “to those who are mindful”: those who fear Allah, who take the signs seriously, who live with awareness. The mindfulness is the key that opens the clarity: the same signs are before everyone, but the mindful ones see them and follow the straight path.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer two things. First, the confidence in the path: the path of Allah is perfectly straight — the believer does not doubt the path because it is narrow in the eyes of the world; its straightness is its perfection. Second, the condition of clarity: the signs are clear for the mindful — so the believer cultivates mindfulness of Allah, and the path becomes clear before him step by step. The straight path is not hidden; it is made clear for those who are mindful.",
  "127": "“They will have the Home of Peace with their Lord, Who will be their Guardian because of what they used to do.” The verse describes the reward of those who follow the straight path. “They will have the Home of Peace” — the Paradise, called the Home of Peace because it is free of every disturbance: no fear, no grief, no conflict, no end. The home of peace is “with their Lord” — the nearness to Allah is the heart of the reward. And Allah “will be their Guardian”: the Lord Himself protects and cares for the people of Paradise — the Guardian who never abandons.\n\nThe reason is stated at the end: “because of what they used to do.” The reward is tied to their deeds: the faith they held, the prayers they guarded, the sins they shunned, the patience they showed. The same connection runs through the Quran: the reward of the believers is the fruit of their deeds, given by the grace of Allah. Another chapter says the believers will have “the Gardens of ˹Eternal˺ Residence—as an accommodation for what they used to do.”\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the destination of the straight path: the Home of Peace with the Lord, under the guardianship of Allah, earned by what they used to do. The picture is complete: a path, a destination, and a journey of deeds. The believer who walks the straight path is walking toward the Home of Peace — and every good deed he does is a step on the road to the Guardian who never leaves.",
  "128": "“˹Consider˺ the Day He will gather them ˹all˺ together and say, “O assembly of jinn! You misled humans in great numbers.”” The verse paints a scene from the Day of Judgment: Allah gathers everyone — humans and jinn — and addresses the jinn directly: “O assembly of jinn! You misled humans in great numbers.” The jinn — the devils among them — are charged with their work: leading human beings astray in great numbers. The charge is public, before all creation.\n\nThen the human associates answer: “And their human associates will say, “Our Lord! We benefited from each other’s company, but now we have reached the term which You appointed for us.”” The humans who followed the devils confess: we benefited from each other’s company — the devils used the humans and the humans enjoyed their whispered desires — but now the term has arrived, the appointment of the Hereafter has come, and the benefit is over. The confession shows the exchange: the humans received the pleasure of sin, and the devils received followers — and both are left with the Fire.\n\nThen the verdict: “˹Then˺ He will say, “The Fire is your home, yours to stay in forever, except whoever Allah wills to spare.”” The Fire is their home — the humans and the jinn who misled and were misled — forever, with the exception of whoever Allah wills to spare. The exception points to Allah’s mercy and His absolute will: no one is forced into the Fire beyond what justice requires, and the final judgment is His.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “Surely your Lord is All-Wise, All-Knowing.” The gathering, the charge, the confession, and the verdict — all of it is from the All-Wise and the All-Knowing.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the danger of evil company: the association with the whisperers ends in a shared Fire. And it teaches the believer to choose his company now — the company of the guided, not the associates of the devils — because on that Day, every association will be judged by its fruit.",
  "129": "“This is how We make the wrongdoers ˹destructive˺ allies of one another because of their misdeeds.” The verse explains the alliance of evil. “This is how” — by the law described in the previous verses: the misled humans and the misleading jinn — “We make the wrongdoers ˹destructive˺ allies of one another.” The wrongdoers are made allies: the wicked support the wicked, the oppressors support the oppressors, and the misleaders support the misled. The alliance is a punishment in itself: when the wrongdoers unite, their wrongdoing multiplies — each one strengthens the other’s evil.\n\nThe reason is stated: “because of their misdeeds.” The alliance is not random; it is the fruit of their deeds. The wrongdoers are joined to one another because their misdeeds are alike: the sinner is drawn to the sinner, the liar to the liar, the oppressor to the oppressor. The same law appears in another form in the Quran: evil companions are drawn to evil people, and good companions to good people.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer a profound truth about society: the bonds between people follow their deeds. The wrongdoers are made allies of one another — their evil unites them and their union multiplies their evil. And the believer learns the opposite law for himself: his deeds determine his company. The believer who does good is drawn to the good, and the company of the righteous is itself a reward of righteousness. The believer chooses his allies by their deeds — because the alliance of this world will be judged by the deeds on which it was built.",
  "130": "“˹Allah will ask,˺ “O assembly of jinn and humans! Did messengers not come from among you, proclaiming My revelations and warning you of the coming of this Day of yours?”” The verse moves to the second scene of the judgment: Allah addresses the assembly of jinn and humans together and asks the decisive question — did messengers not come to you from among you, proclaiming the revelations and warning of this Day? The question establishes the proof: the messengers came, the revelations were proclaimed, the warning was given. No one can claim ignorance, because the warning reached them from among themselves — humans to humans, and messengers to both.\n\nTheir answer is recorded: “They will say, “We confess against ourselves!”” The confession is total: we confess against ourselves — we heard the messengers, we received the warnings, and we still denied. The confession on that Day is not a repentance that helps; it is the acknowledgment of the proof that condemns.\n\nThen the verse explains why they are in this state: “For they have been deluded by ˹their˺ worldly life.” The world deluded them: its pleasures, its desires, its false promises made them forget the warnings. And the verse ends with their testimony: “And they will testify against themselves that they were disbelievers.” The final testimony is against themselves: their own tongues will confirm what their hearts held.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the value of the present moment: the messengers came, the warning was given, and the confession is coming. The one who responds to the messengers now will not need to confess against himself then. And it teaches the believer the danger of the world’s delusion: the world is the veil that makes people forget the warnings — and the one who sees through the veil now will not be deluded on the Day when the truth is undeniable.",
  "131": "“This ˹sending of the messengers˺ is because your Lord would never destroy a society for their wrongdoing while its people are unaware ˹of the truth˺.” The verse explains the wisdom behind sending the messengers. The destruction of a society is tied to its awareness: Allah “would never destroy a society for their wrongdoing while its people are unaware ˹of the truth˺.” The messengers are sent so that no society is destroyed without a warning: the truth must reach the people, the messengers must proclaim the revelations, and only then — if the people persist in wrongdoing with full knowledge — does the destruction come.\n\nThe same principle is repeated in the Quran: “We would never destroy a society unless its people were warned.” And another chapter says: “Your Lord would never destroy the societies unjustly while their people were righteous.” The mercy is in the warning: Allah does not punish out of nowhere; He sends the messengers first.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer two lessons. First, the justice of Allah: the punishment of the past nations was not unjust — they were warned, they heard the messengers, and they persisted. Second, the purpose of the messengers: they are a mercy — they close the door of ignorance and open the door of repentance. The believer who receives the message today stands exactly where the past nations stood: warned, aware, and responsible. The warning is the mercy; the response is the choice.\n\nThe same mercy continues: the messengers were sent from among the people themselves, speaking their languages, sharing their lives — so that no one could say the message was foreign or impossible. The warning of every prophet was a proof of Allah’s justice and a door of His mercy at the same time.",
  "132": "“They will each be assigned ranks according to their deeds.” The verse states the justice of the final reckoning: “They will each be assigned ranks according to their deeds.” Every person will be placed in the rank his deeds earned — the believers in ranks of Paradise according to their faith and good deeds, and the wrongdoers in ranks of punishment according to their sins. The rank is not random and not inherited: it is earned by deeds. The same principle is stated in another chapter: “Everyone will be ranked according to what they have done.”\n\nThe verse adds: “And your Lord is not unaware of what they do.” The assignment of ranks is based on full knowledge: nothing is missed, nothing is forgotten, nothing is misjudged. The Lord is not unaware of what they do — the smallest deed, the hidden intention, the secret charity and the secret sin — all are known.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the seriousness of deeds: every deed builds a rank — either upward or downward. The believer does not live carelessly, because his rank is being built by his own actions every day. And the verse teaches the believer comfort: the Lord is not unaware of what they do — the good deed done in secret is not lost, the wrong deed done in secret is not hidden, and the final ranks will be assigned with perfect knowledge and perfect justice. The believer works for his rank now, while the deeds are still being written.",
  "133": "“Your Lord is the Self-Sufficient, Full of Mercy.” The verse opens with two great names: the Self-Sufficient — Allah has no need of His creation, of their worship, of their obedience; His riches are complete; and the Full of Mercy — His mercy is vast, reaching His creation even though He does not need them. The two names together are a wonder: the One who needs nothing still showers mercy on everything.\n\nThen comes the warning of power: “If He wills, He can do away with you and replace you with whoever He wills, just as He produced you from the offspring of other people.” The warning is addressed to the people who reject the message: your existence is not necessary to Allah. If He wills, He can take you away and bring another people — just as He produced you from the offspring of peoples before you. The same warning appears in another chapter: “If you ˹still˺ turn away, He will replace you with another people.”\n\nThe verse teaches the believer two lessons. First, humility: the servant is nothing without his Lord — his existence, his survival, his continuity are all from Allah, who is Self-Sufficient and needs nothing. Second, gratitude: the Full of Mercy keeps the servant in existence, provides for him, and gives him time to return — even though He needs nothing from him. The believer responds to the Self-Sufficient’s mercy with gratitude and obedience — knowing that his place in the world is a gift that can be replaced, and his return to mercy is the only safe place.",
  "134": "“Indeed, what you have been promised will certainly come to pass. And you will have no escape.” The verse is a statement of absolute certainty about the promises of Allah. “What you have been promised” — the resurrection, the judgment, the Paradise, the Fire, the punishment of the wrongdoers — “will certainly come to pass.” The promise of Allah is not a hope or a threat; it is a certainty. The verse uses the strongest words: “certainly” and “will come to pass” — the promised matters are as good as done in the decree of Allah.\n\nThen the second half: “And you will have no escape.” The warning is to those who mock the promise: when the promised Day comes, there will be no escape — no place to hide, no power to resist, no intercessor without Allah’s permission. The mockers who think they can escape the promise of Allah will find every door closed.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the foundation of his life: certainty in the promises of Allah. The believer lives on the promise of Allah — the promise of reward for the good, the promise of help for the patient, the promise of the Home of Peace — and he lives with certainty because the promise will certainly come to pass. And the same certainty is a warning: the promises of punishment are equally certain, and there is no escape from what Allah has promised. The believer builds on the promises of Allah — the surest foundation in existence.",
  "135": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “O my people! Persist in your ways, for I ˹too˺ will persist in mine.”” The verse commands the Prophet to address his people with a challenge: persist in your ways — continue in your polytheism and denial — and I will persist in mine — continue in my faith and my call. The challenge is a statement of certainty: the two ways cannot both be right, and persistence will reveal which one stands. The same challenge appears in another chapter: “Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ’Work in your way; I am working in mine.’”\n\nThen comes the promise of clarity: “You will soon know who will fare best in the end.” The future will reveal the truth: the one who fares best in the end — in this world and the Hereafter — will be known. The Prophet does not need to prove himself by arguments alone; time itself will testify.\n\nAnd the verse ends with the verdict: “Indeed, the wrongdoers will never succeed.” The same verdict appeared earlier in this chapter: “Indeed, the wrongdoers will never succeed.” The wrongdoers may seem to succeed for a time — their plots, their power, their persistence — but success in the final sense is never theirs. The verse teaches the believer the confidence of the truth: he can challenge the wrongdoers to persist because their end is fixed — they will never succeed. And it teaches the believer patience: the question of who fares best is answered in the end, and the end belongs to Allah.",
  "136": "“The pagans set aside for Allah a share of the crops and cattle He created, saying, “This ˹portion˺ is for Allah,” so they claim, “and this ˹one˺ for our associate-gods.”” The verse exposes the absurdity of the pagans’ religious inventions. They divided the crops and cattle Allah created into two shares: one for Allah and one for their associate-gods. The division itself was their invention — Allah never commanded it. And the verse exposes the injustice of their system: “Yet the portion of their associate-gods is not shared with Allah while Allah’s portion is shared with their associate-gods.” The idol’s share was protected — nothing of it went to Allah; but Allah’s share could be given to the idols — when the idol’s share was lost or the idol seemed to need more, they took from Allah’s portion. Their reverence was for the idols, not for Allah.\n\nThen comes the verdict: “What unfair judgment!” The verse condemns their judgment with amazement: they claim to honour Allah, yet they honour the idols above Him. The same pattern of invented shares appears in the coming verses, where they invent rules about cattle and crops and attribute them to Allah.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to examine every religious claim: is this from Allah, or is it a human invention dressed in religion? The pagans claimed their shares were religious — and the Quran exposed them as unfair inventions. The believer takes his religion from revelation: no invented shares, no invented rules, no division that honours anything above Allah. And the verse teaches the believer the purity of giving to Allah: the believer gives his best to Allah, not the leftovers of the idols.",
  "137": "“Likewise, the pagans’ evil associates have made it appealing to them to kill their own children—only leading to their destruction as well as confusion in their faith.” The verse exposes another horror of the pagans’ religion: the evil associates — the devils and the wicked who deceived them — made the killing of their own children appealing to them. The pagan Arabs killed their children: some buried their daughters alive out of fear of poverty and shame, and some killed their children as offerings to their idols. The verse says the act was made appealing to them — decorated by the evil associates until it looked like devotion. And the result: “only leading to their destruction as well as confusion in their faith” — the killing destroyed the children, destroyed the killers’ humanity, and confused their religion until they thought murder was worship.\n\nThen comes the divine wisdom: “Had it been Allah’s Will, they would not have done such a thing.” The same statement repeated in this chapter: their deeds are within Allah’s will and test — He did not force them to stop, because the test requires the freedom of choice.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “So leave them and their falsehood.” The Prophet is told to leave them and their falsehood — the call has been delivered, the truth has been made clear, and the falsehood is theirs to carry.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the depth of the pre-Islamic darkness: the religion of the pagans reached the point where killing children was made to look good. And it teaches the believer the value of the revelation that came to end it: the Quran forbade the killing of children absolutely, as the coming verses of this chapter will state. The religion of Allah protects life; the inventions of the devils destroy it — and the believer holds firmly to the protection.",
  "138": "“They say, “These cattle and crops are reserved—none may eat them except those we permit,” so they claim.” The verse continues exposing the invented rules of the pagans about their cattle and crops. They declared some cattle and crops “reserved” — sacred — and none may eat them except those they permitted: the men, the priests, the idols’ servants — while the women and others were excluded. The verse adds the phrase “so they claim” — their permission and prohibition were nothing but claims, without any authority from Allah.\n\nThen the next invention: “Some other cattle are exempted from labour and others are not slaughtered in Allah’s Name—falsely attributing lies to Him.” They exempted some cattle from work — the sacred animals that could not be ridden or loaded; and they slaughtered others without the Name of Allah — dedicating them to the idols. All of it was “falsely attributing lies to Him”: they claimed these rules were from Allah, and the claim was a lie.\n\nAnd the verse ends with the promise of recompense: “He will repay them for their lies.” The lies are not forgotten: Allah will repay them for their false attribution — in this world by exposing their falsehood, and in the Hereafter by the full account.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the difference between revelation and invention: the pagans had a whole system of invented sanctities — reserved cattle, exempted animals, idol-slaughter — all presented as religion. The Quran came to sweep the inventions away and to restore the rule of revelation: the lawful is what Allah made lawful, and the forbidden is what Allah made forbidden. The believer measures every religious claim by the revelation — and he does not follow the invented sanctities of any people, no matter how old or how dressed in the language of devotion.",
  "139": "“They ˹also˺ say, “The offspring of this cattle is reserved for our males and forbidden to our females; but if it is stillborn, they may all share it.”” The verse records another of the pagans’ invented rules, and it exposes the injustice at the heart of it. They said: the offspring of the reserved cattle is for our males only and forbidden to our females — the sons could eat it, the daughters could not. But if the animal was stillborn — if it was born dead — then they could all share it. The rule is built on pure discrimination and convenience: the living offspring is a privilege of the males, and the stillborn — which no one wanted as a privilege — is shared by all.\n\nThen comes the verdict: “He will repay them for their falsehood. Surely He is All-Wise, All-Knowing.” Allah will repay them for their falsehood — the invented rules, the discrimination, the attribution of their whims to Allah. And the repayment is from the All-Wise — His judgment is perfectly just — and the All-Knowing — He knows exactly what they invented and why.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer two lessons. First, the injustice of invented religion: the rules the pagans attributed to Allah were built on their own desires — favouring the males, excluding the females, and bending the rules when it suited them. Invented religion always serves the desires of the inventors. Second, the importance of taking religion from revelation alone: the believer’s lawful and forbidden come from Allah’s Book, not from human whims dressed as divine rules. And the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the account: every falsehood attributed to Allah will be repaid — by the All-Wise, the All-Knowing.",
  "140": "“Lost indeed are those who have murdered their own children foolishly out of ignorance and have forbidden what Allah has provided for them— falsely attributing lies to Allah.” The verse passes the final verdict on the pagans’ practices. “Lost indeed are those who have murdered their own children” — the verdict of loss falls on the killers of their own children, the parents who buried their daughters alive and offered their sons to idols. The murder was “foolishly out of ignorance”: foolish in its reasoning and ignorant in its religion. And they “have forbidden what Allah has provided for them” — they forbade the good food Allah provided, the cattle and crops of the previous verses — “falsely attributing lies to Allah”: they claimed their murderous and prohibitive rules were Allah’s religion.\n\nThen the double verdict: “They have certainly strayed and are not ˹rightly˺ guided.” The straying is certain: they left the path of Allah in the worst way — killing children and forbidding provisions while claiming religion. And they are not rightly guided: no guidance reached their hearts, because their hearts had turned the worst crimes into worship.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the sanctity of life and provision: both come from Allah, and both are protected by His law. The murder of children is the deepest violation of the sanctity of life, and the forbidding of lawful provision is the violation of His bounty. And the verse teaches the believer to examine every practice presented as religion: the test of a practice is not its age or its claim but its agreement with the revelation — because the practices of the pagans, with all their claimed sanctity, left them lost, strayed, and unguided.",
  "141": "“He is the One Who produces gardens—both cultivated and wild—and palm trees, crops of different flavours, olives, and pomegranates—similar ˹in shape˺, but dissimilar ˹in taste˺.” The verse returns to the signs of provision. Allah produces the gardens — the cultivated ones, planted and tended by people — and the wild ones, which grow without human care; the palm trees; the crops of different flavours; the olives and the pomegranates — similar in shape but dissimilar in taste. The variety is from Allah: the same earth, the same water, and fruits of different flavours — the sign of the Creator’s freedom and wisdom.\n\nThen comes the command: “Eat from the fruit they bear and pay the dues at harvest, but do not waste.” The believer eats from the fruit — the provision is lawful and good — and he pays the dues at harvest: the charity and the rights of the poor at the time of reaping, which the scholars explain as the zakat and the shares Allah commanded on the produce. And the warning: “but do not waste.” The waste is forbidden — wasting food, wasting wealth, wasting the blessings of Allah. The believer eats, gives, and does not waste.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “Surely He does not like the wasteful.” The wasteful are disliked by Allah — the ones who throw away the provision, who exceed the limits in eating and spending, who treat the blessings of Allah as if they had no value. The verse teaches the believer the balance of provision: eat from the fruit, pay the dues, and do not waste. The provision of Allah is received with gratitude, shared with the needy, and guarded from waste — because the One who produces the gardens loves the grateful and does not love the wasteful.",
  "142": "“Some cattle are fit for labour, others are too small.” The verse describes the variety of cattle: some are fit for labour — the strong ones that carry loads, pull ploughs, and serve people in their work; others are too small — the young or the weak, kept for their milk, their wool, or their meat. The variety is from Allah's wisdom: every kind of cattle has its purpose, and all of it is a provision for people.\n\nThen comes the command: “Eat of what Allah has provided for you and do not follow Satan's footsteps. Certainly, he is your sworn enemy.” The believer is commanded to eat of the lawful provision — the cattle, the milk, the meat — and to guard against the second thing: following the footsteps of Satan. The same command appears in an earlier chapter: “O humanity! Eat from what is lawful and good on the earth and do not follow Satan's footsteps. He is truly your sworn enemy.” The footsteps of Satan here are the invented rules of the pagans: the reserved cattle, the exempted animals, the forbidden shares — Satan's footsteps are the paths that lead people to forbid what Allah made lawful and to make lawful what Allah forbade.\n\nAnd the verse states the reason for the warning: “Certainly, he is your sworn enemy.” Satan is the sworn enemy of the human being — his enmity is declared and open, from the time of Adam until the end of time. The believer does not follow the footsteps of his enemy: he eats the lawful provision with gratitude, and he refuses the invented rules that the enemy decorates as religion.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the two guards of his table: gratitude for what Allah provided, and vigilance against the footsteps of Satan — the whispered rules that turn provision into prohibition and prohibition into provision.",
  "143": "“˹Allah has created˺ four pairs: a pair of sheep and a pair of goats—ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, “Has He forbidden ˹to you˺ the two males or the two females or what is in the wombs of the two females? Tell me with knowledge, if what you say is true.”—” The verse begins the argument about the cattle. Allah created “four pairs”: a pair of sheep — the male and the female — and a pair of goats — the male and the female. The pagans had invented rules about which of these were forbidden: some said the males were forbidden, some said the females, some said what is in the wombs. The verse challenges them with a question: “Has He forbidden ˹to you˺ the two males or the two females or what is in the wombs of the two females?”\n\nThe question exposes the emptiness of their claims. If Allah had forbidden some of these cattle, which one did He forbid? The male of the sheep? The female? What is in the womb? They could not answer, because Allah had revealed nothing of the kind — the rules were their own inventions. And the verse demands the proof: “Tell me with knowledge, if what you say is true.” Their claim must be supported by knowledge — by revelation from Allah. They had none.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the standard of religious claims: every claim about the lawful and the forbidden must be supported by knowledge — by what Allah actually revealed. The pagans could not produce knowledge for their invented rules, and their rules fell. The believer takes his lawful and forbidden from the revealed knowledge, and he rejects every claim that comes without it — no matter how old the claim or how confident the claimant.",
  "144": "“and a pair of camels and a pair of oxen. Ask ˹them˺, “Has He forbidden ˹to you˺ the two males or the two females or what is in the wombs of the two females? Or were you present when Allah gave you this commandment?”” The verse completes the four pairs: after the sheep and the goats come the camels and the oxen — the larger cattle of the Arabs. And the question is repeated: has He forbidden the two males or the two females or what is in the wombs? And a new question is added: “Or were you present when Allah gave you this commandment?” The question is devastating: were you present when Allah commanded this? They were not — no one was present, because Allah never gave such a commandment. Their claims of forbidden cattle had no witness and no revelation.\n\nThen the verdict: “Who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah to mislead others without ˹any˺ knowledge?” The same question asked earlier in this chapter: no one does more wrong than the one who fabricates lies against Allah. And here the fabrication has a purpose: “to mislead others” — the inventors of false religious rules not only go astray themselves; they lead others astray, all “without ˹any˺ knowledge.”\n\nAnd the verse ends: “Surely Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people.” The fabricators are the wrongdoers, and the guidance of Allah does not reach those who persist in wrongdoing.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the gravity of inventing religion: to claim that Allah forbade what He did not forbid is to fabricate lies against Him, to mislead others, and to close the door of guidance on oneself. The believer guards his tongue and his religion: he says about Allah only what Allah said about Himself, and he never invents a commandment and attributes it to the Lord.",
  "145": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “I do not find in what has been revealed to me anything forbidden to eat except carrion, running blood, swine—which is impure—or a sinful offering in the name of any other than Allah.”” The verse is the final word on the forbidden foods. The Prophet is commanded to state what the revelation actually forbids: four things. First, “carrion” — the animal that died without being slaughtered. Second, “running blood” — the blood that flows, which is forbidden to eat. Third, “swine—which is impure” — the pig, described as impure in itself. Fourth, “a sinful offering in the name of any other than Allah” — the animal slaughtered for other than Allah, dedicated to idols or false gods. These are the only forbidden foods named in the revelation — and everything else is lawful.\n\nThe verse answers the pagans' inventions: they had forbidden many cattle and crops with their own rules; the revelation forbids only these four things. The lawful is wider than the inventors claimed, and the forbidden is exactly what Allah forbade — no more and no less.\n\nThen comes the exception of necessity: “But if someone is compelled by necessity—neither driven by desire nor exceeding immediate need—then surely your Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” In genuine necessity — when a person's life depends on it — the forbidden may be eaten to survive, with two conditions: the person is not driven by desire — he does not eat for pleasure; and he does not exceed immediate need — he takes only what saves his life. For such a person, the Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful: the necessity is excused, and the mercy of Allah covers the compelled.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the mercy of the food laws: the forbidden is few, the lawful is many, and the door of necessity is open. The believer eats the lawful with gratitude, avoids the forbidden with obedience, and trusts the mercy of Allah in the hour of compulsion.",
  "146": "“For those who are Jewish, We forbade every animal with undivided hoofs and the fat of oxen and sheep except what is joined to their backs or intestines or mixed with bone.” The verse explains that the earlier Jewish law was stricter than the law of Islam. For the Jews, Allah forbade every animal with undivided hoofs — the camel, for example — and the fat of the oxen and sheep, except the fat joined to the backs, the intestines, or mixed with the bone. The Jewish law was a heavy law, with many restrictions on food.\n\nThen comes the reason: “In this way We rewarded them for their violations. And We are certainly truthful.” The extra restrictions were a reward — the word is used in the sense of recompense — for their violations: because of their wrongdoing, their transgressions, and their disobedience, Allah made their law heavier. The restriction was a consequence of their sins, not an original mercy. The same meaning is carried in another chapter, where the Quran says that the heavy restrictions came because of the wrongs of the Jews.\n\nAnd the verse ends with the seal of truth: “And We are certainly truthful.” The account of the Jewish law and its reason is true — the Quran reports it truthfully, and Allah's statement is truth itself.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer two lessons. First, gratitude for the ease of the Islamic law: the food of the Muslim is wider and lighter than the food of the Jews — and the ease is a mercy of Allah to this community. Second, the weight of sin: the restrictions of the earlier law were a recompense for violations — sin brings heaviness into religion, and obedience brings ease. The believer who sees the connection understands that the lightness of his law is a gift to be protected by obedience — because the law of Allah responds to the deeds of the people.",
  "147": "“But if they deny you ˹O Prophet˺, say, “Your Lord is infinite in mercy, yet His punishment will not be averted from the wicked people.”” The verse prepares the Prophet for the denial of his people. After the clear statement of the food laws, some would still deny the message. The Prophet is told: if they deny you, say two things. First: “Your Lord is infinite in mercy.” The mercy of Allah is vast — it fills everything, reaches everyone, and never runs out. The first statement opens the door: mercy is the dominant quality of the Lord.\n\nBut then comes the second: “yet His punishment will not be averted from the wicked people.” The mercy of Allah does not cancel His justice: the punishment of the wicked is certain, and nothing can avert it — not the mercy of Allah, because the mercy is received by the repentant; not the intercession of the idols, because the idols have no power; not the delay, because the delay has an end. The two statements together are the complete picture: mercy for those who turn, punishment for those who persist.\n\nThe same pair appears throughout the Quran: the believers are taught to live between hope and fear — hope in the infinite mercy, and fear of the punishment that will not be averted from the wicked. The verse teaches the believer the balance: do not despair of the mercy, and do not feel safe from the punishment. The one who hears the Prophet's call and denies it places himself in the path of the punishment that mercy itself cannot avert for the wicked — and the one who responds enters the mercy that is infinite.",
  "148": "“The polytheists will argue, “Had it been Allah’s Will, neither we nor our forefathers would have associated others with Him ˹in worship˺ or made anything unlawful.”” The verse records the excuse of the polytheists: they argued that if Allah had willed, they would not have been polytheists and would not have made things unlawful. The excuse uses the will of Allah as a shield: they claimed their polytheism was Allah's decree, so they were not to blame. The same excuse is recorded in other chapters of the Quran, where the polytheists say that if Allah had willed, they would not have worshipped idols.\n\nThen the verse answers with history: “Likewise, those before them rejected the truth until they tasted Our punishment.” The nations before them had the same excuse — and it did not save them. They rejected the truth and persisted until the punishment came. The excuse of divine will did not protect the past nations from destruction, and it will not protect the polytheists.\n\nThen the challenge: “Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, “Do you have any knowledge that you can produce for us? Surely you follow nothing but ˹false˺ assumptions and you do nothing but lie.”” Their excuse is built on assumptions and lies — not on knowledge. If their claim were true, they could produce knowledge — revelation or proof. They have none: they follow assumptions and they lie.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the answer to the excuse of divine will: the will of Allah does not cancel human responsibility, because Allah willed that people choose — and He sent the messengers to make the choice clear. The excuse of the polytheists was the same excuse of every generation: “if Allah willed, we would not have sinned” — and the answer is always the same: the messengers came, the choice was real, and the punishment followed the rejection. The believer accepts the mystery of the divine will and carries the responsibility of his own choice.",
  "149": "“Say, “Allah has the most conclusive argument. Had it been His Will, He would have easily imposed guidance upon all of you.”” The verse answers the polytheists' excuse with the decisive truth. “Allah has the most conclusive argument” — the final proof, the decisive argument, belongs to Allah alone. Whatever the polytheists claim, the conclusive argument is Allah's: His messengers, His revelations, His signs, His justice. No one can argue against Allah's argument.\n\nThen comes the second half: “Had it been His Will, He would have easily imposed guidance upon all of you.” The statement confirms what the polytheists said — that Allah's will is supreme — and turns it into an answer: yes, had Allah willed, He could have imposed guidance on all of you by force. But He did not will forced belief — because the test of this world requires real choice. The imposition of guidance would have cancelled the test, the messengers, the patience, and the reward.\n\nThe same truth is stated earlier in this chapter: “Had Allah so willed, He could have guided them all.” And in another chapter: “Had We willed, We could have easily imposed guidance on every soul. But My Word will come to pass: I will surely fill up Hell with jinn and humans all together.”\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to rest in the conclusive argument of Allah: the believer does not need to answer every excuse perfectly — the conclusive argument is Allah's, and it will silence every excuse on the Day of Judgment. And the believer understands the wisdom of the will: Allah did not impose guidance, because the guidance that is chosen is the guidance that is real. The believer chooses, and his choice is his honour.",
  "150": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Bring your witnesses who can testify that Allah has forbidden this.”” The verse demands the proof for the pagans' claims. They claimed that Allah had forbidden certain cattle and crops. The Prophet is told to say: bring your witnesses — those who can testify that Allah forbade this. The demand is the test: the claim of a divine commandment requires witnesses — prophets, revelation, or proof. The pagans had none.\n\nThen comes the warning to the Prophet: “If they ˹falsely˺ testify, do not testify with them.” If the pagans bring false witnesses who claim Allah forbade what He did not forbid, the Prophet must not testify with them — he must not accept their testimony or join their claim. The command protects the Prophet from being drawn into their falsehood.\n\nAnd the verse ends with the command to avoid the misleaders: “And do not follow the desires of those who deny Our proofs, disbelieve in the Hereafter, and set up equals with their Lord.” Three descriptions of the people to avoid: those who deny the proofs — the signs and revelations; those who disbelieve in the Hereafter — the return and the judgment; and those who set up equals with their Lord — the polytheists. Their desires must not be followed, because their desires are built on denial, disbelief, and the setting up of equals.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the standard of testimony in religion: claims about what Allah commanded or forbade must be supported by witnesses — by revelation and proof. And it teaches the believer the company he keeps: the desires of the deniers, the Hereafter-deniers, and the polytheists are not to be followed — the believer takes his religion from the witnesses of revelation, not from the desires of the misleaders.",
  "151": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Come! Let me recite to you what your Lord has forbidden to you: do not associate others with Him ˹in worship˺.”” The verse begins the great list of the forbidden things — the commandments that the scholars call the ten commandments of the Quran. The Prophet is told to recite what the Lord has forbidden: the list begins with the greatest sin — “do not associate others with Him ˹in worship˺.” The association of partners with Allah is the first and greatest prohibition: the worship of anything beside the Creator. The same command opens the list in other chapters, where the Quran commands the worship of none but Allah.\n\nThen the second command: “˹Do not fail to˺ honour your parents.” After the command to Allah comes the command to the parents — the two commands are joined throughout the Quran, because the right of Allah is followed by the right of the parents. The honour of the parents is a command, not a suggestion — the believer honours his parents, serves them, and speaks to them kindly. Another chapter says: “For your Lord has decreed that you worship none but Him. And honour your parents.”\n\nThe third: “Do not kill your children for fear of poverty. We provide for you and for them.” The command forbids the killing of children — the pagan practice of burying daughters and sacrificing sons. The reason is given: “We provide for you and for them” — the provision is from Allah, and He provides for the parents and the children together. The fear of poverty is answered by the promise of provision. Another chapter says: “Do not kill your children for fear of poverty. We provide for them and for you. Surely killing them is a heinous sin.”\n\nThe fourth: “Do not come near indecencies, openly or secretly.” The command is not only to avoid indecencies but to not come near them — to stay away from their edges and their paths. The indecencies are forbidden whether open or secret — public or private.\n\nThe fifth: “Do not take a ˹human˺ life—made sacred by Allah—except with ˹legal˺ right.” The life of a human being is sacred — made sacred by Allah — and it may not be taken except with legal right: the punishment for murder, the punishment for mischief, the defence of life. The command protects life absolutely.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “This is what He has commanded you, so perhaps you will understand.” The list is a command with a purpose: understanding — the believer who understands the wisdom of the prohibitions obeys them from the heart.",
  "152": "“And do not come near the wealth of the orphan—unless intending to enhance it—until they attain maturity.” The verse continues the list of the great commands. The orphan — the child who has lost his father — has wealth that is entrusted to his guardian. The command is strict: “do not come near the wealth of the orphan,” except in one case: “unless intending to enhance it.” The guardian may manage the orphan's wealth only to improve it — to grow it, protect it, and invest it — until the orphan “attains maturity” and can manage it himself. The same protection appears in another chapter: “Indeed, those who unjustly consume orphans' wealth ˹in fact˺ consume nothing but fire into their bellies.” The wealth of the orphan is sacred: the one who touches it unjustly is eating fire.\n\nThen comes the command of fairness: “Give full measure and weigh with justice.” The believer must be exact in trade: the full measure and the full weight, with justice — not cheating the buyer, not shortchanging the seller, not using false scales. The command protects the market and the trust between people.\n\nThen the command of fairness in speech: “Whenever you speak, maintain justice—even regarding a close relative.” The believer's tongue must be just — in testimony, in advice, in judgment — even when the truth is against a close relative. The justice of the tongue is not bent by love: the believer speaks the truth about his brother, his father, his friend, exactly as he would speak about a stranger. The same command appears in the earlier verses of this chapter, where the believers are told to stand firm for justice even against the hated.\n\nThen the command of the covenant: “And fulfil your covenant with Allah.” The covenant with Allah — the promise of faith and obedience — must be fulfilled: the promises made to Allah, the obligations taken, the word given.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “This is what He has commanded you, so perhaps you will be mindful.” The commands are given so that mindfulness — the awareness of Allah in every dealing — may grow. The believer who guards the orphan's wealth, the scales, the tongue, and the covenant is the believer who is mindful of Allah.",
  "153": "“Indeed, that is My Path—perfectly straight. So follow it and do not follow other ways, for they will lead you away from His Way.” The verse gathers the commands of the previous verses into one picture: “that is My Path—perfectly straight.” The path of Allah — the worship of Allah alone, the honouring of parents, the protection of children, the purity from indecency, the sanctity of life, the justice in measure and speech, the fulfilment of covenants — that is the straight path. The commands are not separate rules; they are the milestones of one road.\n\nThen the command: “So follow it and do not follow other ways, for they will lead you away from His Way.” The believer is commanded to follow the straight path and to refuse the other ways — the ways of desire, of invention, of the pagans' rules, of every path that branches off the road of Allah. The warning is clear: the other ways “will lead you away from His Way” — every other path is a detour, and every detour ends away from Allah.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “This is what He has commanded you, so perhaps you will be conscious ˹of Allah˺.” The command is repeated with a new ending — “so perhaps you will be conscious ˹of Allah˺” — the previous verses ended with “understand” and “be mindful,” and this verse ends with “be conscious”: the path is commanded so that the believer may live in consciousness of Allah.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the unity of the religion: the commands are one path, and the believer follows the path as a whole — not picking some commands and leaving others. And it teaches the believer to guard the road: the other ways are many and attractive, but the believer refuses them all, because every other way leads away from His Way.",
  "154": "“Additionally, We gave Moses the Scripture, completing the favour upon those who do good, detailing everything, and as a guide and a mercy, so perhaps they would be certain of the meeting with their Lord.” The verse turns from the commands to the earlier scriptures. “Additionally, We gave Moses the Scripture” — the Torah was given to Moses as another gift of revelation. The verse describes the Torah with four qualities: it completed the favour upon those who do good — it perfected the blessing of guidance for the righteous; it detailed everything — the laws, the rulings, the stories were explained in detail; it was a guide — pointing people to the truth; and it was a mercy — bringing compassion to those who received it.\n\nThe purpose is stated at the end: “so perhaps they would be certain of the meeting with their Lord.” The Torah was given so that its people would be certain of the meeting with Allah — the resurrection and the judgment. The certainty of the meeting was the goal of the revelation: the one who is certain of meeting his Lord lives accordingly.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the unity of the revelation: the Torah and the Quran come from the same Lord, with the same goal — the certainty of the meeting with Allah. The Torah detailed everything for its time, and the Quran details everything for its time. The believer honours the earlier scriptures as revelations from Allah, while following the final Book that confirms and completes them. And the believer holds the purpose of all revelation: the certainty of the meeting with his Lord.",
  "155": "“This is a blessed Book We have revealed. So follow it and be mindful ˹of Allah˺, so you may be shown mercy.” The verse turns back to the Quran. “This is a blessed Book We have revealed” — the same description given earlier in this chapter: the Quran is a blessed Book, carrying blessing in its recitation, its study, and its following. The blessing of the Book is its barakah: every engagement with it brings good.\n\nThen comes the command: “So follow it and be mindful ˹of Allah˺.” The Book is revealed to be followed — not only recited, not only admired, but followed: its commands put into practice, its prohibitions avoided, its guidance taken. And with the following comes mindfulness — the awareness of Allah that makes the following sincere.\n\nThe purpose is stated: “so you may be shown mercy.” The reward of following the blessed Book and being mindful of Allah is mercy — the mercy of Allah in this world and the Hereafter. The believer who follows the Book lives under the mercy: the mercy that guides, the mercy that protects, the mercy that forgives, the mercy that admits to Paradise.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the relationship with the Quran: it is blessed, so it deserves love; it is revealed, so it deserves following; it is followed with mindfulness, so it brings mercy. The believer does not treat the Quran as a decoration — he treats it as the path: follow it, be mindful, and the mercy will come. The same call is repeated throughout the chapter: the Book is revealed to be followed, and the follower is the one who receives the mercy.",
  "156": "“You ˹pagans˺ can no longer say, “Scriptures were only revealed to two groups before us and we were unaware of their teachings.”” The verse removes the excuse of the pagans. Before the coming of the Quran, the Arabs could say: the scriptures were revealed to two groups before us — the Jews and the Christians — and we were unaware of their teachings. The excuse was: the revelation never reached us; we did not know what the scriptures said; we are not responsible.\n\nThe verse says: “You ˹pagans˺ can no longer say” — the excuse is finished. With the revelation of the Quran to the Prophet — an Arab, speaking Arabic, living among them — the excuse of unawareness is gone. The Arabs could no longer claim that the scriptures passed them by: the final scripture came to them, in their own language, through a messenger from among them.\n\nThe next verse will record the second excuse that is also removed. Together, the two verses close every door of escape: no one can say the scriptures did not come, and no one can say that if the scriptures had come, they would have been better guided.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the mercy of the final revelation: the Quran closed the gap of unawareness. Before it, many peoples could claim ignorance; after it, the message reached the whole world — and the claim of ignorance is finished for those who have received it. And the verse teaches the believer the responsibility that comes with the message: the more the message reaches, the less the excuse remains. The believer receives the Quran not as a luxury but as the closing of every excuse and the opening of every responsibility.",
  "157": "“Nor can you say, “If only the Scriptures had been revealed to us, we would have been better guided than they.”” The verse records the second excuse of the pagans: they used to say that if the scriptures had been revealed to them, they would have been better guided than the Jews and the Christians. The claim was empty — they had no proof that they would have been better; it was a wish dressed as a boast.\n\nThe verse answers: “Now there has come to you from your Lord a clear proof—a guide and mercy.” The scripture they wished for has come: the Quran is “a clear proof” — an evident truth; “a guide” — pointing to the path; and “a mercy” — carrying compassion. The wish is granted, and the test is real: now that the clear proof has come, their claim will be tested by their actual response.\n\nThen the verdict: “Who then does more wrong than those who deny Allah's revelations and turn away from them?” The worst wrongdoers are those who deny the revelations and turn away — who reject the clear proof after it came and who walk away from it. The same verdict was given earlier in this chapter about those who fabricate lies against Allah.\n\nAnd the verse ends with the promise of punishment: “We will reward those who turn away from Our revelations with a dreadful punishment for turning away.” The turning away itself is punished: the one who received the clear proof and turned away faces a dreadful punishment — the recompense of his turning.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the seriousness of receiving the message: the arrival of the clear proof ends the era of wishes and begins the era of responsibility. The one who wished for the scripture is now tested by it; the one who denied it after it came is the worst wrongdoer; and the turning away is punished with a dreadful punishment.",
  "158": "“Are they awaiting the coming of the angels, or your Lord ˹Himself˺, or some of your Lord’s ˹major˺ signs?” The verse asks the deniers what they are waiting for. They rejected the clear proof and the warnings — so what are they waiting for? Three things are named: the coming of the angels — the angels of punishment; or the coming of your Lord Himself — the final judgment; or some of the major signs — the great signs of the Hour, like the rising of the sun from the west. The verse exposes their state: they are waiting for the Day of Judgment itself — and the waiting is their destruction.\n\nThen comes the terrible truth: “On the Day your Lord's signs arrive, belief will not benefit those who did not believe earlier or those who did no good through their faith.” When the major signs come, belief becomes useless: the person who did not believe earlier will not benefit from believing at that moment, and the person who believed but did no good will not benefit from his faith. The door of faith closes with the arrival of the decisive signs — because belief then is not choice; it is compulsion. The same teaching appears in another chapter: the faith of the past nations did not benefit them when they saw the punishment.\n\nAnd the verse ends with the command of waiting: “Say, “Keep waiting! We too are waiting.”” The Prophet is told to say to the deniers: keep waiting for the angels, the Lord, the signs — and we too are waiting: waiting for the promise of Allah, the help of Allah, and the judgment of Allah. The mockers wait for their demand; the believers wait for their promise.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the value of the present: belief and good deeds are accepted now, while belief is a choice — and the believer does not delay his faith or his deeds, because the day may come when belief will not benefit. And it teaches the believer to wait for the promise of Allah with certainty — the waiting of the believers ends in victory, while the waiting of the deniers ends in the signs they refused to heed.",
  "159": "“Indeed, you ˹O Prophet˺ are not responsible whatsoever for those who have divided their faith and split into sects.” The verse absolves the Prophet from the sects. “Those who have divided their faith and split into sects” — the people who broke the religion into pieces: some following one part and leaving another, some taking one sect and fighting another. The commentators mention that this verse refers to those who divided the religion after their prophets — the People of the Book who split into sects, and all who repeat their pattern. The Prophet is told: “you are not responsible whatsoever” for them — their division is not his doing and not his burden.\n\nThen comes the judgment: “Their judgment rests only with Allah. And He will inform them of what they used to do.” The sects and their divisions will be judged by Allah alone: He knows who divided, who followed, who fought, who was sincere — and He will inform them of what they used to do on the Day of Judgment.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer two lessons. First, the danger of dividing the religion: the religion of Allah is one, and those who split it into sects — taking some parts and rejecting others — carry a heavy responsibility. The believer holds the whole religion: he does not divide the faith by partial obedience or by sectarian hatred. Second, the limit of the caller's responsibility: the Prophet himself was not responsible for the sects — the caller of truth conveys the message, and the division of those who divide is their own account. The believer follows the one religion of Allah and leaves the judgment of the dividers to Allah.",
  "160": "“Whoever comes with a good deed will be rewarded tenfold.” The verse states the generosity of Allah's accounting. The good deed is rewarded tenfold — one good deed becomes ten — and the Quran elsewhere mentions even more: the good deed is multiplied up to seven hundred times and beyond. The generosity is from Allah: the believer does one good deed, and Allah writes it as ten — the multiplication is a gift.\n\nThen the justice of the bad deed: “But whoever comes with a bad deed will be punished for only one.” The bad deed is punished as one — no multiplication, no inflation: one bad deed, one punishment. The contrast is the heart of the verse: the good is multiplied, and the bad is not. The same contrast appears in another chapter, where the Quran says the good deed is rewarded tenfold and the bad deed with the like of it.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “None will be wronged.” The accounting of Allah wrongs no one: the good is not decreased, the bad is not increased, and the final recompense is exactly just. No soul will be wronged — not by a missing reward, not by an added punishment.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the hope and the fear of the accounting: hope, because the good deed is multiplied by the generosity of Allah; fear, because the bad deed is recorded and punished. The believer races to the good deeds — the deeds that multiply — and flees the bad deeds — the deeds that are weighed. And the believer trusts the justice: none will be wronged, and the accounting of Allah is the most just of all accounts.",
  "161": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Surely my Lord has guided me to the Straight Path, a perfect way, the faith of Abraham, the upright, who was not one of the polytheists.”” The verse is the Prophet's declaration of his guidance. “Surely my Lord has guided me” — the guidance is from the Lord: the Prophet did not find the path by his own effort alone; his Lord guided him to it. And the path is described: “the Straight Path, a perfect way” — the path that is straight and the way that is perfect, complete in its truth and its justice.\n\nThen the path is identified: “the faith of Abraham, the upright, who was not one of the polytheists.” The Straight Path is the faith of Abraham — the religion of pure submission to Allah alone. Abraham is described as “the upright” — the one who turned to Allah with complete devotion, inclining to the truth and away from every falsehood — and “who was not one of the polytheists”: his faith was pure, untainted by any partner. The same description appears in other chapters: Abraham was not a polytheist, and his faith was the faith of pure submission — Islam.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the identity of his religion: Islam is the faith of Abraham — not a new religion invented by Muhammad, but the continuation of the pure faith of the father of the prophets. The believer follows the Straight Path, the perfect way, the faith of the upright Abraham. And the verse teaches the believer to declare his guidance with gratitude: “my Lord has guided me” — the guidance is a gift from the Lord, and the guided one acknowledges the Giver with every mention of the path.",
  "162": "“Surely my prayer, my worship, my life, and my death are all for Allah—Lord of all worlds.” — these words are the believer's declaration of total devotion, commanded by the Prophet's Lord. The Prophet is commanded to say: my prayer — the worship of the body; my worship — the devotion of the heart, the sacrifice, the service; my life — all the days and deeds of living; and my death — the end of the journey and everything that comes with it. All of it is “for Allah—Lord of all worlds.” The declaration covers everything: the believer's prayer belongs to Allah, his worship belongs to Allah, his life is lived for Allah, and his death is met for Allah.\n\nThe same declaration is the meaning of the verse that every Muslim recites in prayer: the believer's prayer, his bowing, and his prostration are all for Allah. The declaration of devotion is not a slogan; it is the intention that shapes every action: the believer prays for Allah, lives for Allah, and is ready to die for Allah — because his Lord is the Lord of all worlds, the One who created everything and owns everything.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the completeness of sincerity: the religion is not a part of life — it is the whole of life. The believer does not divide his life into a religious part and a worldly part; his life is one life, and all of it is for Allah. The one who says this declaration with truth lives differently: his prayer is for Allah, so it is not for show; his life is for Allah, so it is not wasted; his death is for Allah, so it is not feared. The declaration of total devotion is the heart of the religion — and the believer repeats it every day to renew his direction.",
  "163": "“He has no partner. So I am commanded, and so I am the first to submit.”” The verse completes the declaration of devotion. “He has no partner” — the Lord of all worlds is alone: no partner in His lordship, no partner in His worship, no partner in His names. The declaration of oneness is the foundation of everything that follows: because He has no partner, the worship belongs to Him alone.\n\nThen comes the command: “So I am commanded” — the declaration of oneness is not only a statement; it is a command: because He has no partner, the servant is commanded to worship Him alone, to live for Him alone, to die for Him alone. The command follows the creed: the oneness of Allah demands the oneness of devotion.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “and so I am the first to submit.” The Prophet declares that he is the first to submit — the first of his people to surrender to Allah in Islam. The same statement appeared earlier in this chapter: “I have been commanded to be the first to submit and not be one of the polytheists.” The first to submit is the one who leads by example: he does not ask others to do what he does not do himself. His submission comes first, and his call follows his example.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the order of the faith: first the oneness of Allah — He has no partner; then the command — the servant is commanded to worship Him alone; then the submission — the servant surrenders and leads the way. The believer who says “He has no partner” must live by the command and lead with his own submission — because the declaration of oneness is the beginning, and the submission is the proof.",
  "164": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Should I seek a lord other than Allah while He is the Lord of everything?”” The verse returns to the question that closes the chapter's argument. The Prophet is told to ask: should I seek a lord other than Allah — another authority, another judge, another object of devotion — while He is the Lord of everything? The question answers itself: no. The Lord of everything is the only Lord; seeking another lord beside the Lord of everything is absurd. The same question was asked earlier in this chapter about seeking a judge other than Allah.\n\nThen comes the principle of personal responsibility: “No one will reap except what they sow.” The harvest of every person is his own planting: the one who sows good reaps good, and the one who sows evil reaps evil. The same principle appears in another chapter: “and that each person will only have what they endeavoured towards.”\n\nThen the second principle: “No soul burdened with sin will bear the burden of another.” The sins of one person are not carried by another — no one bears another's burden: the father does not carry the son's sins, and the son does not carry the father's. The same statement appears in another chapter: “No soul burdened with sin will bear the burden of another.” The justice of Allah is individual: each soul answers for itself.\n\nAnd the verse ends: “Then to your Lord is your return, and He will inform you of your differences.” The return is to the Lord, and there He will inform every person of the truth about his differences — the disputes of this world, the claims of the sects, the arguments about religion — all settled by the One who knows.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the three foundations of the end: the Lord of everything is the only Lord; every person reaps what he sowed; and no soul carries another's burden. The believer plants his own seeds, carries his own account, and returns to the Lord who will settle everything with knowledge.",
  "165": "“He is the One Who has placed you as successors on earth and elevated some of you in rank over others, so He may test you with what He has given you.” The verse closes the chapter with the purpose of human life on earth. “He is the One Who has placed you as successors on earth” — the human being is the successor of previous generations, inheriting the earth, building on it, and passing it on. The succession is from Allah: He places peoples in the earth, one after another, as His deputies in tending it.\n\nThen the variety: “and elevated some of you in rank over others” — the people of the earth are not equal in rank: some are given wealth, some poverty; some strength, some weakness; some knowledge, some ignorance. The differences in rank are not random — they are the test: “so He may test you with what He has given you.” The one given wealth is tested by his wealth, the one given poverty by his poverty, the one given power by his power. The same teaching appears in another chapter: “He has ˹also˺ subjected for you...” — and in the chapter of the Cattle's opening: the succession and the testing are the purpose of creation.\n\nThen the verse ends with the double description of the Lord: “Surely your Lord is swift in punishment, but He is certainly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” The chapter closes with the two ends of the test: swift in punishment for those who fail the test with evil, and All-Forgiving, Most Merciful for those who turn back. The verse is the summary of the whole chapter: the Lord of everything, the test of this world, and the two doors of the end — punishment and mercy.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the meaning of his life: he is a successor on earth, placed in a rank, tested by what he was given. The believer does not envy the ranks of others — every rank is a test; and the believer does not waste his own rank — it is his test. And the believer lives between the swift punishment and the vast mercy — fearing the first, hoping for the second, and working for the mercy while the test continues."
};
